window.B59_RECORDS = [{"id":"B59-000","title":"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2008-10-31","type":"writing","description":"The Bitcoin whitepaper: nine pages, posted to the Cryptography Mailing List on 31 October 2008. Proposes a purely peer-to-peer electronic cash system that solves double-spending without a trusted third party, by timestamping transactions into an ongoing chain of hash-based proof-of-work such that the longest chain is both the majority decision and the record hardest to rewrite. The anchor record of the vault — everything else is a precursor, a parallel lineage, or a consequence.","citation":"Satoshi Nakamoto, \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System,\" 31 October 2008. https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf","provenance":"Canonical PDF fetched with curl directly from https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf on 2026-08-31; bitcoin.org distributes the whitepaper under the MIT licence. The bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper landing page is archived alongside it as a second snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the canonical bitcoin.org PDF bytes and matches the value widely cited for that file (b1674191…f553). Public domain / MIT-licensed; hosted in full.","hash":"b1674191a88ec5cdd733e4240a81803105dc412d6c6708d53ab94fc248f4f553","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","whitepaper","primary-source","proof-of-work","peer-to-peer","electronic-cash","2008","the-anchor"],"excerpt":"What is needed is an electronic payment system based on cryptographic proof instead of trust, allowing any two willing parties to transact directly with each other without the need for a trusted third party.","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-whitepaper.html","size":5973,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"bitcoin.pdf","size":184292,"label":"Canonical PDF (Primary Source)"},{"path":"bitcoin-org-bitcoin-paper.html","size":44883,"label":"bitcoin.org paper page (snapshot)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-000","local":["content/B59-000/bitcoin-whitepaper.html","content/B59-000/bitcoin.pdf"],"see_also":["B59-400.005","B59-400.002","B59-400.001"]},{"id":"B59-001.001","title":"1995 Extropian Blueprint","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"writing","description":"Research report focused on the Extropian movement circa 1995 and its intersections with cryptography, digital markets, and the emerging internet economy. Examines the intellectual environment that connected transhumanist philosophy with practical work on electronic cash, agoric computing, and digital liberty.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"1995 Extropian Blueprint.\" Research report generated via Google NotebookLM from collected primary and secondary sources.","provenance":"AI-generated research report produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not a primary source document.","hash":"16e35560bd08da677e5312d452b227e551ee3b5374c3b64986bcd67ecdd53dd2","tags":["extropians","1995","digital-markets","transhumanism","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"1995-extropian-blueprint.pdf","size":14977629}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.001","local":["content/B59-001.001/1995-extropian-blueprint.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-001.002","title":"Computation and Liberty","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"writing","description":"Research document exploring the relationship between computational systems and individual liberty. Examines how cryptographic tools, agoric open systems, and decentralized architectures serve as mechanisms for preserving freedom in digital environments, drawing on the work of the Cypherpunks, Extropians, and early digital cash pioneers.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"Computation and Liberty.\" Research document generated via Google NotebookLM from collected sources.","provenance":"AI-generated research document produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not a primary source document.","hash":"8aacec58914e713abdd93d4999b1f06d922815fbaa9c0ab1ab0281642fba1ced","tags":["computation","liberty","cryptography","agoric","privacy","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"computation-and-liberty.pdf","size":1078907}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.002","local":["content/B59-001.002/computation-and-liberty.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-001.003","title":"Key Historical Milestones in Cryptography and Digital Currency","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"writing","description":"Comprehensive research timeline documenting 49 key events in the history of cryptography and digital currency from 1964 to 2008. Covers Paul Baran's distributed networks, public-key cryptography, David Chaum's blind signatures and DigiCash, the Cypherpunks, PGP, Crypto Wars, e-gold, Hashcash, b-money, bit gold, RPOW, and the Bitcoin whitepaper. Each entry includes date, event, project, individuals, location, description, and source references. 49 cited sources.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"Key Historical Milestones in Cryptography and Digital Currency.\" Research compilation generated via Google NotebookLM from 49 primary and secondary sources.","provenance":"Generated by Google NotebookLM as a structured data table from sources uploaded to the Project B59 research notebook. Sources include academic papers, Wikipedia articles, archived websites, and original research documents. This is a research compilation, not a primary source.","hash":"37c1ef5918b3cd5122e94e7280260d62205b2c90d7398a3d452a5c68b5ad2dcb","tags":["timeline","research-compilation","cryptography","digital-cash","cypherpunks","milestones"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"key-historical-milestones.ods","size":30957}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.003"},{"id":"B59-001.004","title":"Phil Salin: Architect of Agoric Computing and Digital Liberty","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"writing","description":"Research document on Phil Salin's contributions to agoric computing, digital markets, and the philosophical case for software as protected speech. Covers Salin's work at the American Information Exchange (AMIX), his collaboration with Mark Miller and Eric Drexler, and his 1991 position paper to the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office arguing that computer programs are a form of writing protected by the First Amendment.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"Phil Salin: Architect of Agoric Computing and Digital Liberty.\" Research document generated via Google NotebookLM from collected sources.","provenance":"AI-generated research document produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not a primary source document.","hash":"8b0ca937fc2d8d6de37760eb7c4f46fbf2dc4ab882d525d3c2efb89385c05423","tags":["phil-salin","agoric","digital-liberty","free-speech","software-rights","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"phil-salin-agoric-computing.pdf","size":504544}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.004","local":["content/B59-001.004/phil-salin-agoric-computing.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-001.005","title":"Rise and Fall of DigiCash","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"video","description":"Video overview examining the rise and fall of DigiCash, David Chaum's pioneering electronic cash company founded in 1989 in Amsterdam. Covers the invention of blind signatures, the eCash protocol, CyberBucks trial, partnerships with Mark Twain Bank and Deutsche Bank, and the company's eventual bankruptcy in 1998 despite having solved the technical problems of digital cash years before the market was ready.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"Rise & Fall of DigiCash.\" Video overview generated via Google NotebookLM from collected research sources.","provenance":"AI-generated video overview produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. Content synthesizes multiple primary and secondary sources on DigiCash history. This is a research compilation, not primary source footage.","hash":"100609291a0b1cb9f452a53bc6d7485e1e3748effcbaa31104842982e358bd9f","tags":["digicash","david-chaum","ecash","digital-cash","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"rise-fall-of-digicash.mp4","size":35788364}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.005","local":["content/B59-001.005/rise-fall-of-digicash.mp4"]},{"id":"B59-001.006","title":"The Cyber Blueprint","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"writing","description":"Research report examining the technological and ideological blueprint of the cypherpunk movement and its direct influence on the development of digital cash, privacy tools, and decentralized systems. Synthesizes material from the Cypherpunks Mailing List, academic papers, and historical accounts into a comprehensive overview of the technical lineage from cryptographic theory to practical implementation.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"The Cyber Blueprint.\" Research report generated via Google NotebookLM from collected primary and secondary sources.","provenance":"AI-generated research report produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not a primary source document.","hash":"2e54b34c2724bb770dea2d8b72bbcd009b0b379e6d956df399c92ad5fd6bdabe","tags":["cypherpunks","cryptography","digital-cash","research-compilation","technical-history"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-cyber-blueprint.pdf","size":13367781}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.006","local":["content/B59-001.006/the-cyber-blueprint.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-001.007","title":"The Extropian Blueprint","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"video","description":"Video overview exploring the Extropian movement and its connections to cryptography, digital cash, and the cypherpunk ethos. Covers the founding of Extropy journal by Max More in 1988, the intellectual crossover between transhumanism and crypto-anarchy, and how Extropian ideas about technology, liberty, and self-sovereignty influenced the development of digital currency systems.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"The Extropian Blueprint.\" Video overview generated via Google NotebookLM from collected research sources.","provenance":"AI-generated video overview produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not primary source footage.","hash":"d2945b6797cc50b69f227659e6f34e81556118904ba36d0e6030ee8c13c1d788","tags":["extropians","transhumanism","crypto-anarchy","max-more","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"extropian-blueprint.mp4","size":53021555}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.007","local":["content/B59-001.007/extropian-blueprint.mp4"]},{"id":"B59-001.008","title":"The Phil Salin Story","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"video","description":"Video overview documenting the life and work of Phil Salin, a largely overlooked figure in the pre-history of digital markets and smart contracts. Covers Salin's work on the American Information Exchange (AMIX), his 1991 position paper arguing that software is protected speech under the First Amendment, his connections to the Agorics project applying Hayekian economics to computation, and his influence on the intellectual foundations that led to cryptocurrency.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"The Phil Salin Story.\" Video overview generated via Google NotebookLM from collected research sources.","provenance":"AI-generated video overview produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook including Phil Salin's original writings and the Agorics project documentation. This is a research compilation, not primary source footage.","hash":"252f794f56423fb182c6578935ec442d725bc647371b3873aac9dbc91ecad7b1","tags":["phil-salin","amix","agoric","digital-markets","free-speech","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-phil-salin-story.mp4","size":41882063}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.008","local":["content/B59-001.008/the-phil-salin-story.mp4"]},{"id":"B59-001.009","title":"The Prophet Too Early","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-24","type":"video","description":"Video overview exploring the story of visionaries in cryptography and digital cash who were ahead of their time. Examines figures whose ideas and implementations preceded mainstream adoption by decades, and the pattern of technical solutions arriving before the cultural and economic conditions necessary for their success.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 24). \"The Prophet Too Early.\" Video overview generated via Google NotebookLM from collected research sources.","provenance":"AI-generated video overview produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook. This is a research compilation, not primary source footage.","hash":"c360170b3da2bf939621ef55a7d47f12b94ab7388dfba23176f41516d5585efc","tags":["digital-cash","early-history","visionaries","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-prophet-too-early.mp4","size":46544939}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.009","local":["content/B59-001.009/the-prophet-too-early.mp4"]},{"id":"B59-001.010","title":"Hal Finney: Digital Architect","author":"Project B59 Research / NotebookLM","date":"2026-05-25","type":"video","description":"Long-form video overview (9 minutes) profiling Hal Finney as a foundational architect of digital privacy and electronic cash systems. Covers Finney's role as the original PGP 2.0 programmer working directly with Phil Zimmermann, his work on the RPOW (Reusable Proofs of Work) system that prefigured Bitcoin's mining mechanism, his receipt of the first Bitcoin transaction from Satoshi Nakamoto, and his philosophical writings on remailers, privacy, and the technical underpinnings of crypto-anarchy.","citation":"Project B59. (2026, May 25). \"Hal Finney: Digital Architect.\" Long-form video overview generated via Google NotebookLM from collected primary sources including Finney's essays preserved in The Vault.","provenance":"AI-generated video overview produced by Google NotebookLM from sources in the Project B59 research notebook, including the Hal Finney essay collection (B59-304.005, B59-303.004 through B59-703.001, B59-201.002 through B59-106.002) preserved in The Vault. This is a research compilation, not primary source footage.","hash":"9a2673dba41edac16d5beb744dbb49c996a1c47f75af5975b9a105d862ecc71e","tags":["hal-finney","pgp","rpow","bitcoin","satoshi","cypherpunks","video-overview","research-compilation"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"hal-finney-digital-architect.mp4","size":48659967,"label":"Watch Overview (9 min)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-001.010","local":["content/B59-001.010/hal-finney-digital-architect.mp4"]},{"id":"B59-002.001","title":"Cypherpunks Mailing List Archive -- Searchable by Contributor","author":"Maaria Bajwa (archivist)","date":"1992-01-01","type":"website","description":"Searchable archive of the Cypherpunks Mailing List organized by contributor, containing thousands of messages from key figures in cryptography and digital privacy. The archive indexes 28+ contributors including Timothy C. May (4,104 messages), Perry E. Metzger (2,042), Jim Bell (1,180), Eric Hughes (1,073), Duncan Frissell (991), and others down to single-digit contributions from figures like Bram Cohen and John Perry Barlow. Messages are filterable by label, subject, sender, and date. The Cypherpunks Mailing List was the primary forum where the technical and philosophical foundations of digital cash, anonymous communication, and cryptographic freedom were debated from 1992 onward.","citation":"Bajwa, M. (n.d.). Cypherpunks Mailing List Archive. Published at cypherpunk.maaria.com. Contact: maariabajwa@gmail.com.","provenance":"Live web archive maintained by Maaria Bajwa. Original messages sourced from the Cypherpunks Mailing List (cypherpunks@toad.com and successor lists). This record references the live site; a full local mirror should be created for permanent preservation.","hash":"","tags":["cypherpunks","mailing-list","tim-may","eric-hughes","perry-metzger","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://cypherpunk.maaria.com/"},{"id":"B59-002.002","title":"Cypherpunks Mailing List Archive -- Cryptoanarchy Wiki","author":"Cryptoanarchy Wiki (maintainers), Ryan Lackey (original archive source)","date":"1992-09-01","type":"website","description":"Complete archive of the Cypherpunks Mailing List from 1992 through early 1999, sourced from Ryan Lackey's cypherpunks.venona.com archive. Each message is indexed with a SHA-256 hash, original headers, and full text. Browsable by date and by author (ranked by frequency or alphabetical). Covers the mailing list's formative years when it was hosted on toad.com before transitioning to distributed architecture. The archive documents discussions on encryption, digital money, anonymous networks, digital pseudonyms, zero knowledge proofs, reputations, and information markets. Archives from 2000-2013 are noted as missing.","citation":"Cryptoanarchy Wiki. (n.d.). Cypherpunks Mailing List Archive. Published at mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki. Source: Ryan Lackey's cypherpunks.venona.com archive. GitHub: cryptoanarchywiki.","provenance":"Community-maintained archive sourced from Ryan Lackey's original mailing list dumps at cypherpunks.venona.com. Each message is individually hashed (SHA-256). Known issues include timezone parsing problems in 1992 data and some orphaned thread replies. Coverage: 1992-1999. Gap: 2000-2013 archives remain unavailable.","hash":"","tags":["cypherpunks","mailing-list","primary-source","ryan-lackey","external-reference","1992-1999"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki/"},{"id":"B59-002.003","title":"Cypherpunk Research Repository (Tom Busby, 2018)","author":"Tom Busby","date":"2018-05-26","type":"code","description":"Curated GitHub repository compiled by Tom Busby (Twitter: @_tombusby) cataloging the cypherpunk movement: 40+ individual person profiles (Adam Back, Wei Dai, Julian Assange, David Chaum, Hal Finney, Satoshi Nakamoto, Cody Wilson, Ross Ulbricht, Phil Zimmermann, and many more), notable events (Silk Road, Mt.Gox, DAO Hack, Snowden leaks, 90s Crypto Wars), organizations (EFF, Paralelni Polis), and project documentation. Each entry is a structured Markdown file with biographical info, key contributions, and links to primary sources. The repository includes references to the Cypherpunks Mailing List Archives (1992-1998) and the Cyphernomicon FAQ. Designed as a research backbone for articles and book-length work on cypherpunk history.","citation":"Busby, T. (2018, May 26). \"cypherpunk-research.\" GitHub repository. Commit hash: be387755363b0d8c5bd38aabec8385f449a262ef. Compiled to support future articles and a planned book on cypherpunk history.","provenance":"Cloned from GitHub at commit be387755363b0d8c5bd38aabec8385f449a262ef, dated 26 May 2018. Repository structure intact: README.md, /people/ (40+ profiles), /events/, /organisations/, /images/, /unsorted.md, and contribution guidelines. Preserved as a downloadable ZIP archive plus an inline HTML rendering of the README for browsing.","hash":"ead23ca14a82a12007bfe36d4aaf2663a6650ec0fd2b494efd51e221b49c0938","tags":["cypherpunks","research-compilation","github","tom-busby","reference","external-research","mailing-list"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"cypherpunk-research-readme.html","size":53953,"label":"Browse Repository README","entry":true},{"path":"cypherpunk-research-2018.zip","size":132537,"label":"Download Full Repository (ZIP)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-002.003","local":["content/B59-002.003/cypherpunk-research-readme.html"]},{"id":"B59-003.001","title":"mIRC 7.83 -- IRC Client","author":"Khaled Mardam-Bey","date":"2023-01-01","type":"code","description":"Installer for mIRC version 7.83, the Windows IRC (Internet Relay Chat) client that was ubiquitous in early internet culture. IRC was the primary real-time communication platform for the cypherpunks, open-source developers, hackers, and early cryptocurrency communities throughout the 1990s and 2000s. Preserved as a representative artifact of the communication infrastructure that enabled the cypherpunk movement and early digital culture.","citation":"Mardam-Bey, K. (2023). \"mIRC 7.83.\" Windows IRC client. Published at mirc.com.","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. mIRC is proprietary software (mIRC Co. Ltd.); redistribution of the installer binary is not permitted under its licence. This record links to the official download page at mirc.com rather than hosting the installer. Preserved as a reference to the IRC client that was ubiquitous in 1990s and 2000s cypherpunk, open-source, and early-cryptocurrency culture.","hash":"","tags":["irc","mirc","communication","software","early-internet","tools","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://www.mirc.com/get.html","retrieved":"2026-09-01"},{"id":"B59-101.001","title":"New Directions in Cryptography","author":"Whitfield Diffie & Martin E. Hellman","date":"1976-11-01","type":"writing","description":"The paper that opened public-key cryptography to the world. Diffie and Hellman show that two parties can agree on a shared secret over a channel an eavesdropper fully controls, and that a \"one-way authentication\" scheme can give the digital equivalent of a written signature. It introduces public key distribution, the Diffie-Hellman key exchange based on discrete exponentiation, trap-door one-way functions, and the idea of a public file of enciphering keys. RSA (1978) is the answer to the open problem this paper poses.","excerpt":"We stand today on the brink of a revolution in cryptography.","citation":"Whitfield Diffie and Martin E. Hellman, \"New Directions in Cryptography,\" IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, vol. IT-22, no. 6, November 1976, pp. 644-654.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Martin Hellman’s official Stanford faculty page (ee.stanford.edu/~hellman/publications/24.pdf) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified as the source snapshot alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. The Internet Archive was offline at archiving time, so an independent Wayback cross-check could not be completed; the file is Hellman’s own posted copy and the text matches the widely reproduced IEEE original.","hash":"03fa2f493a1448a309586b9cf9e5c3a4dfd14735e0786cc94674519570b890ff","tags":["public-key-cryptography","diffie-hellman","key-exchange","digital-signatures","cryptography","primary-source","whitfield-diffie","martin-hellman","1976"],"files":[{"path":"new-directions-in-cryptography.html","size":4849,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"new-directions-in-cryptography-source.pdf","size":2216632,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.001","local":["content/B59-101.001/new-directions-in-cryptography.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.002","title":"A Method for Obtaining Digital Signatures and Public-Key Cryptosystems","author":"Ronald L. Rivest, Adi Shamir & Leonard Adleman","date":"1978-02-01","type":"writing","description":"The RSA paper. It answers the open problem Diffie and Hellman posed in 1976 with a concrete trap-door function: exponentiation modulo the product of two large secret primes. One method gives both public-key encryption and unforgeable digital signatures, with security resting on the difficulty of factoring. RSA is still, decades later, one of the most widely deployed public-key systems in existence.","excerpt":"Publicly revealing an encryption key does not thereby reveal the corresponding decryption key.","citation":"R. L. Rivest, A. Shamir, and L. Adleman, \"A Method for Obtaining Digital Signatures and Public-Key Cryptosystems,\" Communications of the ACM, vol. 21, no. 2, February 1978, pp. 120-126.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ronald Rivest’s official MIT CSAIL page (people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/Rsapaper.pdf) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. This is a clean typeset copy hosted by the author, not the CACM scan. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed.","hash":"f7b1f78d9a7cbeb85e32b8c563a6db60771a5cc4bdc55580645f7cb778a4966b","tags":["rsa","public-key-cryptography","digital-signatures","rivest-shamir-adleman","factoring","cryptography","primary-source","1978"],"files":[{"path":"a-method-for-obtaining-digital-signatures.html","size":4702,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"a-method-for-obtaining-digital-signatures-source.pdf","size":182059,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.002","local":["content/B59-101.002/a-method-for-obtaining-digital-signatures.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.003","title":"Secure Communications Over Insecure Channels","author":"Ralph C. Merkle","date":"1978-04-01","type":"writing","description":"Ralph Merkle’s \"puzzles\" paper: the first published method for two parties to establish a shared secret over a channel an eavesdropper hears completely. Written as an undergraduate project at Berkeley in 1974, submitted to CACM in 1975, and published only in 1978 after nearly three years of rejection. The scheme forces an eavesdropper to do work that grows as the square of the honest parties’ work, the first concrete demonstration of a public key exchange.","excerpt":"This paper shows that it is possible to select a key over open communications channels in such a fashion that communications security can be maintained.","citation":"Ralph C. Merkle, \"Secure Communications Over Insecure Channels,\" Communications of the ACM, vol. 21, no. 4, April 1978, pp. 294-299.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ralph Merkle’s personal site (ralphmerkle.com/1974/PuzzlesAsPublished.pdf, the \"as published\" CACM version) on 2026-08-31. The same paths formerly lived at merkle.com, which now redirects to an unrelated marketing company; ralphmerkle.com is Merkle’s current canonical host. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed.","hash":"e89c2f2563f5c73472fe7983c05051e6f542a075a148a28fac76a6b6713e91cd","tags":["public-key-cryptography","ralph-merkle","key-exchange","merkle-puzzles","cryptography","primary-source","1978"],"files":[{"path":"secure-communications-over-insecure-channels.html","size":4784,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"secure-communications-over-insecure-channels-source.pdf","size":576003,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.003","local":["content/B59-101.003/secure-communications-over-insecure-channels.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.004","title":"An Overview of Public Key Cryptography","author":"Martin E. Hellman","date":"1978-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Hellman’s plain-language survey of public key cryptography two years after \"New Directions.\" Written for a general engineering audience, it lays out the commercial need for encryption, the mechanics of public key distribution and digital signatures, and the three then-live candidate systems: the Diffie-Hellman exponential key exchange, the Merkle-Hellman trapdoor knapsack, and RSA. A useful snapshot of how the field explained itself at the moment it went public.","excerpt":"With a public key cryptosystem, the key used to encipher a message can be made public without compromising the secrecy of a different key needed to decipher that message.","citation":"Martin E. Hellman, \"An Overview of Public Key Cryptography,\" IEEE Communications Society Magazine, vol. 16, no. 6, November 1978, pp. 24-32. (Reprinted in IEEE Communications Magazine, 50th Anniversary issue, May 2002.)","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Martin Hellman’s official Stanford faculty page (ee.stanford.edu/~hellman/publications/31.pdf) on 2026-08-31. The hosted file is the original November 1978 IEEE Communications Society Magazine printing (page furniture and 1978 references intact). SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed.","hash":"d32a2216fa3f9182daf6ecb4531ffffcf23496a1865c2e4d65d63e847baf99c7","tags":["public-key-cryptography","martin-hellman","cryptography","primary-source","trapdoor-knapsack","digital-signatures","1978"],"files":[{"path":"overview-of-public-key-cryptography.html","size":4563,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"overview-of-public-key-cryptography-source.pdf","size":846772,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.004","local":["content/B59-101.004/overview-of-public-key-cryptography.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.005","title":"Protocols for Public Key Cryptosystems","author":"Ralph C. Merkle","date":"1980-04-14","type":"writing","description":"The paper that introduces tree authentication, now universally known as the Merkle tree or hash tree. Merkle compares protocols for public key distribution and digital signatures, and shows how a single hash at the root of a binary tree can authenticate any one of a large number of leaf values with a short path of sibling hashes. Bitcoin’s block structure, Certificate Transparency, Git, IPFS and every modern append-only log use this construction.","excerpt":"New cryptographic protocols which take full advantage of the unique properties of public key cryptosystems are now evolving.","citation":"Ralph C. Merkle, \"Protocols for Public Key Cryptosystems,\" Proceedings of the 1980 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, Oakland, CA, April 1980, pp. 122-134.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ralph Merkle’s personal site (ralphmerkle.com/papers/Protocols.pdf) on 2026-08-31. Byte size (742408) matches the last Internet Archive capture of the identical file at merkle.com/papers/Protocols.pdf, which serves as the independent cross-check since the live Wayback service was offline at archiving time. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes.","hash":"d568120ba4745335ea98988cb9b43f7143d06507ee42b0fca17bc91ea9732167","tags":["public-key-cryptography","ralph-merkle","hash-trees","merkle-tree","digital-signatures","key-distribution","primary-source","1980"],"files":[{"path":"protocols-for-public-key-cryptosystems.html","size":4251,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"protocols-for-public-key-cryptosystems-source.pdf","size":742408,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.005","local":["content/B59-101.005/protocols-for-public-key-cryptosystems.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.006","title":"The First Ten Years of Public-Key Cryptography","author":"Whitfield Diffie","date":"1988-05-01","type":"writing","description":"A retrospective and technical survey by one of the two people who started the field. Diffie traces the origins of public key cryptography (including his account of Merkle’s independent contribution and the later GCHQ disclosure debate), surveys the systems that existed by 1988 (RSA, knapsacks and their breaks, McEliece, discrete-log methods), and reviews the state of key management, digital signatures and cryptographic protocols. Widely cited as the standard first-person history of the period.","excerpt":"Diffie’s own history of the idea he and Hellman set loose, written from inside it, a decade on.","citation":"Whitfield Diffie, \"The First Ten Years of Public-Key Cryptography,\" Proceedings of the IEEE, vol. 76, no. 5, May 1988, pp. 560-577.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Daniel J. Bernstein’s bibliography mirror (cr.yp.to/bib/1988/diffie.pdf) on 2026-08-31; this is linked as the reference copy from Ralph Merkle’s own site (ralphmerkle.com/1974/). The file is a SCANNED IMAGE PDF: no machine-readable text could be extracted in the archiving environment and no OCR was performed, so no verbatim excerpt is transcribed on the reader page below. The full 18-page scan is preserved unmodified as the source snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed. A verified transcription is a good follow-up task.","hash":"2b097bbff52613c6097ba2b47249e08aa1ed2dab7d94842bf060964967de7f87","tags":["public-key-cryptography","whitfield-diffie","history-of-cryptography","digital-signatures","primary-source","1988"],"files":[{"path":"first-ten-years-of-public-key-cryptography.html","size":4305,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"first-ten-years-of-public-key-cryptography-source.pdf","size":2016864,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.006","local":["content/B59-101.006/first-ten-years-of-public-key-cryptography.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.007","title":"Introducing Public Keys","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1992-10-04","type":"writing","description":"Hughes on the key-distribution problem, from the list's first month. Responding to someone trying to become a FidoNet key \"introducer,\" he argues that certification has to start from out-of-band exchange (diskettes, printouts, in-person handoff), that introducers are not a special class and most people should certify keys for redundancy, and that a non-hierarchical public key distribution system for the general public had never been built and was an open research problem.","citation":"Eric Hughes, \"introducing public keys,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 4 October 1992.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: hughes@soda.berkeley.edu). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim. Selected as the closest match to the \"key-server\" writeup named in the archiving brief; see the manifest note.","hash":"ee54cf14fc2539e1c537bfbbb1cacce9d23c40bebd7b597e7f5de68b59c18714","tags":["cypherpunks","eric-hughes","primary-source","cryptography","mailing-list","1992"],"excerpt":"Remember, no one has ever set up a non-hierarchical public key distribution system to the general public. This is research.","files":[{"path":"introducing-public-keys.html","size":4444,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"introducing-public-keys-source.html","size":8960,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.007","local":["content/B59-101.007/introducing-public-keys.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.008","title":"PGP Math Library Documentation","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-11-26","type":"writing","description":"Technical documentation by Finney explaining the inner workings of the mathematical functions in PGP's math library. As one of the original PGP 2.0 programmers working directly with Phil Zimmermann, Finney provides authoritative insight into the multi-precision arithmetic, modular exponentiation, and prime number operations that made PGP's RSA implementation work on the hardware of the early 1990s.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, November 26). \"PGP Math Library.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/pgp_math_lib.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"32260c1e38afba315bcb242b9b76b14039e0df70e0534223ec5d00cb1bad5718","tags":["hal-finney","pgp","cryptography","mathematics","rsa","documentation","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"pgp-math-library.html","size":13598,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.008","local":["content/B59-101.008/pgp-math-library.html"]},{"id":"B59-101.009","title":"Publishing a New Idea","author":"Ralph C. Merkle","date":"2005-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Merkle’s first-person account of inventing public key cryptography as a Berkeley undergraduate in 1974 and the three years of rejection that followed. He reproduces the CS244 project proposal his instructor turned down, the referee letters from Communications of the ACM (\"not in the main stream of present cryptography thinking\"), and his reasoning for holding out for the original submission date rather than publishing elsewhere. A primary document on how institutions receive ideas with no precedent.","excerpt":"The human mind treats a new idea the same way the body treats a strange protein; it rejects it.\" (P. B. Medawar, quoted by Merkle)","citation":"Ralph C. Merkle, \"Publishing a New Idea,\" essay, ralphmerkle.com/1974/ (undated; internal references date composition to 2005 or later).","provenance":"Primary-source HTML retrieved by curl from Ralph Merkle’s personal site (ralphmerkle.com/1974/) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. The page is an undated living essay; internal references to the 8 September 2005 rediscovery of his 1974 proposal and to the 1988 Diffie survey place composition in or after 2005, so the record date is set to 2005 for Kat to refine. SHA-256 is computed over the archived HTML bytes. Linked PDFs of the 1974 proposals are referenced by the essay but are separate files not included in this snapshot.","hash":"4b28414a06dabef4ad1fa61403ccf37fd0fe357eaa381f8dc4f1b34011b175aa","tags":["public-key-cryptography","ralph-merkle","history-of-cryptography","merkle-puzzles","essay","primary-source"],"files":[{"path":"publishing-a-new-idea.html","size":4822,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"publishing-a-new-idea-source.html","size":6686,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-101.009","local":["content/B59-101.009/publishing-a-new-idea.html"]},{"id":"B59-102.001","title":"The MD5 Message-Digest Algorithm (RFC 1321)","author":"Ronald L. Rivest","date":"1992-04-01","type":"writing","description":"The specification of MD5: input a message of any length, output a 128-bit digest, with the design goal that finding two messages with the same digest is infeasible. Published as an informational RFC with a reference C implementation. MD5 became one of the most widely used hash functions on the internet; it is now cryptographically broken for collision resistance, but the RFC remains a foundational primary document and MD5 is still met everywhere as a checksum.","excerpt":"The algorithm takes as input a message of arbitrary length and produces as output a 128-bit fingerprint or message digest of the input.","citation":"R. Rivest, \"The MD5 Message-Digest Algorithm,\" RFC 1321, Internet Engineering Task Force, April 1992.","provenance":"Primary-source plain-text retrieved by curl from the RFC Editor, the canonical IETF RFC repository (rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1321.txt), on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. RFCs are stable published documents; this text is byte-identical to the IETF copy. SHA-256 is computed over the archived text bytes.","hash":"284a79d148400d9cd2a423211d1103b5cef0fb9256a4cbe6d7ebe5197c3149dd","tags":["md5","hash-functions","message-digest","ronald-rivest","rfc","cryptography","primary-source","1992"],"files":[{"path":"rfc-1321-md5-message-digest-algorithm.html","size":4672,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"rfc-1321-md5-message-digest-algorithm-source.txt","size":35222,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-102.001","local":["content/B59-102.001/rfc-1321-md5-message-digest-algorithm.html"]},{"id":"B59-103.001","title":"A Certified Digital Signature","author":"Ralph C. Merkle","date":"1979-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Merkle’s full treatment of hash-based digital signatures: an infinite-tree signature scheme built from nothing but a one-way function, plus the tree-authentication construction that carries his name. Submitted to the CACM in 1979, stalled in review for a decade, and finally published at CRYPTO ’89. The hosted copy is the author’s own, with his 2012 note on the paper’s history and slides from the conference talk appended.","excerpt":"A digital signature based only on a conventional encryption function... as secure as the underlying encryption function.","citation":"Ralph C. Merkle, \"A Certified Digital Signature,\" in G. Brassard (ed.), Advances in Cryptology — CRYPTO ’89, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 435, Springer, 1990, pp. 218-238. Manuscript first submitted to Communications of the ACM in 1979.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ralph Merkle’s personal site (ralphmerkle.com/papers/Certified1979.pdf) on 2026-08-31. SUBSTITUTION NOTE: the task list named Merkle’s CRYPTO ’87 paper \"A Digital Signature Based on a Conventional Encryption Function.\" No freely hostable copy of the 1987 paper could be located (it is not on Merkle’s own site and the only copies found are behind the Springer paywall; Internet Archive was offline for a Wayback attempt). \"A Certified Digital Signature\" is the fuller, author-hosted development of the same hash-based signature and tree-authentication ideas and is used in its place. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes.","hash":"18725c8593a7b8786ab02a0621f2e76ed525aef05290d2aa30769241441b56c6","tags":["hash-trees","merkle-tree","ralph-merkle","digital-signatures","one-time-signatures","post-quantum","primary-source","1979"],"files":[{"path":"a-certified-digital-signature.html","size":4645,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"a-certified-digital-signature-source.pdf","size":913678,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-103.001","local":["content/B59-103.001/a-certified-digital-signature.html"]},{"id":"B59-103.002","title":"A Digital Signature Based on a Conventional Encryption Function","author":"Ralph Merkle","date":"1987","type":"writing","description":"Merkle's CRYPTO '87 paper introducing what is now called the Merkle signature scheme: a digital signature built entirely from a conventional (symmetric) encryption or hash function, with authentication of one-time public keys via a hash tree. A foundational reference for hash-based signatures and for the Merkle-tree constructions used throughout Bitcoin and later systems. Placeholder pending a freely hostable copy.","citation":"Merkle, R. (1988). CRYPTO '87, LNCS 293, Springer, pp. 369-378.","provenance":"Source pending: no freely hostable copy located as of 2026-08-31. To be supplied.","hash":"","tags":["ralph-merkle","digital-signatures","hash-trees","source-pending"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null},{"id":"B59-105.001","title":"Timed-Release Crypto","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1993-02-10","type":"writing","description":"An early proposal for \"sending a message into the future\": encrypting data so that it cannot be read until a chosen later date. May sketches approaches using chained remailers with held keys and cooperating escrow agents, and discusses uses such as sealed bids, wills, deferred disclosure, and protecting whistle-blowers. A precursor to the later academic literature on timed-release and time-lock cryptography.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"Timed-Release Crypto,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 10 February 1993.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki. The page carries the full raw message; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page. The reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"ea6ec6a231cf3853d9ebb9628f0ddc2ec4849972aa2ed08c0e24898a2b894720","tags":["cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","cryptography","mailing-list","1993"],"excerpt":"I've been thinking about ways to combine crypto protocols to allow the timed-release of encrypted messages, that is, to send a message into the future.","files":[{"path":"timed-release-crypto.html","size":15525,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"timed-release-crypto-source.html","size":20405,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-105.001","local":["content/B59-105.001/timed-release-crypto.html"]},{"id":"B59-105.002","title":"Truly Stealthy PGP","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-03-06","type":"writing","description":"Analysis of what would be required to make PGP-encrypted messages indistinguishable from random data. Finney examines the limitations of \"Stealth PGP\" variants and discusses how the structure of PGP packets makes encrypted messages identifiable as PGP traffic. Relevant to traffic analysis resistance and steganographic uses of encryption.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, March 6). \"Truly Stealthy PGP.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/stealth_pgp.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"d1db80c4103a12efad56fef305c4affaaabe5207bdebc341f83bce05a98fff85","tags":["hal-finney","pgp","steganography","stealth","traffic-analysis","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"truly-stealthy-pgp.html","size":3028,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-105.002","local":["content/B59-105.002/truly-stealthy-pgp.html"]},{"id":"B59-105.003","title":"PGP Web of Trust Misconceptions","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-03-30","type":"writing","description":"Essay addressing common misconceptions about PGP's web of trust model. Finney examines whether the decentralized trust architecture that worked for the early cypherpunk community could scale to global usage, and what would be required for the model to remain useful as the user base grew beyond people who could meet in person at key-signing parties.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, March 30). \"PGP Web of Trust Misconceptions.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/web_of_trust.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"d3dadbba97af4edeca1d92600278ebf1c9b8c4616de5c76289bbba28da2efc8a","tags":["hal-finney","pgp","web-of-trust","trust-models","cryptography","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"pgp-web-of-trust-misconceptions.html","size":5587,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-105.003","local":["content/B59-105.003/pgp-web-of-trust-misconceptions.html"]},{"id":"B59-105.004","title":"Crypto Kong — documentation","author":"James A. Donald","date":"2001-11-15","type":"writing","description":"Documentation for Crypto Kong, James A. Donald’s Windows tool for digitally signing and encrypting text and email, built on a deliberately non-hierarchical trust model: your key is your identity, and you decide which keys to trust by direct experience rather than through a certificate authority. Pages mirrored here: the index / Kong.htm overview, how.htm (how it works), identity.htm (the \"your signature is your name\" argument against X.509), and digicash.htm (Donald’s note on digital cash). A concrete late-1990s / early-2000s implementation of the web-of-trust idea by the man who would later interrogate Satoshi on the cryptography list.","citation":"James A. Donald, \"Crypto Kong\" documentation, echeque.com/Kong/ and jim.com/jamesd/Kong/, c. 1998–2002. Retrieved via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.","provenance":"Both of James A. Donald’s domains that hosted Crypto Kong are dead: echeque.com is parked and jim.com now hosts an unrelated commercial blog. Pages retrieved from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine as raw (id_) snapshots of http://www.echeque.com/Kong/ captured 2001-11 to 2002-01: index.html / Kong.htm (2001-11-15 / 2001-11-10), how.htm (2001-11-15), identity.htm (2001-11-15), digicash.htm (2002-01-26). Donald’s site carried no explicit licence. Stored unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over index.html (the primary file); per-file SHA-256 in the manifest. The Crypto Kong installer and VB source were also archived by IA but are not mirrored here.","hash":"47572afe7ab30790485a21bbe6b8bd12258ea2b0e727ff665041035651ce3d75","tags":["james-donald","web-of-trust","digital-signatures","crypto-kong","cypherpunks","wayback","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Your signature is your true name. It cannot be forged, and it cannot be taken from you.","files":[{"path":"crypto-kong.html","size":12643,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"crypto-kong-index.html","size":29674,"label":"Crypto Kong — index / Kong.htm (Primary Source)"},{"path":"crypto-kong-how.html","size":11290,"label":"Crypto Kong — how.htm"},{"path":"crypto-kong-identity.html","size":4440,"label":"Crypto Kong — identity.htm"},{"path":"crypto-kong-digicash.html","size":2302,"label":"Crypto Kong — digicash.htm"}],"content_path":"content/B59-105.004","local":["content/B59-105.004/crypto-kong.html"]},{"id":"B59-106.001","title":"Crypto++ Library — Readme & History","author":"Wei Dai","date":"1995-06-01","type":"writing","description":"The Readme and release history of Crypto++, the free C++ class library of cryptographic algorithms that Wei Dai first released in 1995 and maintained for two decades. Readme.txt states the library is in the public domain, lists the algorithm set (from the mid-1990s primitives through AES, SHA-2/3, elliptic-curve and authenticated encryption), and documents building and validation. History.txt is the version-by-version changelog from 1.0 (1995) onward. Widely used in research and production; the same author who wrote b-money maintained one of the most-used open crypto toolkits.","citation":"Wei Dai et al., \"Crypto++ Library,\" Readme.txt and History.txt, 1995–2024. Repository: https://github.com/weidai11/cryptopp","provenance":"Readme.txt (24,651 bytes) and History.txt (30,775 bytes) retrieved with curl from raw.githubusercontent.com/weidai11/cryptopp/master (HTTP 200). These are the current maintained files; the repository is the citation. Stored unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over Readme.txt (the primary file). Crypto++ 1.0 was released by Wei Dai in June 1995; project maintenance later passed to a team while Dai remains the originator.","hash":"dc90823f9d8c5c72af963e8ed733c38eb210db4a2bb16b2419ab230dfbbc3dd7","tags":["wei-dai","cryptography","open-source","cryptopp","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Crypto++ Library is a free C++ class library of cryptographic schemes.","files":[{"path":"cryptopp-library.html","size":15378,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"cryptopp-Readme.txt","size":24651,"label":"Readme.txt (Primary Source)"},{"path":"cryptopp-History.txt","size":30775,"label":"History.txt (release history)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.001","local":["content/B59-106.001/cryptopp-library.html"]},{"id":"B59-106.002","title":"The SSL Challenge","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1995-08-01","type":"writing","description":"In August 1995 Hal Finney issued a public challenge to the cryptographic community: break a sample web browsing session run in secure mode using Netscape's Secure Socket Layer (SSL) protocol with the export-grade 40-bit encryption then mandated by US regulations. The challenge was broken in short order, demonstrating that export-controlled encryption was inadequate for any serious commercial use and contributing to the eventual relaxation of US encryption export controls.","citation":"Finney, H. (1995, August). \"The SSL Challenge.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/sslchal.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"c77fcb51fa2d5c64022d18f7ec86ddeeaea32d6af453aad020a4390cb2260b3a","tags":["hal-finney","ssl","netscape","crypto-wars","export-controls","challenge","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-ssl-challenge.html","size":12668,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.002","local":["content/B59-106.002/the-ssl-challenge.html"]},{"id":"B59-106.003","title":"The SSL Challenge: Solution Announcement","author":"Damien Doligez","date":"1995-08-15","type":"writing","description":"PGP-signed announcement by Damien Doligez (INRIA) reporting the successful brute-force solution of the SSL encryption challenge that Hal Finney posted on 17 July 1995. Using roughly 120 workstations and several parallel computers across INRIA, Ecole Polytechnique, and ENS, Doligez recovered the 40-bit export-grade secret key (7e f0 96 1f a6) after scanning about half the key space in eight days, demonstrating that export-strength SSL was trivially breakable. The decrypted payload is a sample Netscape Commerce order famously addressed to 'Mr Cosmic Kumquat, SSL Trusters Inc., 1234 Squeamish Ossifrage Road.' A landmark Crypto Wars result and a direct answer to Finney's challenge.","citation":"Doligez, D. (1995, August). 'SSL challenge -- broken.' PGP-signed announcement. Archived at http://pauillac.inria.fr/~doligez/ssl/announce.txt","provenance":"Plain-text, PGP-signed message (PGP 2.6.2) announcing the solution to Hal Finney's SSL challenge of 17 July 1995, including an appended PGP timestamp. Preserved as a primary source from the Crypto Wars era. Companion to record B59-106.002 ('The SSL Challenge').","hash":"01861c7ea085905bfce331b6dfd7c5a9624e8fd438bf93b970325cdfdd768c3d","tags":["hal-finney","ssl","crypto-wars","cryptography","brute-force","netscape","cypherpunks","pgp"],"excerpt":"The 40-bit secret part of the key is 7e f0 96 1f a6. I found it by a brute force search on a network of about 120 workstations and a few parallel computers at INRIA, Ecole Polytechnique, and ENS.","files":[{"path":"ssl-challenge-solution-announcement.txt","size":5880,"label":"Read Announcement (PGP-Signed)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.003","local":["content/B59-106.003/ssl-challenge-solution-announcement.txt"]},{"id":"B59-106.004","title":"The Abuse of Statistics in Cryptography","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1999-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's technical critique of how statistical arguments are misused in cryptographic analysis and security claims. Examines common fallacies in probabilistic security arguments and the epistemological standards that should apply to cryptographic proof.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 1999). The Abuse of Statistics in Cryptography. Retrieved from https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/rob/Courses/InformationInSpeech/CDROM/Literature/LOTwinterschool2006/szabo.best.vwh.net/statistics.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net. Original filename: statistics.html (also referenced as abuse.html on the site index).","hash":"d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","cryptography","statistics","security","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"abuse-of-statistics-in-cryptography.html","entry":true,"size":8596}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.004","local":["content/B59-106.004/abuse-of-statistics-in-cryptography.html"]},{"id":"B59-106.005","title":"An Introduction to Algorithmic Information Theory","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2000-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's accessible introduction to algorithmic information theory - the mathematics of randomness, complexity, and compression. Important foundation for understanding the information-theoretic properties of cryptographic systems.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2000). \"An Introduction to Algorithmic Information Theory.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/introduction-to-algorithmic-information-theory/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/introduction-to-algorithmic-information-theory/","hash":"6f15f9f9469f809a589d41eadb2ebf9a5f90e4fbb76ed995f62f13f601ff2414","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"introduction-algorithmic-info-theory.html","size":75367,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.005","local":["content/B59-106.005/introduction-algorithmic-info-theory.html"]},{"id":"B59-106.006","title":"Intrapolynomial Cryptography","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's exploration of cryptographic constructions based on intrapolynomial functions. Technical paper on alternative mathematical foundations for cryptographic primitives.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Intrapolynomial Cryptography.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/intrapoly.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/intrapoly.html","hash":"a3df64381edda745d25a44f43ac13c33aeae037c6372f8d7c6becc16e76c4f04","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"intrapolynomial-cryptography.html","entry":true,"size":3873}],"content_path":"content/B59-106.006","local":["content/B59-106.006/intrapolynomial-cryptography.html"]},{"id":"B59-201.001","title":"Blind Signatures for Untraceable Payments","author":"David Chaum","date":"1982-08-23","type":"writing","description":"The paper that makes untraceable electronic money possible. A blind signature lets a bank sign a token without learning what it signed; the user later unblinds it into a valid, bank-signed coin the bank cannot recognize as the one it issued. The bank still controls issuance and can detect double-spending, but cannot link a coin to a withdrawal. This is the mechanism behind DigiCash, ecash, and every later Chaumian-mint design.","excerpt":"A signature you can obtain on a message without the signer seeing the message, the cryptographic core of anonymous digital cash.","citation":"David Chaum, \"Blind Signatures for Untraceable Payments,\" in D. Chaum, R. L. Rivest, and A. T. Sherman (eds.), Advances in Cryptology: Proceedings of CRYPTO ’82, Plenum Press, 1983, pp. 199-203.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from a university course mirror (hit.bme.hu, Budapest University of Technology) on 2026-08-31. No author-hosted or open-access copy of this CRYPTO ’82 paper could be located and the Springer/Plenum original is paywalled; Internet Archive was offline for a Wayback attempt. The file is a SCANNED IMAGE PDF: no machine-readable text could be extracted in the archiving environment and no OCR was performed, so no verbatim excerpt is transcribed on the reader page below. The scan is preserved unmodified as the source snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. A verified transcription and a second-source check are good follow-up tasks.","hash":"1f251b9d511ac8951e673cdc5d775e12b5181ad32f8af122af6decbea970ff09","tags":["blind-signatures","digital-cash","david-chaum","untraceable-payments","privacy","primary-source","1982"],"files":[{"path":"blind-signatures-for-untraceable-payments.html","size":4537,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"blind-signatures-for-untraceable-payments-source.pdf","size":269934,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-201.001","local":["content/B59-201.001/blind-signatures-for-untraceable-payments.html"]},{"id":"B59-201.002","title":"Down with Observers!","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-22","type":"writing","description":"Essay critiquing David Chaum's later turn toward tamper-proof hardware \"observer\" chips for digital cash systems. Finney argues that observer-based protocols compromise the privacy guarantees that made Chaum's earlier blind-signature work so important, trading mathematical certainty for trust in opaque hardware. An early articulation of the principle that cryptographic privacy must be enforced by math, not by hardware secrets.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 22). \"Down with Observers!\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/anti_observers.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"d80e5ddebac316eaa9d72845578dc5714d335a24f858e6a0e8bf2b47d3ce8a5d","tags":["hal-finney","david-chaum","digital-cash","observers","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"down-with-observers.html","size":4040,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-201.002","local":["content/B59-201.002/down-with-observers.html"]},{"id":"B59-201.003","title":"Chaum's Cash System: Detecting Double-Spending","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-10-15","type":"writing","description":"Widely-circulated technical writeup explaining the mathematics behind David Chaum's basic digital cash system, particularly how the protocol allows honest users to remain anonymous while exposing the identity of anyone who attempts to double-spend a digital coin. Finney's clearest exposition of the cut-and-choose blind signature protocol that made anonymous-but-accountable digital cash possible.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, October 15). \"Detecting Double-Spending.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/chcash2.html. Widely republished on the net.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"40c3932e4bac1728e8c423d97c4d26731ededb913335cb070af1ad33176d74e4","tags":["hal-finney","david-chaum","digital-cash","double-spending","blind-signatures","cryptography","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"chaums-cash-system-detecting-double-spending.html","size":15747,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-201.003","local":["content/B59-201.003/chaums-cash-system-detecting-double-spending.html"]},{"id":"B59-201.004","title":"David Chaum's Patents on Blind Signatures and Digital Cash","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1995-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Results of Hal Finney's automated patent search on blind-signature-based electronic cash systems, listing patents held by David Chaum and others including Ohta at Nippon Telegraph and Telephone. Compiled as a practical reference for anyone considering implementing digital cash, documenting the patent landscape that constrained development of electronic cash systems in the 1990s.","citation":"Finney, H. (c. 1995). \"Patent Search on Blind Signatures.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/chaum_patents.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via fennetic.net archive. Date approximate based on content context and Finney's active research period on digital cash implementations.","hash":"d14822b19baf22e357160d877b5fb531409d661618e5e467a3040957302bc341","tags":["hal-finney","david-chaum","patents","blind-signatures","digital-cash","legal","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"chaum-patents.html","size":9350,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"chaum-patents.mht","size":15232,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-201.004","local":["content/B59-201.004/chaum-patents.html"]},{"id":"B59-202.001","title":"NetCash: First Reaction","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-17","type":"writing","description":"Finney's initial critical analysis of NetCash, an early digital payment proposal. The essay examines the trade-offs between centralized and decentralized digital payment architectures and identifies weaknesses in systems that depended on trusted intermediaries rather than cryptographic guarantees.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 17). \"NetCash (First Reaction).\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/netcash_crit.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"7bf722559bf632446c85d4599297c16145dc104f9dbe0316ca979e10c07623da","tags":["hal-finney","netcash","digital-cash","criticism","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"netcash-first-reaction.html","size":2302,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-202.001","local":["content/B59-202.001/netcash-first-reaction.html"]},{"id":"B59-202.002","title":"The Beauty of ECash","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-03-16","type":"writing","description":"Finney's appreciation of the elegance of David Chaum's ecash protocol. The essay explains why the cryptographic design of ecash represents a particularly elegant solution to the problem of creating digital cash that is simultaneously anonymous, verifiable, and resistant to double-spending. A clear statement of why Chaum's blind signature approach was, to Finney, the right approach.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, March 16). \"The Beauty of ECash.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/beauty_ecash.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"9b33cf342c27366ba5e2454c80e5b054c075d8fe45fe4d3e93aeb98254d2e80e","tags":["hal-finney","ecash","david-chaum","digital-cash","blind-signatures","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-beauty-of-ecash.html","size":5802,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-202.002","local":["content/B59-202.002/the-beauty-of-ecash.html"]},{"id":"B59-203.001","title":"Security without Identification: Card Computers to Make Big Brother Obsolete","author":"David Chaum","date":"1985-10-01","type":"writing","description":"Chaum’s manifesto-length CACM article laying out an entire alternative architecture for automated transactions: one where individuals hold a \"card computer,\" prove entitlements with unlinkable credentials, and pay with untraceable digital cash, so that organizations get the security and efficiency of automation without building a surveillance infrastructure. It reframes privacy not as secrecy but as a design choice made early, before the \"institutional momentum\" of identity-based systems becomes irreversible.","excerpt":"Computerization is robbing individuals of the ability to monitor and control the ways information about them is used.","citation":"David Chaum, \"Security without Identification: Transaction Systems to Make Big Brother Obsolete,\" Communications of the ACM, vol. 28, no. 10, October 1985, pp. 1030-1044.","provenance":"Primary-source HTML retrieved by curl from David Chaum’s own site (chaum.com/security-without-identification/) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. This is the author’s official reprint. The page renders equations and figures as external image files hosted on chaum.sknodelab.com; those images are NOT included in this snapshot, so the archived HTML carries the full running prose but not the displayed formulas. The surrounding markup is the site’s WordPress template. Internet Archive was offline for a Wayback cross-check. SHA-256 is computed over the archived HTML bytes.","hash":"f2dcb1a0d617bc3fe8f9ee17c4df6779ac66dc706310819c8e90e9e511013589","tags":["digital-cash","david-chaum","privacy","credentials","digital-pseudonyms","surveillance","primary-source","1985"],"files":[{"path":"security-without-identification.html","size":4767,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"security-without-identification-source.html","size":177664,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-203.001","local":["content/B59-203.001/security-without-identification.html"]},{"id":"B59-203.002","title":"Thoughts on Digital Cash","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1992-11-30","type":"writing","description":"Hughes working through the mechanics and economics of Chaumian digital cash on the Cypherpunks list in late 1992: blind signatures, the unlinkability requirement, double-spending detection, the role of the issuing bank, and float. One of the list's early attempts to move digital cash from a paper primitive toward something implementable.","citation":"Eric Hughes, \"thoughts on digital cash,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 30 November 1992.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: hughes@soda.berkeley.edu). \"thoughts on digital cash\" was a thread; this record is Hughes's message in it. SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"18bfeadb6556c8beb122fb0da17456b9582bf5b2afb3b575bc728a8edcc04f48","tags":["cypherpunks","eric-hughes","primary-source","digital-cash","mailing-list","1992"],"excerpt":"The whole thing hinges on the bank not being able to link the withdrawal to the deposit.","files":[{"path":"thoughts-on-digital-cash.html","size":4737,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"thoughts-on-digital-cash-source.html","size":11085,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-203.002","local":["content/B59-203.002/thoughts-on-digital-cash.html"]},{"id":"B59-203.003","title":"Digital Cash and Privacy","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-19","type":"writing","description":"Essay on the role of digital cash in protecting privacy as more transactions move online. Finney argues that the financial system's shift toward electronic payments without anonymous cash equivalents would produce unprecedented transaction surveillance, and that systems like Chaum's ecash were necessary to preserve the privacy properties of physical cash in the digital domain.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 19). \"Digital Cash and Privacy.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/dig_cash_priv.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org/~hal/ via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine. finney.org is currently inaccessible; this archive preserves the essay as it appeared on Hal Finney's personal homepage. Original publication date as indicated in the document text.","hash":"8e7f2f16345663812fcc1240ad4b336bb6f7779ca11713a2993ba4229edbb54b","tags":["hal-finney","digital-cash","privacy","ecash","surveillance","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"digital-cash-and-privacy.html","size":6338,"label":"Read Essay"}],"content_path":"content/B59-203.003","local":["content/B59-203.003/digital-cash-and-privacy.html"]},{"id":"B59-203.004","title":"Is-A-Person Credentials","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-26","type":"writing","description":"Proposes a Chaum-style blind-signature credential system that could verify whether a network identity corresponds to a unique real person without revealing which person, preventing abuse via multiple pseudonyms while preserving anonymity. An early exploration of what would later become known as proof-of-personhood or sybil resistance -- problems still central to digital identity and cryptocurrency systems decades later.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 26). \"Is-A-Person Credentials.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/is_a_person.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive. The essay applies David Chaum's blind signature protocols to the identity verification problem.","hash":"301285bdedf92605d2ebc4e0cd7697e0293009616b3391a1649cab4f96f0c98f","tags":["hal-finney","digital-identity","blind-signatures","david-chaum","credentials","sybil-resistance","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"is-a-person-credentials.html","size":5679,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"is-a-person-credentials.mht","size":11585,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-203.004","local":["content/B59-203.004/is-a-person-credentials.html"]},{"id":"B59-203.005","title":"James A. Donald: essays on digital cash and the web of trust","author":"James A. Donald","date":"1998-12-05","type":"writing","description":"Three essays from James A. Donald’s personal site (jim.com), spanning the late-1990s cypherpunk period and his later restatement of the programme. \"Money\" (money.htm, 1998) argues money as a tool of exchange and sets up his interest in a purely private digital cash. \"The Cypherpunk Program\" restates Tim May’s crypto-anarchy checklist and argues that correct cryptography turned out to be only the beginning of what was needed. \"How to Save the World\" (the jim.com/security/ index) is his web-of-trust position: an identity system built on Zooko’s triangle, \"allowing everyone to have as many IDs as they want, but no one else can forge their IDs.\" Donald was Satoshi’s main early interlocutor on the cryptography list (B59-400.002).","citation":"James A. Donald, \"Money,\" \"The Cypherpunk Program,\" and \"How to Save the World,\" jim.com, c. 1998–2009. Retrieved via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.","provenance":"The domain jim.com no longer hosts James A. Donald’s writing (it now serves an unrelated commercial blog) and echeque.com is a parked domain, so these pages were retrieved from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine using raw (id_) snapshots: money.htm from the 1998-12-05 capture of http://www.jim.com/jamesd/money.htm; the jim.com/security/ index from the 2008-09-25 capture; cypherpunk_program.html from the 2008-08-19 capture. Donald’s site carried no explicit licence; the pages were freely published. Stored unmodified as retrieved. SHA-256 is computed over money.htm (the primary file); per-file SHA-256 in the manifest. Note: money.htm opens with a long verbatim quotation from Ayn Rand’s \"Atlas Shrugged\" (the Francisco d’Anconia money speech) embedded by the author within his own essay.","hash":"a7878ff9c556d252a34ef1226a27892595b0aebd6e8d4e6694f2761634739c07","tags":["james-donald","digital-cash","web-of-trust","cypherpunks","bitcoin-precursor","wayback","primary-source"],"excerpt":"The solution is an ID system based on Zooko’s triangle, allowing everyone to have as many IDs as they want, but no one else can forge their IDs.","files":[{"path":"james-donald-essays.html","size":12820,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"jamesd-money.htm","size":14865,"label":"\"Money\" essay — jim.com/jamesd/money.htm (Primary Source)"},{"path":"jim-com-security-index.html","size":4477,"label":"\"How to Save the World\" — jim.com/security/"},{"path":"jamesd-cypherpunk-program.html","size":6516,"label":"\"The Cypherpunk Program\" — jim.com/security/cypherpunk_program.html"}],"content_path":"content/B59-203.005","local":["content/B59-203.005/james-donald-essays.html"]},{"id":"B59-204.001","title":"b-money","author":"Wei Dai","date":"1998-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Wei Dai’s b-money proposal, announced on the cypherpunks mailing list in November 1998 and hosted at weidai.com/bmoney.txt. Describes \"a scheme for a group of untraceable digital pseudonyms to pay each other with money and to enforce contracts amongst themselves without outside help.\" Two protocols: one where every participant keeps a copy of every account balance and money is created by broadcasting the solution to an unsolved computational problem (proof-of-work) priced in a standard basket of commodities; and a more practical one with a subset of bonded servers. Cited as reference [1] in the Bitcoin white paper.","citation":"Wei Dai, \"b-money,\" 1998. http://www.weidai.com/bmoney.txt","provenance":"Plain-text file retrieved with curl from http://www.weidai.com/bmoney.txt (HTTP 200, Last-Modified 20 April 1999, 8,312 bytes) — the author’s own long-standing hosting of the essay. Stored unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over the plain-text file. Satoshi cited this exact URL, dated 1998, as reference [1] of \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System.\"","hash":"90b8784420809599b422ec61b374db26a65e2b0539ffb4764f3a68c245312415","tags":["b-money","proof-of-work","digital-cash","bitcoin-precursor","wei-dai","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Anyone can create money by broadcasting the solution to a previously unsolved computational problem. ... The number of monetary units created is equal to the cost of the computing effort in terms of a standard basket of commodities.","files":[{"path":"b-money.html","size":12011,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"bmoney.txt","size":8312,"label":"Original Text (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-204.001","local":["content/B59-204.001/b-money.html"]},{"id":"B59-204.002","title":"A Measure of Sacrifice","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2001-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on the concept of proof of work as a measure of sacrifice â€” exploring how time, energy, and computation can serve as a trustworthy signal of commitment and value. A conceptual precursor to Bitcoin's proof-of-work mechanism, framed through the history of labor contracts and synchronization protocols.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 2001). A Measure of Sacrifice. Retrieved from https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/rob/Courses/InformationInSpeech/CDROM/Literature/LOTwinterschool2006/szabo.best.vwh.net/synch.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net. Original filename: synch.html.","hash":"c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","proof-of-work","sacrifice","time","digital-property","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"a-measure-of-sacrifice.html","entry":true,"size":97957}],"content_path":"content/B59-204.002","local":["content/B59-204.002/a-measure-of-sacrifice.html"]},{"id":"B59-204.003","title":"Bit Gold","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2005-12-29","type":"writing","description":"The seminal proposal for Bit Gold - a digital currency system using proof-of-work to create unforgeable scarce digital tokens. Originally circulated on the libtech mailing list in 1998 and published on the Unenumerated blog in 2005. A direct conceptual precursor to Bitcoin's design.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2005). \"Bit Gold.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bit-gold/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bit-gold/","hash":"6e540747c324bb62039abdaf85bf8e788bb4cbdf1bd74fbf820adfb033e94673","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","bit-gold","bitcoin"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"bit-gold.html","size":57980,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-204.003","local":["content/B59-204.003/bit-gold.html"]},{"id":"B59-204.004","title":"Bit Gold Markets","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2008-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Follow-up writing on the market design for Bit Gold, examining how the proof-of-work tokens would establish value and circulate. Refinements to the original Bit Gold proposal addressing market dynamics.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2008). \"Bit Gold Markets.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bit-gold-markets/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bit-gold-markets/","hash":"8326df260f871c56d313a1ede64b7b0b37b42de6f5a4d37ea77e530c3d1bf01a","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","bit-gold"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"bit-gold-markets.html","size":60775,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-204.004","local":["content/B59-204.004/bit-gold-markets.html"]},{"id":"B59-204.005","title":"Wei Dai’s Home Page (weidai.com)","author":"Wei Dai","date":"2026-08-31","type":"writing","description":"Snapshot of weidai.com, Wei Dai’s personal index page, retrieved August 2026 (page Last-Modified January 2021). The single hub linking his cryptography and anonymity work: Crypto++, the b-money essay, PipeNet and the \"two attacks\" note on a PipeNet-like protocol used by the Freedom network, an attack on SSH2, a note on Lucas sequences, plus his writing on the social costs of intelligence and on ethics and decision theory. Primary context for the b-money and PipeNet records.","citation":"Wei Dai, personal home page, http://www.weidai.com/ , snapshot retrieved 31 August 2026.","provenance":"HTML retrieved with curl from http://www.weidai.com/ on 31 August 2026 (HTTP 200, text/html, 4,274 bytes, Last-Modified 11 January 2021). Stored unmodified as the source snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the archived HTML.","hash":"f95a2052f479706bb3ef5d087ac1993b04dd162121196ecfaa772eb14e5f4d4f","tags":["wei-dai","b-money","digital-cash","bitcoin-precursor","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":"b-money, a scheme for a group of untraceable digital pseudonyms to pay each other with money and to enforce contracts amongst themselves without outside help.","files":[{"path":"wei-dai-home-page.html","size":5247,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"weidai-com-2021-snapshot.html","size":4274,"label":"Original Page Snapshot (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-204.005","local":["content/B59-204.005/wei-dai-home-page.html"]},{"id":"B59-205.001","title":"PayWord and MicroMint: Two Simple Micropayment Schemes","author":"Ronald L. Rivest & Adi Shamir","date":"1996-04-01","type":"writing","description":"Two schemes for payments too small to justify a public-key operation each. PayWord is a hash chain: sign the tip once, then spend by walking the chain one hash at a time, so a run of micropayments costs one signature. MicroMint mints \"coins\" as hash collisions, cheap in bulk for the broker and hard to forge in small quantities. Both are direct ancestors of later hash-chain payment ideas, including payment channels.","excerpt":"Our main goal is to minimize the number of public-key operations required per payment, using hash operations instead whenever possible.","citation":"Ronald L. Rivest and Adi Shamir, \"PayWord and MicroMint: Two Simple Micropayment Schemes,\" in M. Lomas (ed.), Security Protocols, International Workshop, Cambridge, April 1996, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 1189, Springer, 1997, pp. 69-87.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ronald Rivest’s official MIT CSAIL publications directory (people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/pubs/RS96a.pdf) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed.","hash":"72a655e85b5fcf8752cb8aba85ca640013b656d89d6c581b5b9f9a6aa4c19b12","tags":["micropayments","digital-cash","hash-chains","ronald-rivest","adi-shamir","payword","micromint","primary-source","1996"],"files":[{"path":"payword-and-micromint.html","size":4541,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"payword-and-micromint-source.pdf","size":1282755,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-205.001","local":["content/B59-205.001/payword-and-micromint.html"]},{"id":"B59-205.002","title":"Electronic Lottery Tickets as Micropayments","author":"Ronald L. Rivest","date":"1997-02-01","type":"writing","description":"A probabilistic approach to micropayments: instead of paying one cent, hand over a ticket worth ten dollars with a one-in-a-thousand chance of being valid. On average the vendor is paid correctly, but the bank only ever processes the rare winners, so per-payment overhead collapses. The first payment scheme where the bank does not see every transaction, an idea that resurfaces in Lightning and in later aggregated-settlement designs.","excerpt":"This scheme is exceptionally efficient since the bank handles only winning tickets, instead of handling each micropayment.","citation":"Ronald L. Rivest, \"Electronic Lottery Tickets as Micropayments,\" in R. Hirschfeld (ed.), Financial Cryptography ’97, Anguilla, February 1997, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 1318, Springer, 1997, pp. 307-314.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from Ronald Rivest’s official MIT CSAIL publications directory (people.csail.mit.edu/rivest/pubs/Riv97b.pdf) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes. Internet Archive was offline at archiving time so a Wayback cross-check could not be completed.","hash":"32a271ab6bdbf5649aa936a06a71a270e9faf4ee35b80899ad4658ac292c0c85","tags":["micropayments","digital-cash","probabilistic-payments","ronald-rivest","primary-source","1997"],"files":[{"path":"electronic-lottery-tickets-as-micropayments.html","size":4185,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"electronic-lottery-tickets-as-micropayments-source.pdf","size":613121,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-205.002","local":["content/B59-205.002/electronic-lottery-tickets-as-micropayments.html"]},{"id":"B59-205.003","title":"Micropayments and Mental Transaction Costs","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1999-05-01","type":"writing","description":"Presented at the 2nd Berlin Internet Economics Workshop, May 1999. Argues that the failure of micropayment systems is due not just to technical barriers but to the cognitive burden of evaluating many small transactions - the \"mental transaction cost\" barrier.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1999). \"Micropayments and Mental Transaction Costs.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/micropayments-and-mental-transaction-costs/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/micropayments-and-mental-transaction-costs/","hash":"b13386ce2723fcaac12ff56906866a389ed28a451ce24bfba617044929f40d0c","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","micropayments"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"micropayments-mental-transaction-costs.html","size":141922,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-205.003","local":["content/B59-205.003/micropayments-mental-transaction-costs.html"]},{"id":"B59-301.001","title":"DES Review at Stanford University -- NBS/NSA Meeting Transcript, 1976","author":"John Gilmore (transcription), various speakers","date":"1976-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Transcript of the historic 1976 meeting at Stanford University between Stanford computer science researchers, the National Bureau of Standards (NBS), and the National Security Agency (NSA) to discuss the proposed Data Encryption Standard (DES). This meeting was a pivotal moment in the Crypto Wars, where academic cryptographers challenged the government's encryption standard and the NSA's role in weakening it. Background notes by John Gilmore. Transcribed and published to preserve the historical record of government-academic conflict over public cryptographic standards.","citation":"Gilmore, J. (transcriber). (1976/2000s). \"DES Review at Stanford University: A meeting between Stanford computer science researchers, NBS, and NSA, 1976.\" Transcription published at toad.com/des-stanford-meeting.html.","provenance":"Transcript prepared by John Gilmore from recordings of the original 1976 meeting. Published at toad.com. Retrieved from Internet Archive Wayback Machine (toad.com currently offline). The meeting documents the earliest public confrontation between academic cryptographers and US intelligence agencies over encryption standards.","hash":"","tags":["des","nsa","nbs","stanford","crypto-wars","encryption-standards","john-gilmore","transcript","1976","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"http://web.archive.org/web/20260417033144/http://www.toad.com/des-stanford-meeting.html"},{"id":"B59-301.002","title":"Cracking DES: Secrets of Encryption Research, Wiretap Politics, and Chip Design","author":"Electronic Frontier Foundation (John Gilmore, project lead)","date":"1998-05-01","type":"writing","description":"The EFF’s 1998 book documenting \"Deep Crack,\" the US $250,000 machine it built to break the Data Encryption Standard by brute force. The machine won RSA’s DES Challenge II in July 1998, recovering a key in about 56 hours and disproving government claims that cracking DES required multimillion-dollar resources and months of time. Because export controls barred publishing the design as software, the EFF published it as a printed book and placed it in the public domain. This transcription carries Whitfield Diffie’s foreword, the preface and Chapter 1 overview by John Gilmore, and the design and cryptanalysis chapters.","citation":"Electronic Frontier Foundation, \"Cracking DES: Secrets of Encryption Research, Wiretap Politics, and Chip Design,\" 1st ed., May 1998. Distributed by O’Reilly & Associates. Public domain except where noted.","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. Full-length commercial book (Electronic Frontier Foundation, distributed by O'Reilly & Associates, 1st ed. May 1998). A full-text transcription assembled by Cryptome is available at https://cryptome.org/jya/cracking-des/cracking-des.htm; this record links there rather than hosting a copy. The transcription (SHA-256 8c05ec70ccd9852d420c50acb6c0afdb77d90ef1c1beceb5d3f03e666b9bdc9b) is parked in the reconciliation held-copyright area (C:/Users/katja/AppData/Local/Temp/b59-merge/held-copyright/B59-301.002-cracking-des) so the decision is reversible. The separate EFF DES Cracker FAQ (B59-301.003) is EFF's own material and stays hosted.","hash":"","tags":["des","encryption","crypto-wars","export-controls","free-speech","eff","john-gilmore","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://cryptome.org/jya/cracking-des/cracking-des.htm"},{"id":"B59-301.003","title":"Frequently Asked Questions About the EFF DES Cracker","author":"Electronic Frontier Foundation (John Gilmore, project lead)","date":"1998-07-16","type":"writing","description":"The plain-language question-and-answer document the EFF released with the DES Cracker on 16 July 1998. It defines cryptography and cryptanalysis, explains what DES is and who relied on it, sets out the government claims the machine was built to test, describes how Deep Crack worked and what it cost, gives guidance on key lengths, and argues that export controls on cryptographic research delayed the public demonstration of DES’s weakness by years.","citation":"Electronic Frontier Foundation, \"Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) About the Electronic Frontier Foundation’s ‘DES Cracker’ Machine,\" 16 July 1998.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 from the EFF’s own legacy server (w2.eff.org/Privacy/Crypto/Crypto_misc/DESCracker/HTML/19980716_eff_des_faq.html); byte-identical copy confirmed at foo.be/docs/eff-des-cracker/text/19980716_eff_des_faq.html. Archived unmodified alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published under Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY).","hash":"cef4ec9314c8df8b705bc7bfa27150f7f2f186d42f738f51b36aee8ef59b15d3","tags":["des","encryption","crypto-wars","export-controls","eff","john-gilmore","primary-source"],"excerpt":"EFF’s DES Cracker entered and won the RSA DES Challenge II-2 competition in less than 3 days, proving that DES is not secure and that such a machine is inexpensive to design and build.","files":[{"path":"eff-des-cracker-faq.html","size":28565,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"eff-des-cracker-faq-source.html","size":28663,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-301.003","local":["content/B59-301.003/eff-des-cracker-faq.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.001","title":"The Crypto Anarchist Manifesto","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1988-06-01","type":"writing","description":"The founding text of crypto anarchism. Written mid-1988 and read aloud at the founding meeting of the Cypherpunks in September 1992, it argues that public-key cryptography and anonymous networks will let individuals transact and communicate without revealing their identity, structurally limiting the ability of any government to regulate, tax, or surveil those interactions. Distributed at the Crypto ’88 and Hackers Conference gatherings before being posted to the Cypherpunks mailing list in November 1992.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"The Crypto Anarchist Manifesto,\" written mid-1988, posted to the Cypherpunks mailing list 22 November 1992.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly from the activism.net cypherpunk archive (activism.net/cypherpunk/crypto-anarchy.html), a long-standing, widely-cited mirror of primary cypherpunk-era documents; archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. Independent cross-check against a second mirror was attempted (cypherpunks.venona.com, cryptome.org) but neither was reachable at archiving time; text verified by direct comparison against the well-established, widely-quoted original wording.","hash":"7512b14fcb84d414fb0b4515d0a3e0bc08e5b1a359abe365a4665cccb2d20cd1","tags":["crypto-anarchy","tim-may","primary-source","cypherpunks","manifesto","1988","cryptography","privacy"],"excerpt":"A specter is haunting the modern world, the specter of crypto anarchy.","files":[{"path":"crypto-anarchist-manifesto.html","size":6997,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"crypto-anarchy-manifesto-source.html","size":5508,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.001","local":["content/B59-302.001/crypto-anarchist-manifesto.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.002","title":"St. Jude and the Origin of the Word \"Cypherpunk\"","author":"Project B59 Research","date":"1992-09-01","type":"writing","description":"An assembled record documenting how the word \"cypherpunk\" was coined by Judith \"Jude\" Milhon, who wrote as St. Jude: self-taught programmer on the Berkeley Computer Company timesharing system, co-founder of Community Memory (1973), civil-rights organiser, and senior editor at Mondo 2000. At one of the first meetings of the mailing list that Eric Hughes, Tim May and John Gilmore convened in the autumn of 1992, she called the participants \"a bunch of cypherpunks,\" a pun on cipher and the cyberpunk genre; the list and the movement took the name at once. Milhon left comparatively little freely licensed primary text, so this record is built from near-primary attestations and a CC BY-SA biography.","citation":"Project B59 Research, \"St. Jude and the Origin of the Word ‘Cypherpunk’,\" 2026. Assembled from Tim May, The Cyphernomicon (1994) §2.4.10; Steven Levy, Crypto (2001); The Guardian obituary (8 August 2003); Wired, \"Modem Grrrl\" (1 February 1995).","provenance":"This is an assembled Project B59 record, not a single primary document. The hosted source snapshot is the English Wikipedia article \"Jude Milhon\" (Wikimedia REST API render, revision 1336650540), fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 and reused under CC BY-SA 4.0; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot. The coinage itself is documented from near-primary sources cited on the reader page: The Cyphernomicon (freely redistributable, quoted under its fair-use terms), Levy’s Crypto and the Guardian and Wired pieces (in copyright, cited only). No substantial body of freely hostable primary text authored by Milhon was located; her Mondo 2000 columns and the books How to Mutate and Take Over the World and The Cyberpunk Handbook are in copyright, and the one archived interview found (verbosity zine, 1996) is marked all rights reserved by its publisher. See the scrape manifest for the gap note.","hash":"3292630b74e0c12e9fe796fc5ee77c5256f93746f414c34daf49eced37c0848d","tags":["cypherpunks","mondo-2000","privacy","etymology","primary-source","john-gilmore"],"excerpt":"You guys are just a bunch of cypherpunks.","files":[{"path":"origin-of-the-word-cypherpunk.html","size":8760,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"jude-milhon-wikipedia-source.html","size":72247,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.002","local":["content/B59-302.002/origin-of-the-word-cypherpunk.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.003","title":"Crypto Rebels (WIRED, 1993) and Crypto (2001)","author":"Steven Levy","date":"1993-02-01","type":"writing","description":"Steven Levy's chronicle of the cypherpunks. \"Crypto Rebels\" (WIRED 1.02, 1993) was the first mainstream feature to name the movement, its list, and its figures (Hughes, May, Gilmore, Chaum, Zimmermann). His book Crypto: How the Code Rebels Beat the Government, Saving Privacy in the Digital Age (Viking, 2001) is the long-form history of public-key cryptography and the crypto wars, from Diffie and Hellman through RSA, PGP, and Bernstein. Linked, not archived: both are under commercial copyright.","citation":"Steven Levy, \"Crypto Rebels,\" WIRED 1.02, 1993. Steven Levy, Crypto: How the Code Rebels Beat the Government, Saving Privacy in the Digital Age, Viking, 2001.","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. \"Crypto Rebels\" is a WIRED magazine feature (Conde Nast); Crypto is a commercial book (Viking Penguin). This record is a citation and a pointer to where each can be read, with no hosted copy, per the vault's link-only policy for full-length commercial works.","hash":"","tags":["steven-levy","cypherpunks","crypto-wars","history-of-cryptography","journalism","wired","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://www.wired.com/1993/02/crypto-rebels/","retrieved":"2026-09-01"},{"id":"B59-302.004","title":"A Cypherpunk’s Manifesto","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1993-03-09","type":"writing","description":"A founding statement of the cypherpunk movement, arguing privacy from first principles: privacy is not secrecy, it is the power to selectively reveal yourself to the world, and in an electronic age it requires cryptography and anonymous transaction systems rather than trust in institutions. Closes with the movement’s working method as much as its slogan: \"Cypherpunks write code.\" First posted to the Cypherpunks mailing list 9 March 1993.","citation":"Eric Hughes, \"A Cypherpunk’s Manifesto,\" 9 March 1993, first posted to the Cypherpunks mailing list.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly from the activism.net cypherpunk archive (activism.net/cypherpunk/manifesto.html), a long-standing, widely-cited mirror of primary cypherpunk-era documents; archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. Independent cross-check against a second mirror was attempted (cypherpunks.venona.com, cryptome.org) but neither was reachable at archiving time; text verified by direct comparison against the well-established, widely-quoted original wording.","hash":"e63fe33f5de9f49b842d02d3d8e42c5f132edb480d6c2173b54084bcf090afe1","tags":["cypherpunks","eric-hughes","primary-source","manifesto","1993","cryptography","privacy","anonymity"],"excerpt":"Cypherpunks write code.","files":[{"path":"a-cypherpunks-manifesto.html","size":8389,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"cypherpunks-manifesto-source.html","size":5883,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.004","local":["content/B59-302.004/a-cypherpunks-manifesto.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.005","title":"Libertaria in Cyberspace","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1993-08-09","type":"writing","description":"A May essay-post arguing that attempts to build physical libertarian enclaves (ocean platforms, micro-states, space colonies) have mostly failed on the vulnerability of physical territory to state power, and that cyberspace, secured by strong cryptography, offers the same goal without the same weakness: jurisdictions of interest rather than of geography, enforced by mathematics instead of borders.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"Libertaria in Cyberspace,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 9 August 1993.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki. The page carries the full raw message; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page. The reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"9ab505ee14ac5ba75e431109db210303f2a76921c67527ab3675afa8b539c56f","tags":["crypto-anarchy","cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","mailing-list","1993"],"excerpt":"Note: I put time and money into writing this posting. I hope you enjoy it.","files":[{"path":"libertaria-in-cyberspace.html","size":13341,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"libertaria-in-cyberspace-source.html","size":17232,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.005","local":["content/B59-302.005/libertaria-in-cyberspace.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.006","title":"Democracy vs. Libertarianism","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-15","type":"writing","description":"Brief essay in which Finney argues that democracy implicitly recognizes the coercive power of large numbers, and that a populace wise enough to accept a libertarian society would likely be trustworthy enough for democracy anyway. A philosophical exploration of the tension between libertarian ideals and democratic governance that was central to cypherpunk political thought.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 15). \"Democracy vs. Libertarianism.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/liberty_demo.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive.","hash":"a8237cc5e4c19d4ccf909f412627c6c906f811e36430949b41e7a8beee1e7c59","tags":["hal-finney","democracy","libertarianism","political-philosophy","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"democracy-vs-libertarianism.html","size":1512,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"democracy-vs-libertarianism.mht","size":7432,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.006","local":["content/B59-302.006/democracy-vs-libertarianism.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.007","title":"Politics vs Technology","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-01-02","type":"writing","description":"Essay posted to the Cypherpunks Mailing List on January 2, 1994, in which Hal Finney argues that technology alone is insufficient to secure privacy and that political action remains necessary. Responding to the prevailing cypherpunk ethos of \"write code, not laws,\" Finney contends that the Clipper chip proposal, the federal investigation of Phil Zimmermann over PGP, and the difficulty of keeping PGP available on US FTP sites demonstrate that political engagement is indispensable alongside technical work.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, January 2). \"Politics vs Technology.\" Posted to the Cypherpunks Mailing List (hal@rain.org). Originally published at finney.org/~hal/pol_v_tech.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine capture dated 13 May 2013. Content verified against known Cypherpunks Mailing List archives. The essay is a direct response to discussions on the list regarding the balance between political activism and code-writing as strategies for securing privacy.","hash":"53e17e2b2f70ccf21e56136040c109792d9bb9fc97a1a8a61b4f6e9a18985cba","tags":["hal-finney","cypherpunks","politics","privacy","pgp","clipper-chip","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"We will have the kind of society that most people want. If we want freedom and privacy, we must persuade others that these are worth having. There are no shortcuts.\"","files":[{"path":"politics-vs-technology.html","size":3778,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"politics-vs-technology.mht","size":9674,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.007","local":["content/B59-302.007/politics-vs-technology.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.008","title":"The Cyphernomicon","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1994-09-10","type":"writing","description":"The Cypherpunks FAQ and the movement's founding reference document, version 0.666, dated 1994-09-10. Around 1.3 MB across 20 chapters, it collects the arguments, history, jargon, and open problems of the early cypherpunks: crypto anarchy, remailers and anonymity, digital cash, reputations, information markets, BlackNet, key escrow and the Clipper fight, digital pseudonyms, and the legal landscape of the first Crypto War. Compiled and self-published by May and distributed by anonymous FTP from ftp.netcom.com.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"The Cyphernomicon: Cypherpunks FAQ and More,\" Version 0.666, 10 September 1994. Self-published; distributed by anonymous FTP (ftp.netcom.com:/pub/tcmay) and posted to the Cypherpunks mailing list.","provenance":"Plain-text snapshot fetched with curl from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute mirror (cdn.nakamotoinstitute.org/docs/cyphernomicon.txt), a long-standing, widely-cited archive of primary cypherpunk-era documents. Archived unmodified as the source file; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot. The reader page reproduces the opening and chapter outline in full and links to the complete source snapshot for the remainder (the full FAQ is preserved verbatim in the source file). Text is consistent with other public copies of v0.666.","hash":"64468fcf04ce2076684a2596f3d697c301cf46dd15cc55141df0b62bab66f8bd","tags":["crypto-anarchy","cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","faq","remailers","digital-cash","1994"],"excerpt":"The Cypherpunks have existed since September, 1992. In that time, a vast amount has been written on cryptography, key escrow, Clipper, the Net, cyber terrorists, and crypto anarchy.","files":[{"path":"the-cyphernomicon.html","size":64044,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"the-cyphernomicon-source.txt","size":1101047,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.008","local":["content/B59-302.008/the-cyphernomicon.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.009","title":"Announcement: “CYPHERNOMICON” FAQ Available","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1994-09-11","type":"writing","description":"May's post to the Cypherpunks mailing list announcing the release of the Cyphernomicon. He explains why it is a 1.3 MB single file rather than a web document, why there is no \"official\" cypherpunks FAQ, which sections are strongest (crypto anarchy, reputations, black markets, digital cash) and which he considers weakest, and gives the anonymous-FTP retrieval instructions. Sent \"blind\" to the list because Netcom mail delivery was failing at the time.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"Announcement: ‘CYPHERNOMICON’ FAQ available,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 11 September 1994.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (HyperKitty-style mirror of the historic Cypherpunks list, sourced from Ryan Lackey's cypherpunks.venona.com archive). The page carries the full raw message; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page. The reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"84136124f758dab589af37c6295825c1414510bd4127acbb569554429d725be1","tags":["cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","mailing-list","faq","1994"],"excerpt":"The FAQ I've been working on for many months is now available by anonymous ftp, details below.","files":[{"path":"cyphernomicon-announcement.html","size":8020,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"cyphernomicon-announcement-source.html","size":11733,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.009","local":["content/B59-302.009/cyphernomicon-announcement.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.010","title":"John Gilmore's Home Page","author":"John Gilmore","date":"1995-01-01","type":"website","description":"Personal homepage of John Gilmore, co-founder of the Electronic Frontier Foundation, co-founder of the Cypherpunks with Eric Hughes and Timothy May, 5th employee at Sun Microsystems, and founder of Cygnus Solutions (sold to Red Hat for $675 million). The page documents his work on encryption export controls, the freedom of movement lawsuit against TSA (Gilmore v. Gonzales), the GNU Radio project, the Free Software movement, criticism of DRM, and his role hosting the original Cypherpunks Mailing List at cypherpunks@toad.com. The live site (toad.com) is currently offline; this record references the Wayback Machine capture.","citation":"Gilmore, J. (c. 1995-2020s). Personal homepage. Originally published at toad.com/gnu/. Wayback Machine capture: web.archive.org/web/20260420104846/http://www.toad.com/gnu/.","provenance":"Original site at toad.com is currently offline (connection refused as of May 2026). Content retrieved from Internet Archive Wayback Machine capture dated April 20, 2026. Gilmore maintained this page for approximately two decades. The site going offline demonstrates exactly the kind of loss The Vault is designed to prevent.","hash":"","tags":["john-gilmore","eff","cypherpunks","sun-microsystems","cygnus","free-software","toad-hall","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"http://web.archive.org/web/20260420104846/http://www.toad.com/gnu/"},{"id":"B59-302.011","title":"Toad Hall -- John Gilmore's Website","author":"John Gilmore","date":"2000-01-01","type":"website","description":"Archived website of Toad Hall, John Gilmore's San Francisco residence that served as a meeting place for early cypherpunks gatherings. Gilmore, co-founder of the Electronic Frontier Foundation and the Cypherpunks Mailing List, maintained toad.com as a hub linking to key documents including Phil Salin's patent office submission on software freedom, Paul Baran's 1964 distributed communications papers, the Gilmore v. Gonzales Supreme Court petition against secret ID requirements for air travel, Linux FreeS/WAN, early Sun User Group tapes (1985-1989), and USENIX newsletters.","citation":"Gilmore, J. (c. 2000s). Toad Hall website. Published at toad.com. Archived as MHT.","provenance":"Retrieved from toad.com. The site served as John Gilmore's personal web presence and a portal to primary documents in cryptography, digital rights, and internet history. Toad Hall (the physical location) hosted early cypherpunks meetings in the San Francisco Bay Area.","hash":"4b933133ffca9498a22a17d3158b0c21c4068140ea67cd227d00730bb2b150e8","tags":["john-gilmore","toad-hall","cypherpunks","eff","website-archive","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"toad-hall.html","size":3626,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"toad-hall.mht","size":4159,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.011","local":["content/B59-302.011/toad-hall.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.012","title":"State and Terrorist Conspiracies","author":"Julian Assange","date":"2006-11-10","type":"writing","description":"The first version of Assange's essay on conspiratorial governance, dated 10 November 2006, weeks before WikiLeaks began publishing. It models an authoritarian regime as a graph of conspirators joined by communication links, argues that leaks raise the internal \"secrecy tax\" and degrade the regime's ability to think and coordinate, and concludes that exposure is therefore a general-purpose lever against unjust power. This snapshot is Cryptome's compiled PDF, which carries both this essay and its revision \"Conspiracy as Governance\" (3 December 2006; see B59-302.013).","citation":"Julian Assange, \"State and Terrorist Conspiracies,\" 10 November 2006. Self-published at iq.org; compiled with the revised essay by Cryptome, 31 July 2010.","provenance":"PDF fetched with curl from cryptome.org/0002/ja-conspiracies.pdf. Cryptome's cover note states the first essay was retrieved from Assange's then-live iq.org and the second from an archive.org capture. The file contains both dated versions plus a short Cryptome header and a blog excerpt; this record foregrounds the 10 November 2006 text, which is not separately hosted anywhere as a standalone file. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the PDF.","hash":"5a4a7bfc7877bc35f02abb211af211956a53a307029b89465bb7269b99b182e7","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","wikileaks","primary-source","conspiracy","2006"],"excerpt":"The more secretive or unjust an organization is, the more leaks induce fear and paranoia in its leadership and planning coterie.","files":[{"path":"state-and-terrorist-conspiracies.html","size":8026,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"state-and-terrorist-conspiracies-source.pdf","size":159446,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.012","local":["content/B59-302.012/state-and-terrorist-conspiracies.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.013","title":"Conspiracy as Governance","author":"Julian Assange","date":"2006-12-03","type":"writing","description":"The revised version of the conspiracy essay, dated 3 December 2006, self-published as conspiracies.pdf on Assange's own site iq.org. It reworks the first draft (B59-302.012) into a tighter argument: a conspiracy is treated as a computational device whose \"thinking\" is the communication between its members, so cutting or poisoning those links, by making leaks likely, throttles the regime's cognition and its grip on power. This snapshot is the standalone six-page PDF as it stood on iq.org in early 2007.","citation":"Julian Assange, \"Conspiracy as Governance,\" 3 December 2006. Self-published at http://iq.org/conspiracies.pdf.","provenance":"PDF fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://iq.org/conspiracies.pdf (snapshot 20070129125831, retrieved via the id_ raw-content endpoint). This is Assange's own hosted copy. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the PDF. The 20070110 and 20070129 captures are byte-identical.","hash":"0da511ad0567018405db510329311e22ad29fdb4a1859facf5810961e744cbf6","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","wikileaks","primary-source","conspiracy","2006"],"excerpt":"We will have identified conspiracy as a type of cognitive device that acts on information acquired from its environment.","files":[{"path":"conspiracy-as-governance.html","size":9604,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"conspiracy-as-governance-source.pdf","size":84796,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.013","local":["content/B59-302.013/conspiracy-as-governance.html"]},{"id":"B59-302.014","title":"IQ.ORG — Selected Correspondence","author":"Julian Assange","date":"2007-08-29","type":"writing","description":"Assange's personal weblog at iq.org, styled \"Selected Correspondence,\" as captured on 20 October 2007. A single reverse-chronological page of dated entries from 2006 and 2007: aphorisms, letters, political notes and short essays, including the announcement of \"State and terrorist conspiracies,\" the widely-quoted \"The Australian lagoon,\" \"Witnessing,\" \"Taming the inner rat,\" and the 29 August 2007 entry on irrationality in argument that opens with the line above. Written in the same period he was building WikiLeaks.","citation":"Julian Assange, \"Selected Correspondence,\" iq.org, entries 2006–2007; snapshot of 20 October 2007.","provenance":"HTML snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://iq.org/ (snapshot 20071020051936, id_ raw-content endpoint). The iq.org front page accreted entries over time; this late-2007 capture holds the fullest run of the 2006–2007 posts on one page. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched HTML. The reader page reproduces the visible text of the blog, opening entries in full and the remainder up to a length cap, with the complete page preserved in the source snapshot.","hash":"926fb371ab6f8fa43297fb7c118b37a3c2b17fe9699fd2807e26fefec9b48c24","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","wikileaks","primary-source","blog-post","2007"],"excerpt":"The truth is not found on the page, but is a wayward sprite that bursts forth from the reader's mind for reasons of its own.","files":[{"path":"iq-org-selected-correspondence.html","size":27476,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"iq-org-selected-correspondence-source.html","size":216027,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-302.014","local":["content/B59-302.014/iq-org-selected-correspondence.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.001","title":"Untraceable Electronic Mail, Return Addresses, and Digital Pseudonyms","author":"David L. Chaum","date":"1981-02-01","type":"writing","description":"The mix network paper. Chaum shows how to defeat traffic analysis (who is talking to whom, and when) by routing messages through a series of relays, each of which decrypts a layer, reorders a batch, and forwards, so that inputs cannot be linked to outputs unless every mix in the chain colludes. It also introduces untraceable return addresses and digital pseudonyms. Every remailer, onion-routing system and Tor descends from this.","excerpt":"A technique... that allows an electronic mail system to hide who a participant communicates with as well as the content of the communication.","citation":"David L. Chaum, \"Untraceable Electronic Mail, Return Addresses, and Digital Pseudonyms,\" Communications of the ACM, vol. 24, no. 2, February 1981, pp. 84-88.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from the Free Haven anonymity bibliography cache (freehaven.net/anonbib/cache/chaum-mix.pdf) on 2026-08-31, a long-running curated academic mirror. Chaum’s own site does not host a clean copy and the ACM original is paywalled; Internet Archive was offline for a Wayback attempt. The text matches the widely reproduced CACM original (page furniture credits R. Rivest as area editor). SHA-256 is computed over the archived PDF bytes.","hash":"f7996dbb8496c100a67db1f627342d0187a1f6625afc021f24610520adc7e1c6","tags":["mix-networks","anonymity","david-chaum","remailers","privacy","traffic-analysis","digital-pseudonyms","primary-source","1981"],"files":[{"path":"untraceable-electronic-mail-return-addresses-digital-pseudonyms.html","size":4789,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"untraceable-electronic-mail-return-addresses-digital-pseudonyms-source.pdf","size":419790,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.001","local":["content/B59-303.001/untraceable-electronic-mail-return-addresses-digital-pseudonyms.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.002","title":"The Hopping Remailer Is Done -- Eric Hughes","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1992-09-25","type":"writing","description":"Email from Eric Hughes to the Cypherpunks Mailing List announcing the completion of the first hopping remailer implementation, written in Perl. The system enabled sequential message forwarding through multiple remailers by parsing a 'Request-Remailing-To' header field. Hughes demonstrated the system successfully forwarding a test message through three sequential hops. He acknowledged the system was 'not at all secure by itself' but proposed that combining message body encryption with the hopping mechanism could conceal routing instructions. This is one of the earliest posts on the Cypherpunks Mailing List and documents the first practical implementation of chained anonymous remailing.","citation":"Hughes, E. (1992, September 25). \"the hopping remailer is done.\" Email to cypherpunks@toad.com. From: hughes@soda.berkeley.edu. Message hash: 2fe5a28bc8c23820dc93de1603b58c1ac84c93a16bc852a9b51eb3faed1aa231.","provenance":"Retrieved from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki, which sources from Ryan Lackey's cypherpunks.venona.com archive. Message authenticated by SHA-256 hash. Original message sent from hughes@soda.berkeley.edu (UC Berkeley) to cypherpunks@toad.com.","hash":"2fe5a28bc8c23820dc93de1603b58c1ac84c93a16bc852a9b51eb3faed1aa231","tags":["eric-hughes","remailers","cypherpunks","mailing-list","perl","anonymous-communication","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki/archive/1992/09/2fe5a28bc8c23820dc93de1603b58c1ac84c93a16bc852a9b51eb3faed1aa231/"},{"id":"B59-303.003","title":"Nuts & Acorns","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1992-10-06","type":"writing","description":"An early Hughes post, from the Cypherpunks list's first month, arguing that identity is the wrong thing to certify in networked communication. What matters for ongoing dealings is persistence, that you are corresponding with the same party each time, not their legal name or physical existence. A concise statement of the pseudonymity model the remailer and digital-cash work would build on.","citation":"Eric Hughes, \"Nuts & Acorns,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 6 October 1992.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki. \"Nuts & Acorns\" was a thread; this record is the message in it written by Hughes (From: hughes@soda.berkeley.edu). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"b80f290754a26ffff06560292da108b21981d17853a6cd98b611c0f6d0925b28","tags":["cypherpunks","eric-hughes","primary-source","anonymous-communication","mailing-list","1992"],"excerpt":"In the electronic world, all you have are persistent pseudonyms. Most of them, I care about only in so far as they remain the same over time.","files":[{"path":"nuts-and-acorns.html","size":5646,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"nuts-and-acorns-source.html","size":10493,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.003","local":["content/B59-303.003/nuts-and-acorns.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.004","title":"Why Remailers I","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1992-11-15","type":"writing","description":"Essay from November 1992 in which Hal Finney recounts how he discovered David Chaum's cryptographic work and Vernor Vinge's science fiction novella \"True Names,\" explaining why anonymous remailers are an important practical step toward realizing the privacy-preserving technologies those visionaries described. One of the earliest articulations of the practical case for anonymous communication infrastructure on the Cypherpunks Mailing List.","citation":"Finney, H. (1992, November 15). \"Why Remailers I.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/why_rem1.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive. Content verified against known Cypherpunks Mailing List archives. This is one of Finney's earliest published essays on the list, written within weeks of the group's founding.","hash":"b58b1fb0a2e61a17e23cf57662297d441f8369a9adf3883560e8a4fdea534c86","tags":["hal-finney","remailers","anonymous-communication","david-chaum","vernor-vinge","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"why-remailers-i.html","size":4701,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"why-remailers-i.mht","size":10572,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.004","local":["content/B59-303.004/why-remailers-i.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.005","title":"Why Remailers II","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-02-24","type":"writing","description":"Follow-up essay explaining the practical purposes of anonymous remailers: defeating traffic analysis on email, enabling whistleblowing, and protecting communications privacy beyond what encryption of message content alone provides. Finney argues that even if message contents are encrypted, the metadata of who communicates with whom remains a powerful surveillance tool that remailers are designed to defeat.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, February 24). \"Why Remailers II.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/why_rem2.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive. Content verified against known Cypherpunks Mailing List archives.","hash":"672b75c2134b793493a00c07fa57f22b095d178e6a2c0ed881a913b5a5b2e4ae","tags":["hal-finney","remailers","anonymous-communication","traffic-analysis","privacy","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"why-remailers-ii.html","size":5563,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"why-remailers-ii.mht","size":11436,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.005","local":["content/B59-303.005/why-remailers-ii.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.006","title":"Steganography No Solution","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1993-08-12","type":"writing","description":"Essay arguing that steganography and cryptography tools alone will not be sufficient to achieve crypto-anarchy. Finney contends that government opposition and the need for public infrastructure such as digital cash systems cannot be overcome by technology alone -- political action remains necessary. A counterpoint to the prevailing cypherpunk emphasis on code over politics.","citation":"Finney, H. (1993, August 12). \"Steganography No Solution.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/steg_no_soln.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive. Content verified against known Cypherpunks Mailing List archives.","hash":"a6a3845418307af567ea8e820f1c236a1ee96b7f03ec9b886ea06711d16dfd74","tags":["hal-finney","steganography","crypto-anarchy","politics","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"steganography-no-solution.html","size":3205,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"steganography-no-solution.mht","size":9122,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.006","local":["content/B59-303.006/steganography-no-solution.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.007","title":"REMAIL: Attacks on Remailers","author":"Eric Hughes","date":"1993-08-31","type":"writing","description":"Hughes's contribution to an August 1993 thread systematically enumerating ways to attack an anonymous remailer network: traffic analysis by message size and timing, flooding, subverted nodes, replay, and spam-based abuse, together with the countermeasures (padding, reordering, latency, message quotas) that later remailer designs adopted. An early piece of the threat model that Mixmaster (B59-303.010 to B59-303.020) was built to answer.","citation":"Eric Hughes, \"REMAIL: Attacks on remailers,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 31 August 1993.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: hughes@ah.com). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"fe8821a66775577ce2b519e660b888ea32eaf31b05c0418db630f4669af465a9","tags":["cypherpunks","eric-hughes","primary-source","remailers","anonymous-communication","mailing-list","1993"],"excerpt":"The whole point of a remailer network is that you have to subvert many of them in order to breach the security of the system.","files":[{"path":"attacks-on-remailers.html","size":3975,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"attacks-on-remailers-source.html","size":9874,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.007","local":["content/B59-303.007/attacks-on-remailers.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.008","title":"Remailer Abuse Prevention","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-03-27","type":"writing","description":"Describes a scheme using blind-signature non-abuse tokens built on the Magic Money digital cash software that would allow anonymous remailers to withhold service from users whose messages generate complaints, without compromising sender anonymity. A practical application of digital cash technology to solve the governance problem of anonymous communication systems.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, March 27). \"Remailer Abuse Prevention.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/remailer_abuse.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive. References Magic Money, an early digital cash implementation circulated on the Cypherpunks Mailing List.","hash":"54e840066e666337cc4c76a316f5cfe5e2afd6413b8d41df043ca487e70bf01c","tags":["hal-finney","remailers","abuse-prevention","digital-cash","magic-money","blind-signatures","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"remailer-abuse-prevention.html","size":5842,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"remailer-abuse-prevention.mht","size":11777,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.008","local":["content/B59-303.008/remailer-abuse-prevention.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.009","title":"For-Pay Remailers","author":"Hal Finney","date":"1994-10-28","type":"writing","description":"Argues that introducing payment for anonymous remailer services would improve their reliability and sustainability, counteracting the problems inherent in altruism-driven remailers: lack of motivation for operators, vulnerability to spam, and poor quality of service. An early argument for economic sustainability in privacy infrastructure -- the same problem that would later drive the design of incentivized networks like Tor and cryptocurrency mining.","citation":"Finney, H. (1994, October 28). \"For-Pay Remailers.\" Originally published at finney.org/~hal/pay_remail.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from finney.org via Wayback Machine archive.","hash":"7285eaeb69de130100f17a1fe94c105e630cbe6ed15613460270d3d3ccd3f740","tags":["hal-finney","remailers","digital-cash","economics","privacy-infrastructure","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"for-pay-remailers.html","size":12645,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"for-pay-remailers.mht","size":18552,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.009","local":["content/B59-303.009/for-pay-remailers.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.010","title":"Mixmaster and Remailers","author":"Lance Cottrell","date":"1995-01-27","type":"writing","description":"Cottrell's essay introducing Mixmaster to the Cypherpunks list: how ordinary cypherpunk (\"type I\") remailers work and where they fail against traffic analysis, and how Mixmaster (\"type II\") answers those failures with fixed-size message packets, a pool that reorders messages, defence against replay, and no useful correlation between a remailer's input and output. First posted with a Macintosh client at a San Diego cypherpunks meeting.","citation":"Lance Cottrell, \"Mixmaster and remailers,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 27 January 1995.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: lcottrell@popmail.ucsd.edu). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim. A later, more polished version of this essay is archived as B59-303.020.","hash":"55ac8425f32bb8bf7681eb1d7f8def7026b876ba13fc30925349cb6a8fd301c0","tags":["cypherpunks","lance-cottrell","primary-source","remailers","mixmaster","anonymous-communication","mailing-list","1995"],"excerpt":"Here is a text version of an essay on Mixmaster and remailers in general.","files":[{"path":"mixmaster-and-remailers.html","size":16781,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mixmaster-and-remailers-source.html","size":20461,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.010","local":["content/B59-303.010/mixmaster-and-remailers.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.011","title":"Mixmaster Client Release","author":"Lance Cottrell","date":"1995-02-05","type":"writing","description":"Cottrell's release announcement for the first widely-available Mixmaster client, a PGP-signed post to the Cypherpunks list pointing to a SPARC build, describing the message format, how chaining and the reordering pool work, and how to run a Mixmaster remailer node. Effectively the project's first README, distributed the way cypherpunk software usually was, as a signed message to the list.","citation":"Lance Cottrell, \"Mixmaster client release,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 5 February 1995.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: lcottrell@popmail.ucsd.edu; message PGP-signed). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"3c299c6b777069d9156c9c35dd22a232064dd35a74cfb168490c43421685b726","tags":["cypherpunks","lance-cottrell","primary-source","remailers","mixmaster","anonymous-communication","mailing-list","1995"],"excerpt":"I have put a SPARC executable for the Mixmaster client on my home page.","files":[{"path":"mixmaster-client-release.html","size":4244,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mixmaster-client-release-source.html","size":8340,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.011","local":["content/B59-303.011/mixmaster-client-release.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.012","title":"Premail Web Server Strangeness","author":"Julian Assange","date":"1995-12-28","type":"writing","description":"An early cypherpunks-list post from Assange writing as proff@suburbia.net, noting how much a web server (here, one fronting the premail remailer-automation tool) leaks about its visitors through CGI environment variables. A small, characteristic piece of his mid-1990s list presence: close attention to incidental information leakage in deployed systems.","citation":"Julian Assange (proff@suburbia.net), \"Premail web server strangeness,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 28 December 1995.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: Julian Assange <proff@suburbia.net>). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"e70dc1bea09d10f827a4cea349a6b069247b3abb74818c440fbed29a643ce329","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","primary-source","remailers","anonymous-communication","mailing-list","1995"],"excerpt":"Surprising amount of information one can gain from an env array, really.","files":[{"path":"proff-premail-web-server.html","size":4780,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"proff-premail-web-server-source.html","size":8711,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.012","local":["content/B59-303.012/proff-premail-web-server.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.013","title":"The Anonymizer","author":"Lance Cottrell / Anonymizer, Inc.","date":"1997-04-03","type":"website","description":"The front page of anonymizer.com as first captured by the Internet Archive on 3 April 1997: the web proxy that let users browse the web without revealing their IP address, browser, or referring page to the sites they visited. The Anonymizer originated in 1995 (from Justin Boyan's work) and was operated commercially by Lance Cottrell's company Infonex / Anonymizer, Inc.; Cottrell ran it for roughly two decades.","citation":"Anonymizer, Inc. (Lance Cottrell), anonymizer.com home page; earliest Internet Archive capture, 3 April 1997.","provenance":"HTML snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://www.anonymizer.com/ (snapshot 19970403034043, id_ raw-content endpoint), with the FAQ page from the same crawl also on file. No standalone 1995 \"launch announcement\" for the Anonymizer was locatable in the available archives (the cypherpunks-list archive and the Wayback Machine); anonymizer.com has no capture before April 1997. This earliest surviving site snapshot is archived here in its place, and the gap is noted in the archiving manifest. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched HTML.","hash":"a9fdd49eeec608a9b0961d9c5b7df075228f459dfc2cee0136610b73b413adfb","tags":["cypherpunks","lance-cottrell","primary-source","anonymous-communication","privacy","website","1997"],"excerpt":"Because on today's Internet, people do know you're a dog.","files":[{"path":"anonymizer-launch.html","size":4415,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"anonymizer-launch-source.html","size":3072,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.013","local":["content/B59-303.013/anonymizer-launch.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.014","title":"Marutukku — Cryptographic Filing System","author":"Julian Assange","date":"1997-10-17","type":"writing","description":"Assange's post to the Cypherpunks list, as proff@iq.org, introducing Marutukku (later released as Rubberhose): a cryptographic filesystem and block device with a deniability property, an eye on portability across BSD and Linux, and a modular cipher layer. The list announcement that corresponds to the project documentation archived here as B59-303.017 to B59-303.019.","citation":"Julian Assange (proff@iq.org), \"Marutukku - cryptographic filing system,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 17 October 1997.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki (From: Julian Assange <proff@iq.org>). SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page; the reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"d28f871bf21a378c9dc4451c75fd72e909d86c40fef2bac6546f3d30c4a2e60e","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","deniable-encryption","primary-source","rubberhose","mailing-list","1997"],"excerpt":"Marutukku (my cryptographic file system/block device) is due for first release soon.","files":[{"path":"proff-marutukku-cfs.html","size":11558,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"proff-marutukku-cfs-source.html","size":15448,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.014","local":["content/B59-303.014/proff-marutukku-cfs.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.015","title":"PipeNet 1.1","author":"Wei Dai","date":"1998-11-26","type":"writing","description":"Wei Dai’s PipeNet: a design for low-latency anonymous communication, developed and discussed on the cypherpunks mailing list in the late 1990s alongside b-money. Nodes identified by public key exchange fixed-rate, constant-size padded packet streams over virtual links; each switch decrypts, checks a sequence number and MAC, re-randomises and re-tags, and forwards in shuffled order. Trades throughput and a global-stall failure mode for strong traffic-analysis resistance — a stricter cousin of the mix networks that led to onion routing and Tor.","citation":"Wei Dai, \"PipeNet 1.1,\" c. 1998. http://www.weidai.com/pipenet.txt (discussed on the cypherpunks mailing list).","provenance":"Plain-text file retrieved with curl from http://www.weidai.com/pipenet.txt (HTTP 200, Last-Modified 26 November 1998, 4,753 bytes) — the author’s own hosting. Stored unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over the plain-text file. The companion \"two attacks\" note (weidai.com/freedom-attacks.txt) against a PipeNet-like protocol used by ZKS’s Freedom network is referenced from Wei Dai’s home page (B59-204.005) but not mirrored here.","hash":"7a09e588a6ddea64d100476a11486e6b38a8ca1af1b01f25bcef9371d1fe39ec","tags":["wei-dai","anonymity","cypherpunks","pipenet","traffic-analysis","bitcoin-precursor","primary-source"],"excerpt":"After an anonymous connection is established, the originating node sends a constant stream of (constant size) packets to a second node at a fixed rate.","files":[{"path":"pipenet.html","size":8309,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"pipenet-1.1.txt","size":4753,"label":"Original Text (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.015","local":["content/B59-303.015/pipenet.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.016","title":"Rubberhose — Mythology","author":"Julian Assange","date":"1999-09-09","type":"writing","description":"The MYTHOLOGY file from the Rubberhose source: a long, discursive essay by Assange on his hunt for a name for the encrypted filesystem, wandering through Greek and Roman deities and finally settling on Marduk/Marutukku from the Babylonian creation epic Enuma Elish. Part joke, part manifesto, it lays out the moral framing of the project, cryptography as a defence against coercion, in the voice Assange used on the cypherpunks list.","citation":"Rubberhose source distribution, MYTHOLOGY, revision 1.2, 9 September 1999. rubberhose.org.","provenance":"Plain-text snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://www.rubberhose.org/current/src/MYTHOLOGY (snapshot 20001206213900, id_ raw-content endpoint). Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot.","hash":"c09711b5c1f3aec960755b0c667e61478192322693b294f1710f0af140cb0a45","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","deniable-encryption","primary-source","rubberhose","1999"],"excerpt":"Here is something to amuse, delight and horrify — the tail of: ONE MAN'S SEARCH FOR A CRYPTOGRAPHIC MYTHOLOGY.","files":[{"path":"rubberhose-mythology.html","size":31262,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"rubberhose-mythology-source.txt","size":27797,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.016","local":["content/B59-303.016/rubberhose-mythology.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.017","title":"Rubberhose (Marutukku) — Project Page","author":"Julian Assange, Ralf-P. Weinmann, Suelette Dreyfus","date":"2000-10-18","type":"website","description":"The front page of rubberhose.org, the project site for Rubberhose (internally, Marutukku), a deniable disk-encryption system for Linux written by Julian Assange with Ralf-P. Weinmann and Suelette Dreyfus. Rubberhose divides a disk into multiple encrypted \"aspects\" so that a user can hand over one passphrase while credibly denying that any others exist, aimed squarely at rubber-hose cryptanalysis, coercion of a key holder. This snapshot lists the documentation set (architecture, security, mythology, guide) and the alpha releases.","citation":"J. Assange, R.-P. Weinmann, S. Dreyfus, \"Rubberhose cryptographically deniable transparent disk encryption system,\" rubberhose.org, c. 1997–2001; snapshot of 18 October 2000.","provenance":"HTML snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://www.rubberhose.org/ (snapshot 20001018231328, id_ raw-content endpoint). Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched HTML. The linked documentation pages are archived separately as B59-303.018 to B59-303.019.","hash":"818680799e44e435a7c2243a8431a99aef7d50e777a80de07665b6b604aa57cd","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","deniable-encryption","primary-source","rubberhose","anonymous-communication","2000"],"excerpt":"Rubberhose transparently and deniably encrypts disk data, minimising the effectiveness of warrants, coercive interrogations and other compulsive mechanisms, such as U.K. RIP legislation.","files":[{"path":"rubberhose-marutukku.html","size":4704,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"rubberhose-marutukku-source.html","size":3337,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.017","local":["content/B59-303.017/rubberhose-marutukku.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.018","title":"Rubberhose — Architecture","author":"Julian Assange, Ralf-P. Weinmann","date":"2000-12-06","type":"writing","description":"The ARCHITECTURE document from the Rubberhose source tree: a diagram and walkthrough of how the system is put together, the KUE kernel module, the Marutukku kernel module, the hose client and hosed daemon communicating over a UNIX socket, the maru.keymap and maru.extent backing files, and the notion of \"aspects\" as separately-keyed views onto one encrypted extent.","citation":"Rubberhose source distribution, doc/ARCHITECTURE, c. 2000. rubberhose.org.","provenance":"Plain-text snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://www.rubberhose.org/current/src/doc/ARCHITECTURE (snapshot 20001206215000, id_ raw-content endpoint). Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot.","hash":"e0c9941f3e5c9901a5902df06df2e3ba0c8e525cf75c208633e93f878561dbe6","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","deniable-encryption","primary-source","rubberhose","code","2000"],"excerpt":"For the file system drivers a marutukku block device looks just like any block device.","files":[{"path":"rubberhose-architecture.html","size":5757,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"rubberhose-architecture-source.txt","size":2627,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.018","local":["content/B59-303.018/rubberhose-architecture.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.019","title":"Rubberhose — Security","author":"Julian Assange, Ralf-P. Weinmann","date":"2000-12-06","type":"writing","description":"The SECURITY document from the Rubberhose source tree: the project's own statement of its threat model and limits, what deniability does and does not buy, the assumptions an adversary is allowed, known weaknesses, and the operational discipline a user needs for the denial to hold.","citation":"Rubberhose source distribution, SECURITY, c. 2000. rubberhose.org.","provenance":"Plain-text snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://www.rubberhose.org/current/src/SECURITY (snapshot 20001206215700, id_ raw-content endpoint). Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot.","hash":"59904f4cbe7c1bd41b01ad924633497bf80c740cbf0f73e39b9ed4771ddca803","tags":["cypherpunks","julian-assange","deniable-encryption","primary-source","rubberhose","2000"],"excerpt":"The security of a deniable cryptosystem rests not only on the cipher but on the plausibility of the denial.","files":[{"path":"rubberhose-security.html","size":6734,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"rubberhose-security-source.txt","size":3581,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.019","local":["content/B59-303.019/rubberhose-security.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.020","title":"Mixmaster & Remailer Attacks","author":"Lance Cottrell","date":"2000-12-09","type":"writing","description":"Cottrell's essay on his own site (obscura.com/~loki/remailer/) explaining how cypherpunk (type I) and Mixmaster (type II) remailers work and cataloguing the attacks against them: shrinking message size, timing correlation, flooding and (n−1) attacks, subverted nodes, replay, and spam abuse, with the Mixmaster countermeasures for each. The consolidated version of the argument he first made to the Cypherpunks list in 1995 (B59-303.010).","citation":"Lance Cottrell, \"Mixmaster & Remailer Attacks,\" obscura.com/~loki/remailer/remailer-essay.html, c. late 1990s.","provenance":"HTML snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine's unmodified capture of http://obscura.com/~loki/remailer/remailer-essay.html (snapshot 20001209094700, id_ raw-content endpoint). obscura.com was Cottrell's personal site and the original Mixmaster home. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched HTML.","hash":"14e890e2f04c421e48a17cdac30d1b048600f1f5981b8deccd67b31ee8d48898","tags":["cypherpunks","lance-cottrell","primary-source","remailers","mixmaster","anonymous-communication","2000"],"excerpt":"The whole point of a remailer network is that you have to subvert many of them in order to breach the security of the system.","files":[{"path":"mixmaster-and-remailer-attacks.html","size":15624,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mixmaster-and-remailer-attacks-source.html","size":12830,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.020","local":["content/B59-303.020/mixmaster-and-remailer-attacks.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.021","title":"Mixminion: Design of a Type III Anonymous Remailer Protocol","author":"George Danezis, Roger Dingledine & Nick Mathewson","date":"2003-05-11","type":"writing","description":"The design paper for Mixminion, the Type III anonymous remailer protocol, presented at the 2003 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy. Mixminion introduced single-use reply blocks (SURBs) that make anonymous replies as secure as forward messages and indistinguishable from them, TLS link encryption with ephemeral keys for forward anonymity, key rotation for replay prevention, directory servers, and advertised exit policies. It was the successor design to Cottrell's Type II Mixmaster and the reference point for later anonymity systems. Len Sassaman, the long-time Mixmaster maintainer, was a core contributor to the Mixminion project and its deployed network; note that the task brief lists him as a co-author of this specific paper, but the paper's authors are Danezis, Dingledine and Mathewson (see manifest).","citation":"George Danezis, Roger Dingledine, and Nick Mathewson, \"Mixminion: Design of a Type III Anonymous Remailer Protocol,\" Proceedings of the 2003 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, May 2003, pp. 2-15.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved 2026-08-31 with curl from the project site (mixminion.net/minion-design.pdf), the authors' own distributed copy; archived unmodified and hosted in full. SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes. Per the final copyright policy (2026-08-31) academic conference papers that are core primary sources are hosted (link-only is reserved for full-length commercial books). The task brief lists Len Sassaman as a co-author; the paper's authors are Danezis, Dingledine and Mathewson (Sassaman was a Mixminion contributor and the Mixmaster maintainer, see B59-303.023). Internet Archive was unreachable for an independent second-source check.","hash":"790d0abe1a22f9ce849eb3386c5adc72aeef7e45bbb5ef5f0392039073f26067","tags":["remailers","mixminion","len-sassaman","roger-dingledine","nick-mathewson","george-danezis","anonymity","cryptography","2003","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Mix nodes cannot distinguish Mixminion forward messages from reply messages, so forward and reply messages share the same anonymity set. ... Mixminion works in a real-world Internet environment, requires little synchronization or coordination between nodes, and protects against known anonymity-breaking attacks as well as or better than other systems with similar design parameters.","files":[{"path":"mixminion-type-iii-remailer.html","size":12430,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mixminion-type-iii-remailer.pdf","size":104137,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.021","local":["content/B59-303.021/mixminion-type-iii-remailer.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.022","title":"Mixmaster Protocol Version 2 (draft-sassaman-mixmaster-03)","author":"Ulf Möller, Lance Cottrell, Peter Palfrader, Len Sassaman","date":"2004-12-29","type":"writing","description":"The IETF Internet-Draft specifying Mixmaster version 2: the type II anonymous remailer protocol. Written by Ulf Möller, Lance Cottrell, Peter Palfrader, and Len Sassaman, it defines the packet format, the header and body structure, the use of RSA and EDE-3DES, the anti-replay and message-reassembly rules, and the reordering pool, and states the anonymity properties and known attacks. The formal specification of the system Cottrell first released in 1995.","citation":"U. Möller, L. Cottrell, P. Palfrader, L. Sassaman, \"Mixmaster Protocol Version 2,\" IETF Internet-Draft draft-sassaman-mixmaster-03.txt, 29 December 2004.","provenance":"Plain-text Internet-Draft fetched with curl from the IETF's own archive (www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-sassaman-mixmaster-03.txt). IETF Internet-Drafts are published for open distribution. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over that file.","hash":"0d4214d3448cf8f21cd0057328538f29ce78ff89a45a0d31522a56921de76a5c","tags":["cypherpunks","lance-cottrell","primary-source","remailers","mixmaster","anonymous-communication","ietf","2004"],"excerpt":"Most e-mail security protocols only protect the message body ... an eavesdropper can still learn who is communicating with whom.","files":[{"path":"mixmaster-protocol-v2.html","size":47720,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mixmaster-protocol-v2-source.txt","size":44203,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.022","local":["content/B59-303.022/mixmaster-protocol-v2.html"]},{"id":"B59-303.023","title":"Mixmaster Protocol Version 2 (IETF Internet-Draft)","author":"Ulf Moeller, Lance Cottrell, Peter Palfrader & Len Sassaman","date":"2004-12-29","type":"reference","description":"The IETF Internet-Draft specifying Mixmaster version 2, the Type II anonymous remailer protocol, filed as draft-sassaman-mixmaster and authored by Ulf Moeller, Lance Cottrell, Peter Palfrader and Len Sassaman (Nomen Abditum Services). Sassaman maintained the Mixmaster reference implementation (the 2.9.x series and the 3.0 line) for years and signed every release; this is the formal write-up of the message format, the mixing and pooling behaviour, replay prevention, key handling and the client/server roles that a Mixmaster node implements. Preserved as a primary-source specification from the anonymous-remailer lineage that runs from the Cypherpunk Type I remailers through Cottrell's Mixmaster to Mixminion (B59-303.021). IETF Internet-Drafts are published public record.","citation":"U. Moeller, L. Cottrell, P. Palfrader, L. Sassaman, \"Mixmaster Protocol Version 2,\" IETF Internet-Draft draft-sassaman-mixmaster-03.txt, 29 December 2004 (work in progress; expired June 2005).","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-sassaman-mixmaster-03.txt. IETF Internet-Drafts are published public record. The draft is archived unmodified as the source file; SHA-256 is computed over those bytes. The styled reader page is templated mechanically from the same text. Filed as draft-sassaman-mixmaster; Len Sassaman (Nomen Abditum Services) is a co-author and maintained the Mixmaster reference implementation. Companion to the Mixminion reference B59-303.021.","hash":"0d4214d3448cf8f21cd0057328538f29ce78ff89a45a0d31522a56921de76a5c","tags":["remailers","mixmaster","len-sassaman","lance-cottrell","peter-palfrader","ulf-moeller","anonymity","ietf","traffic-analysis","2004","primary-source"],"excerpt":"This document describes Mixmaster version 2, a mail transfer protocol designed to protect electronic mail against traffic analysis. Mixmaster is based on David Chaum's mix concept: messages are relayed through a chain of anonymous remailers, re-encrypted and reordered at each hop.","files":[{"path":"mixmaster-protocol-version-2.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":47285},{"path":"mixmaster-protocol-version-2-source.txt","label":"Original Internet-Draft (Primary Source)","size":44203}],"content_path":"content/B59-303.023","local":["content/B59-303.023/mixmaster-protocol-version-2.html"]},{"id":"B59-304.001","title":"Why I Wrote PGP","author":"Philip R. Zimmermann","date":"1991-06-01","type":"writing","description":"Zimmermann's statement of purpose for Pretty Good Privacy, first written as the rationale section of the original 1991 PGP User's Guide and expanded in 1999. It argues that private communication is a natural right, not a privilege the state grants, and that in a networked age that right can only be preserved with strong cryptography in ordinary hands. The 1999 additions warn that a communications infrastructure optimised for surveillance, once built, is almost impossible to dismantle.","citation":"Philip R. Zimmermann, \"Why I Wrote PGP,\" part of the original 1991 PGP User’s Guide, revised 1999. Published at philzimmermann.com/EN/essays/WhyIWrotePGP.html.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly from the author’s own site (philzimmermann.com/EN/essays/WhyIWrotePGP.html) with curl on 2026-08-31; archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. The page carries the 1999 revision of the essay; the 1991 original appears in the first PGP User’s Guide, preserved separately in this collection as B59-304.002.","hash":"a0f5f871189db32f3030a6a40f61c11031678a2949ad45289477be9f0de9d65a","tags":["pgp","encryption","crypto-wars","free-speech","cypherpunks","privacy","export-controls","primary-source"],"excerpt":"It's personal. It's private. And it's no one's business but yours.","files":[{"path":"why-i-wrote-pgp.html","size":16229,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"why-i-wrote-pgp-source.html","size":13342,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-304.001","local":["content/B59-304.001/why-i-wrote-pgp.html"]},{"id":"B59-304.002","title":"PGP User’s Guide, Volume I: Essential Topics","author":"Philip R. Zimmermann","date":"1994-10-11","type":"writing","description":"The manual shipped with MIT’s PGP 2.6.2 in 1994. Volume I covers the concepts every user needs: how public-key encryption works, key generation and management, protecting public keys from tampering, how PGP decides which keys are valid (the web of trust), radix-64 armour, vulnerabilities, and the \"Beware of Snake Oil\" warning about untested cryptography. It also carries the 1991 essay \"Why Do You Need PGP?\" in its original form. Legal and export-control material is in Volume II.","citation":"Philip R. Zimmermann, \"PGP User’s Guide, Volume I: Essential Topics,\" revised 11 October 1994, distributed with PGP Version 2.6.2 by the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.","provenance":"Raw text (pgpdoc1.txt) extracted from the official PGP 2.6.2 documentation package. The file pgp262dc.zip was fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 from the ftp.acc.umu.se mirror of ftp.sunet.se (/pub/security/pgp/doc/pgp262dc.zip); it contains pgp262di.zip, from which pgpdoc1.txt was extracted unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that extracted pgpdoc1.txt. Text verified against the document’s own internal version line (\"PGP Version 2.6.2 - 11 Oct 94\").","hash":"4bf5c6ebac6eaff481efc3b4a46f9dcea52358dd2b62f2f67000719865f42d61","tags":["pgp","encryption","cryptography","crypto-wars","export-controls","documentation","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Pretty Good Privacy (PGP) — public key cryptography for the masses.","files":[{"path":"pgp-users-guide-volume-i.html","size":89862,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"pgpdoc1.txt","size":85737,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-304.002","local":["content/B59-304.002/pgp-users-guide-volume-i.html"]},{"id":"B59-304.003","title":"Testimony of Philip R. Zimmermann to the US Senate Subcommittee on Science, Technology, and Space","author":"Philip R. Zimmermann","date":"1996-06-26","type":"writing","description":"Zimmermann’s statement to the Senate Subcommittee on Science, Technology, and Space, delivered 26 June 1996 in support of S.1726, the Promotion of Commerce On-Line in the Digital Era (Pro-CODE) Act, which would have relaxed export controls on cryptographic software. Delivered five months after the government closed its three-year criminal investigation of him without charges. He argues that export controls harmed US industry and citizens without stopping the spread of strong cryptography, and that a surveillance-optimised infrastructure, once entrenched, invites later abuse.","citation":"Philip R. Zimmermann, testimony before the Subcommittee on Science, Technology, and Space of the US Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation, 26 June 1996. US Government public record.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 from the author’s site (philzimmermann.com/EN/essays/Testimony.html), which reproduces the statement he submitted to the subcommittee; archived unmodified alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. The testimony is a US Government public record; the document’s own dateline reads \"26 June 1996\".","hash":"840fa95a4eebfab959103fa70b5255ce87ad6d0a8cb722ffe73270ffcdb10139","tags":["pgp","encryption","export-controls","crypto-wars","free-speech","senate-testimony","primary-source"],"excerpt":"If we do nothing, new technologies will give the government new automatic surveillance capabilities that Stalin could never have dreamed of.","files":[{"path":"zimmermann-senate-testimony-1996.html","size":19309,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"zimmermann-senate-testimony-1996-source.html","size":16129,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-304.003","local":["content/B59-304.003/zimmermann-senate-testimony-1996.html"]},{"id":"B59-304.004","title":"PGP Marks 10th Anniversary","author":"Philip R. Zimmermann","date":"2001-06-05","type":"writing","description":"Zimmermann’s first-person account, written on the tenth anniversary, of how PGP 1.0 was released in June 1991: sent to Allan Hoeltje for upload to PeaceNet and to Kelly Goen for posting to Usenet and BBSs, marked \"US only\" in the mistaken belief that the tag controlled propagation. He recounts the five missed mortgage payments, the last-minute discovery of the competing PEM standard, the volunteer engineering team that produced PGP 2.0 in September 1992, the criminal investigation, and the move to MIT. The closest thing to a primary release timeline in his own words.","citation":"Philip R. Zimmermann, \"PGP Marks 10th Anniversary,\" 5 June 2001. Published at philzimmermann.com/EN/essays/PGP_10thAnniversary.html.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 from the author’s site (philzimmermann.com/EN/essays/PGP_10thAnniversary.html); archived unmodified alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot.","hash":"926b27a5bf80a9bbf4b109ee1c1b7fd6ea6627342be64e67829e75cecf1fe68b","tags":["pgp","encryption","crypto-wars","cypherpunks","history","primary-source"],"excerpt":"It was on this day in 1991 that I sent the first release of PGP to a couple of my friends for uploading to the Internet.","files":[{"path":"pgp-marks-10th-anniversary.html","size":8153,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"pgp-marks-10th-anniversary-source.html","size":5542,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-304.004","local":["content/B59-304.004/pgp-marks-10th-anniversary.html"]},{"id":"B59-304.005","title":"Hal Finney Home Page","author":"Hal Finney","date":"2004-01-01","type":"website","description":"Archived personal homepage of Hal Finney (1956-2014), cryptographer, original PGP 2.0 programmer, creator of RPOW (Reusable Proofs of Work), and the first person to receive a Bitcoin transaction from Satoshi Nakamoto. The page documents his work at PGP Corporation, the RPOW.NET server, the 1995 SSL Challenge, and links to his essays on digital cash, anonymous remailers, steganography, and politics. Finney graduated from Caltech in 1979 with a BS in Engineering.","citation":"Finney, H. (c. 2004). Personal homepage. Originally published at finney.org/~hal/home.html. Archived via fennetic.net/Wayback Machine, captured 24 June 2013.","provenance":"Retrieved from fennetic.net mirror of finney.org via the Wayback Machine, capture dated 24 June 2013. Hal Finney maintained this page from approximately the early 2000s through his ALS diagnosis. Content verified against known biographical details and Finney's published works.","hash":"2e39bf3209bba3f0284441dbbf679fd989d667a833a32df80bdc85ec62227e04","tags":["hal-finney","pgp","rpow","cypherpunks","homepage","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"hal-finney-home-page.html","size":8090,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"hal-finney-home-page.mht","size":20184,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-304.005","local":["content/B59-304.005/hal-finney-home-page.html"]},{"id":"B59-305.001","title":"Freedom of Speech in Software","author":"John Gilmore","date":"1998-10-14","type":"writing","description":"Speech delivered by John Gilmore at ApacheCon on October 14, 1998, arguing that software code is protected expression under the First Amendment. Gilmore traces the history of free speech challenges in software: Phil Salin's July 1991 paper (the first to apply free speech concepts to software), software patent fights, cryptographic export controls, the Bernstein case, PGP, and the EFF's publication of the DES-cracking source code in book form. Warns that if courts allow the government to regulate 'functional' software, Congress could ban any kind of software at will.","citation":"Gilmore, J. (1998, October 14). \"Freedom of Speech in Software.\" Speech delivered at ApacheCon. Published at toad.com/freedom.speech.software.","provenance":"Primary text retrieved from toad.com. MHT archive and plain text version both preserved. Content verified against known accounts of Gilmore's ApacheCon appearance and the legal cases referenced (Bernstein v. Department of State, Phil Zimmermann investigation). Gilmore credits Phil Salin as the originator of the software-as-speech argument.","hash":"e9a8484c6388ccdf099387c2252ca6b283eabdcf64f5d64844d60a834699435e","tags":["john-gilmore","free-speech","software-rights","phil-salin","crypto-wars","bernstein","eff","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"freedom-of-speech-in-software.html","size":28277,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"freedom-of-speech-in-software.mht","size":69881,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-305.001","local":["content/B59-305.001/freedom-of-speech-in-software.html"]},{"id":"B59-305.002","title":"Bernstein v. United States (EFF case archive)","author":"Electronic Frontier Foundation","date":"1999-05-06","type":"reference","description":"EFF's case archive for Bernstein v. United States, the lawsuit that established in the Ninth Circuit that source code is expression protected by the First Amendment and that the Export Administration Regulations' prior-restraint licensing of cryptographic software was unconstitutional. Daniel J. Bernstein, then a Berkeley graduate student, was barred from publishing his \"Snuffle\" cipher; EFF assembled the legal team. Judge Marilyn Hall Patel's district rulings (1996-97) and the 1999 panel opinion were pivotal in ending the Crypto Wars and freeing the export of strong cryptography. This record preserves EFF's case summary and hosts the two decisive opinions in full as public-record court documents: Judge Marilyn Hall Patel's 25 August 1997 district-court opinion holding the Export Administration Regulations an unconstitutional prior restraint, and the 6 May 1999 Ninth Circuit panel opinion affirming that source code is speech. See also B59-301.002 and B59-301.003 (EFF's Cracking DES and the DES Cracker FAQ) for the companion hardware side of the same Crypto Wars campaign.","citation":"Bernstein v. U.S. Department of Justice, 176 F.3d 1132 (9th Cir. 1999); Bernstein v. U.S. Department of State, 922 F. Supp. 1426 (N.D. Cal. 1996). EFF case archive.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.eff.org/cases/bernstein-v-us-dept-justice. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published by EFF under CC BY. SHA-256 is over the eff.org case-page snapshot. Two additional files are hosted in the record folder: the Ninth Circuit's 6 May 1999 opinion and Judge Patel's 25 August 1997 opinion, both fetched from eff.org/files/filenode/bernstein/ and both US federal judicial opinions in the public domain (no copyright). Crypto Wars context: the EFF's \"Cracking DES\" book and the DES Cracker FAQ are hosted by the parallel PGP/Gilmore task as B59-301.002 and B59-301.003 (confirmed via cross-session coordination); this record does not duplicate them.","hash":"8fd702f161d9b2c39cd9f1f80d0b24c3a5bb05646a2e884a6f36469a62463dc7","tags":["eff","bernstein","crypto-wars","free-speech","first-amendment","code-is-speech","export-controls","courts","1999","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"This case is about the right to publish and exchange ideas, including scientific ideas ... The government's efforts to regulate and control the spread of knowledge relating to encryption may implicate more than the First Amendment rights of cryptographers.\" - 9th Circuit panel, 1999.","files":[{"path":"bernstein-v-united-states.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":4018},{"path":"bernstein-v-united-states-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":125370},{"path":"opinion-9th-circuit-1999-05-06.html","label":"Ninth Circuit Opinion, 6 May 1999 (public record)","size":80731},{"path":"opinion-patel-district-court-1997-08-25.html","label":"Judge Patel District Court Opinion, 25 Aug 1997 (public record)","size":90770}],"content_path":"content/B59-305.002","local":["content/B59-305.002/bernstein-v-united-states.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.001","title":"Crime and Puzzlement","author":"John Perry Barlow","date":"1990-06-08","type":"writing","description":"Barlow's June 1990 account of the Secret Service's Operation Sun Devil, the raid on Steve Jackson Games, the investigation that swept up hacker \"Acid Phreak\" and others, and Barlow's own visit from an FBI agent over the purloined Apple source code. It sets out the reasoning that led Barlow and Mitch Kapor to found the Electronic Frontier Foundation weeks later: that constitutional protections for speech, press, assembly and property were being quietly rewritten for digital media by law-enforcement agencies acting without adequate legal maps. One of the founding documents of the digital civil-liberties movement.","citation":"John Perry Barlow, \"Crime and Puzzlement,\" June 1990. Electronic Frontier Foundation.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.eff.org/pages/crime-and-puzzlement. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published by EFF under CC BY. First published in Whole Earth Review (Fall 1990); EFF hosts the text on eff.org under its site-wide CC BY licence, under which it is archived here.","hash":"f5c277925ea580806cdaaeeed9e452fb2b5932a4867ea41582e7acc7891d5ece","tags":["john-perry-barlow","eff","civil-liberties","free-speech","operation-sun-devil","1990","primary-source","cyberspace"],"excerpt":"In its present condition, Cyberspace is a frontier region, populated by the few hardy technologists who can tolerate the austerity of its savage computer interfaces, incompatible communications protocols, proprietary barricades, cultural and legal ambiguities, and general lack of useful maps or metaphors.","files":[{"path":"crime-and-puzzlement.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":65820},{"path":"crime-and-puzzlement-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":97538}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.001","local":["content/B59-306.001/crime-and-puzzlement.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.002","title":"Across the Electronic Frontier","author":"Mitchell Kapor & John Perry Barlow","date":"1990-07-10","type":"writing","description":"The founding statement of the Electronic Frontier Foundation, issued by Mitchell Kapor and John Perry Barlow in Washington, D.C. on 10 July 1990. It frames cyberspace as a frontier where constitutional questions about speech, press, property and identity are unresolved, warns that law-enforcement excesses such as Operation Sun Devil are setting precedents that would narrow constitutional protection for digital media, and commits the new organization to legal intervention, public and policymaker education, and the development of tools giving non-technical users full access to networked communication. This is the document the task brief refers to as the EFF founding announcement / \"a framework for a new technology of freedom.\"","citation":"Mitchell Kapor and John Perry Barlow, \"Across the Electronic Frontier,\" Electronic Frontier Foundation, Washington, D.C., 10 July 1990.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.eff.org/pages/across-electronic-frontier. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published by EFF under CC BY.","hash":"fb490c420aa294ec37d3436d7374b28e44e5a77d1dc1deb91650918c89fc57fc","tags":["eff","civil-liberties","free-speech","cyberspace","mitch-kapor","john-perry-barlow","1990","primary-source","founding-document"],"excerpt":"We have concluded that a cure can lie only in bringing civilization to Cyberspace. Unless a successful effort is made to render that harsh and mysterious terrain suitable for ordinary inhabitants, friction between the two worlds will worsen.","files":[{"path":"across-the-electronic-frontier.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":11475},{"path":"across-the-electronic-frontier-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":40835}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.002","local":["content/B59-306.002/across-the-electronic-frontier.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.003","title":"A Not Terribly Brief History of the Electronic Frontier Foundation","author":"John Perry Barlow","date":"1990-11-08","type":"writing","description":"Barlow's own narrative history of how the Electronic Frontier Foundation came to exist: the 1990 hacker raids, the WELL conversations with Mitch Kapor and John Gilmore, the visit from the FBI, the decision to fund a legal defense for Steve Jackson Games and the Phrack editors, and the founding of the organization in July 1990. A primary-source account by a co-founder of the events that established digital civil liberties as an organized cause.","citation":"John Perry Barlow, \"A Not Terribly Brief History of the Electronic Frontier Foundation,\" November 1990. Electronic Frontier Foundation.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.eff.org/pages/not-terribly-brief-history-electronic-frontier-foundation. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published by EFF under CC BY.","hash":"0fd9c9023c24ce6b5306503665111cda32797afa6ec0774b5157671ab741e2f6","tags":["john-perry-barlow","eff","civil-liberties","history","1990","primary-source","john-gilmore","mitch-kapor"],"excerpt":"We were, and are, determined to see that the Constitution of the United States, that ringing 200-year-old Enlightenment declaration of human rights and limited government, would survive the transition into Cyberspace intact.","files":[{"path":"not-terribly-brief-history-of-eff.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":32549},{"path":"not-terribly-brief-history-of-eff-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":62896}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.003","local":["content/B59-306.003/not-terribly-brief-history-of-eff.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.004","title":"Privacy, Technology, and the Open Society","author":"John Gilmore","date":"1991-03-28","type":"writing","description":"Speech delivered by John Gilmore at the First Conference on Computers, Freedom, and Privacy on March 28, 1991. Gilmore argues that the free and open society is being 'nibbled to death by ducks' through small, unheralded changes: the erosion of cash transactions, mandatory identification, mass incarceration, and the danger of census and phone records being used for persecution. He draws parallels to Nazi use of European phone records and Japanese-American internment via census data. Explicitly placed in the public domain by the author. One of the earliest major public speeches connecting cryptographic privacy tools to the defense of civil liberties.","citation":"Gilmore, J. (1991, March 28). \"Privacy, Technology, and the Open Society.\" Speech at the First Conference on Computers, Freedom, and Privacy. Public domain. Originally published at toad.com/gnu/cfp.talk.txt.","provenance":"Original text retrieved from toad.com via Internet Archive Wayback Machine (toad.com currently offline). The speech is explicitly marked as public domain by the author. Delivered at CFP 1991, the first major conference on digital rights, attended by approximately 400 people.","hash":"","tags":["john-gilmore","privacy","open-society","cfp-conference","civil-liberties","speech","public-domain","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"http://web.archive.org/web/20260417235321/http://www.toad.com/gnu/cfp.talk.txt"},{"id":"B59-306.005","title":"Selling Wine Without Bottles: The Economy of Mind on the Global Net","author":"John Perry Barlow","date":"1993-12-01","type":"writing","description":"The EFF's present-day canonical hosting of Barlow's 1993-94 essay on intellectual property in a networked world, under the subtitle \"The Economy of Mind on the Global Net.\" Same core argument as the WIRED version (B59-306.007): that once expression is separated from physical objects, the bottle is worthless without the wine, and law built around copies cannot hold. Kept as a distinct record because EFF's page differs in lead, framing and editing from the WIRED print text. Companion to B59-306.007.","citation":"John Perry Barlow, \"Selling Wine Without Bottles: The Economy of Mind on the Global Net,\" 1993-1994.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot of \"Selling Wine Without Bottles: The Economy of Mind on the Global Net\" (Barlow's own title for the essay published in WIRED 1994 as \"The Economy of Ideas,\" B59-306.007). Retrieved 2026-08-31 with curl from the EFF's current hosting (eff.org/pages/selling-wine-without-bottles-economy-mind-global-net); archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the source snapshot. Per the final copyright policy (2026-08-31) this essay is preserved by hosting rather than by link. Kept as a distinct record from B59-306.007 because the EFF text differs in lead, framing and editing from the WIRED print version.","hash":"09b68d2c75ab4b85df85fb5cd9991be0929803546f9a144dabd1e1bdf0e24458","tags":["john-perry-barlow","eff","intellectual-property","information-economy","1993","1994","civil-liberties"],"excerpt":"The greatest constraint on your future liberties may come not from government but from corporate legal departments laboring to protect by force what can no longer be protected by practical efficiency or general social consent.","files":[{"path":"selling-wine-without-bottles.html","size":64174,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"selling-wine-without-bottles-source.html","size":92052,"label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.005","local":["content/B59-306.005/selling-wine-without-bottles.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.006","title":"\"The Net interprets censorship as damage and routes around it\"","author":"John Gilmore","date":"1993-12-06","type":"writing","description":"The most quoted sentence of the cypherpunk era, and John Gilmore’s own account of what it meant. It first appeared in print in Philip Elmer-DeWitt’s TIME article \"First Nation in Cyberspace\" on 6 December 1993, and was reprinted in the New York Times (1996), Scientific American (2000) and the Communications of the ACM. This record preserves the explanatory note from Gilmore’s toad.com home page: the line originally described how Usenet software routed articles around a node that dropped them, and how IP routing recovers from a censored or damaged node, and how the meaning later broadened to people replicating information that is threatened with destruction.","citation":"John Gilmore, quoted in Philip Elmer-DeWitt, \"First Nation in Cyberspace,\" TIME, 6 December 1993. Author’s annotation from his home page, toad.com/gnu/.","provenance":"toad.com is currently offline (connection refused, 2026-08-31). The annotation is preserved from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine capture of http://www.toad.com/gnu/ dated 17 January 2021 (raw \"id_\" capture), fetched with curl on 2026-08-31. SHA-256 is computed over that archived snapshot. No single originating cypherpunks-list post for the phrase has been identified; Gilmore has written that he never located his own first use of it, and Howard Rheingold’s \"The Virtual Community\" (1993) already attributed it to him. The TIME article itself is under copyright and is not reproduced here beyond the single quoted sentence.","hash":"8901e7c0ad7dbb89a13d43c74980aede384f770238c557c7f807a0aff4f00b05","tags":["free-speech","cypherpunks","des","censorship","john-gilmore","internet-history","primary-source"],"excerpt":"The Net interprets censorship as damage and routes around it.","files":[{"path":"net-interprets-censorship-as-damage.html","size":5289,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"toad-com-gnu-2021-source.html","size":68598,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.006","local":["content/B59-306.006/net-interprets-censorship-as-damage.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.007","title":"The Economy of Ideas","author":"John Perry Barlow","date":"1994-03-01","type":"writing","description":"Barlow's essay on why intellectual property law breaks down when information is detached from physical carriers, published in WIRED 2.03 (March 1994) under the title \"The Economy of Ideas\" and circulated in Barlow's own drafts as \"Selling Wine Without Bottles.\" It argues that digitized expression is closer to a performed activity or a life form than to a manufactured good, that value in an information economy attaches to relationship, immediacy, performance and attention rather than to copies, and that encryption rather than copyright will do the practical work of protecting creators. Companion to B59-306.005, which is the same essay under Barlow's own title \"Selling Wine Without Bottles.\"","citation":"John Perry Barlow, \"The Economy of Ideas,\" WIRED 2.03, March 1994.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot of \"The Economy of Ideas\" (WIRED 2.03, March 1994). Retrieved 2026-08-31 with curl from a raw Wayback capture of Barlow's former EFF home directory (web.archive.org/web/20050212134358id_/http://homes.eff.org:80/~barlow/EconomyOfIdeas.html); archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over the source snapshot. Per the final copyright policy (2026-08-31) web journalism is preserved by hosting rather than by link, because \"links go dead fast and articles get edited.\" Internet Archive was otherwise unreachable during the work window, so an independent second-source check could not be completed.","hash":"819c244fdac0a1fe09e864af2a98433fcb50977cab83933328a008f15e2cf02a","tags":["john-perry-barlow","eff","intellectual-property","information-economy","wired","1994","civil-liberties"],"excerpt":"Intellectual property law cannot be patched, retrofitted, or expanded to contain the gases of digitized expression any more than real estate law might be revised to cover the allocation of broadcasting spectrum.","files":[{"path":"the-economy-of-ideas.html","size":60251,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"the-economy-of-ideas-source.html","size":61693,"label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.007","local":["content/B59-306.007/the-economy-of-ideas.html"]},{"id":"B59-306.008","title":"A Declaration of the Independence of Cyberspace","author":"John Perry Barlow","date":"1996-02-08","type":"writing","description":"Written by John Perry Barlow in Davos on 8 February 1996 in direct response to the passage of the US Telecommunications Act of 1996 and its Communications Decency Act provisions. The Declaration asserts that cyberspace is a self-governing social space beyond the legitimate authority of nation states, that its order arises from ethics and enlightened self-interest rather than physical coercion, and that industrial-era concepts of property, expression, identity and movement do not apply to a world made of thought. It became the most widely reproduced single text of the early net-liberties movement.","citation":"John Perry Barlow, \"A Declaration of the Independence of Cyberspace,\" Davos, Switzerland, 8 February 1996. Electronic Frontier Foundation.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://www.eff.org/cyberspace-independence. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. EFF site content is published by EFF under CC BY.","hash":"a9c832f7600acf88a673ace41f6e5505209a361a1fedd57d0301ec1bc8d392fe","tags":["john-perry-barlow","eff","civil-liberties","free-speech","cyberspace","1996","primary-source","manifesto"],"excerpt":"Cyberspace does not lie within your borders. Do not think that you can build it, as though it were a public construction project. You cannot. It is an act of nature and it grows itself through our collective actions.","files":[{"path":"a-declaration-of-the-independence-of-cyberspace.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":7789},{"path":"a-declaration-of-the-independence-of-cyberspace-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":37134}],"content_path":"content/B59-306.008","local":["content/B59-306.008/a-declaration-of-the-independence-of-cyberspace.html"]},{"id":"B59-400.001","title":"Bitcoin P2P e-cash paper (first announcement)","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2008-10-31","type":"writing","description":"Satoshi Nakamoto's first public announcement of Bitcoin, posted to the Cryptography Mailing List (cryptography@metzdowd.com) on 31 October 2008 — the same day the whitepaper was published. Links to bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf, lists the main properties (double-spending prevented by a peer-to-peer network, no mint or trusted parties, participants can be anonymous, new coins from Hashcash-style proof-of-work) and pastes the abstract inline. The thread it opened is the first recorded technical review of the design.","citation":"Satoshi Nakamoto, \"Bitcoin P2P e-cash paper,\" Cryptography Mailing List, 31 October 2008.","provenance":"Extracted verbatim, with its mailing-list envelope and footer intact, from the metzdowd Cryptography Mailing List pipermail monthly archive (2008-October.txt.gz), fetched with curl on 2026-08-31. A transcription from the Nakamoto Institute’s \"The Complete Satoshi\" is archived alongside as a cross-check. SHA-256 is computed over the extracted message bytes. Public list post; hosted in full.","hash":"ec1e03185633abb65c919fa831abd9c73777d974017ec097de8fa80ef2328816","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","primary-source","metzdowd","cryptography-mailing-list","announcement","2008","proof-of-work"],"excerpt":"I've been working on a new electronic cash system that's fully peer-to-peer, with no trusted third party.","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-p2p-ecash-paper.html","size":6064,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"metzdowd-cryptography-2008-10-31-satoshi.txt","size":2136,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"},{"path":"nakamotoinstitute-the-complete-satoshi-email-1.html","size":63287,"label":"Nakamoto Institute transcription (snapshot)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.001","local":["content/B59-400.001/bitcoin-p2p-ecash-paper.html"],"see_also":["B59-000","B59-400.002","B59-400.005"]},{"id":"B59-400.002","title":"Satoshi Nakamoto ↔ James A. Donald: \"Bitcoin P2P e-cash paper\" thread","author":"James A. Donald & Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2008-11-03","type":"writing","description":"The thread on the cryptography@metzdowd.com mailing list that began with Satoshi Nakamoto’s announcement of the Bitcoin white paper (31 October 2008) and in which James A. Donald <jamesd@echeque.com> became Satoshi’s most persistent early interlocutor. Donald’s first reply: \"We very, very much need such a system, but the way I understand your proposal, it does not seem to scale to the required size.\" The exchange runs through early November on double-spending detection, bandwidth, network broadcast semantics, timestamping and incentives, with Hal Finney, Ray Dillinger, John Levine and others joining. Preserved as the full monthly list archives for October and November 2008.","citation":"\"Bitcoin P2P e-cash paper,\" cryptography mailing list (cryptography@metzdowd.com), 31 October – 17 November 2008. Archives: https://www.metzdowd.com/pipermail/cryptography/2008-October.txt.gz and 2008-November.txt.gz","provenance":"Retrieved with curl as the gzip-compressed Mailman monthly archives from https://www.metzdowd.com/pipermail/cryptography/2008-October.txt.gz (HTTP 200) and 2008-November.txt.gz (HTTP 200), then decompressed with gunzip (deterministic). The metzdowd.com Pipermail archive is the primary archive of the list on which Satoshi first announced Bitcoin; nakamotoinstitute.org’s \"Complete Satoshi\" is a curated rendering of the same messages. Stored as decompressed plain text. SHA-256 is computed over the decompressed November archive (which contains the bulk of the Satoshi–Donald exchange); the October archive, containing Satoshi’s 31 October announcement and the first replies, is included as a second primary-source file. Per-file SHA-256 in the manifest.","hash":"3c0d913db5fc1a40f436ed086e6db84905b1eed104bbfed7254e1f24b2331d4e","tags":["satoshi-correspondence","james-donald","bitcoin-precursor","proof-of-work","cypherpunks","metzdowd","primary-source"],"excerpt":"We very, very much need such a system, but the way I understand your proposal, it does not seem to scale to the required size.","files":[{"path":"satoshi-james-donald-thread.html","size":5196,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"metzdowd-cryptography-2008-october.txt","size":125641,"label":"cryptography@metzdowd.com — October 2008 archive (Primary Source)"},{"path":"metzdowd-cryptography-2008-november.txt","size":175264,"label":"cryptography@metzdowd.com — November 2008 archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.002","local":["content/B59-400.002/satoshi-james-donald-thread.html"]},{"id":"B59-400.003","title":"The Bitcoin Genesis Block","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2009-01-03","type":"code","description":"Block 0 of the Bitcoin blockchain, mined by Satoshi Nakamoto with timestamp 3 January 2009 18:15:05 UTC. Its coinbase input embeds the text “The Times 03/Jan/2009 Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks” — the front-page headline of The Times of London that day — serving as both a timestamp and a comment on the bank bailouts. The 50 BTC coinbase reward is permanently unspendable. This record assembles the full block header, the coinbase transaction, the 285-byte raw serialization and the newspaper reference, cross-checked across two block explorers and Bitcoin Core.","citation":"Bitcoin block 0, hash 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f, timestamp 2009-01-03 18:15:05 UTC. Coinbase text from The Times (London), 3 January 2009, p. 1.","provenance":"Assembled 2026-08-31 from the Blockstream Esplora API (block, coinbase transaction, and raw 285-byte serialization), cross-checked field-by-field against the Blockchain.com rawblock API and the genesis constants in Bitcoin Core src/kernel/chainparams.cpp; all sources agree. The three raw API responses are archived alongside the assembled genesis-block.json. SHA-256 is computed over genesis-block.json. Consensus data / public domain.","hash":"36e93f6c4555af655f3683d9fb3dfcad579338c037087c884d9696495bce1d85","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","genesis-block","primary-source","blockchain","the-times","2009","coinbase","proof-of-work"],"excerpt":"The Times 03/Jan/2009 Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-genesis-block.html","size":7189,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"genesis-block.json","size":3066,"label":"Assembled Record (hashed snapshot)"},{"path":"genesis-block-raw.hex","size":571,"label":"Raw block, 285 bytes (hex)"},{"path":"blockstream-genesis-block.json","size":340,"label":"Blockstream API — block"},{"path":"blockstream-genesis-coinbase-tx.json","size":1212,"label":"Blockstream API — coinbase tx"},{"path":"blockchaininfo-genesis-rawblock.json","size":1416,"label":"Blockchain.com API — rawblock (cross-check)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.003","local":["content/B59-400.003/bitcoin-genesis-block.html","content/B59-400.003/genesis-block.json"],"see_also":["B59-000","B59-400.004"]},{"id":"B59-400.004","title":"Bitcoin v0.1 released","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2009-01-08","type":"writing","description":"Satoshi Nakamoto's announcement of the first Bitcoin release, posted to the Cryptography Mailing List on 8 January 2009 (the message's +0800 header also reads as 9 January), five days after the genesis block. Gives install instructions for the Windows binary on SourceForge with C++ source included, describes IP-to-IP and Bitcoin-address payments, 120-block coin maturity, port 8333, and the first public statement that total circulation will be 21,000,000 coins, halved every four years.","citation":"Satoshi Nakamoto, \"Bitcoin v0.1 released,\" Cryptography Mailing List, 8 January 2009.","provenance":"Extracted verbatim, with its mailing-list envelope and footer intact, from the metzdowd Cryptography Mailing List pipermail monthly archive (2009-January.txt.gz), fetched with curl on 2026-08-31. A transcription from the Nakamoto Institute’s \"The Complete Satoshi\" is archived alongside as a cross-check. SHA-256 is computed over the extracted message bytes. Public list post; hosted in full.","hash":"2678f7f551289b60d914ff582fe7d438f91b11fb612afa08662476d2fac3aad2","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","primary-source","metzdowd","cryptography-mailing-list","release","2009","21-million"],"excerpt":"Total circulation will be 21,000,000 coins. It’ll be distributed to network nodes when they make blocks, with the amount cut in half every 4 years.","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-v0.1-released.html","size":7027,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"metzdowd-cryptography-2009-01-08-satoshi.txt","size":2952,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"},{"path":"nakamotoinstitute-the-complete-satoshi-email-16.html","size":65483,"label":"Nakamoto Institute transcription (snapshot)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.004","local":["content/B59-400.004/bitcoin-v0.1-released.html"],"see_also":["B59-000","B59-400.003"]},{"id":"B59-400.005","title":"Wei Dai ↔ Satoshi Nakamoto emails (2008–2009)","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto & Wei Dai","date":"2009-01-10","type":"writing","description":"The three surviving emails between Satoshi Nakamoto and Wei Dai. 22 August 2008: Satoshi, still pre-\"Bitcoin\", writes asking for the publication year of b-money for a citation, says \"I’m getting ready to release a paper that expands on your ideas into a complete working system,\" and notes \"Adam Back (hashcash.org) noticed the similarities and pointed me to your site.\" The draft is titled \"Electronic Cash Without a Trusted Third Party.\" Wei Dai replies with the 1998 cypherpunks announcement link. 10 January 2009: Satoshi writes again — \"I just released the full implementation... Bitcoin v0.1. I think it achieves nearly all the goals you set out to solve in your b-money paper.\" Wei Dai later cited these emails as evidence that Satoshi is not Nick Szabo.","citation":"Emails between Satoshi Nakamoto and Wei Dai, 22 August 2008 – 10 January 2009. Provided publicly by Wei Dai; quoted in The Sunday Times, 2 March 2014. Archived collection: gwern.net/doc/bitcoin/2008-nakamoto.","provenance":"The task specified nakamotoinstitute.org as the source, but the Satoshi–Wei Dai correspondence is not (and per the site’s open-source repository history never has been) published there — nakamotoinstitute.org hosts only the public cryptography, bitcoin-list and p2p-research mailing lists. The earliest public disclosure of these private emails is Wei Dai’s (quoted in The Sunday Times, 2 March 2014, and discussed by Wei Dai on LessWrong). This record therefore uses gwern.net’s long-standing annotated collection \"Wei Dai / Satoshi Nakamoto 2009 Bitcoin emails\" (gwern.net/doc/bitcoin/2008-nakamoto), retrieved as Markdown with curl (HTTP 200, 20,483 bytes). The collection reproduces the full text of all three emails; it also embeds a short fair-use quotation from The Sunday Times and Gwern’s editorial notes. A second file archives the BitcoinTalk thread (18 April 2013) in which Adam Back describes getting the b-money reference added to Satoshi’s paper. SHA-256 is computed over the archived Markdown. Cross-checked: the email text matches the b-money citation history and Adam Back’s independent account. FLAG for Kat: confirm gwern.net is acceptable as the archival source, or supply the nakamotoinstitute.org / bitcointalk originals.","hash":"a69b819775428c39f9e14cfcea08eee322dfb21dcd90154392629114ca6eae6c","tags":["satoshi-correspondence","wei-dai","b-money","bitcoin-precursor","adam-back","hashcash","primary-source"],"excerpt":"I’m getting ready to release a paper that expands on your ideas into a complete working system. Adam Back (hashcash.org) noticed the similarities and pointed me to your site.","files":[{"path":"wei-dai-satoshi-emails.html","size":19936,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"wei-dai-satoshi-emails-gwern.md","size":20483,"label":"Archived Email Collection (Primary Source)"},{"path":"adam-back-bitcointalk-2013.html","size":292416,"label":"Adam Back BitcoinTalk self-introduction, 18 Apr 2013 (context)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.005","local":["content/B59-400.005/wei-dai-satoshi-emails.html"]},{"id":"B59-400.006","title":"Bitcoin open source implementation of P2P currency","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto","date":"2009-02-11","type":"writing","description":"Satoshi Nakamoto's second public announcement of Bitcoin, posted to the P2P Foundation's Ning forum on 11 February 2009 for a general audience. States the project's motivation in plain language: “The root problem with conventional currency is all the trust that's required to make it work.” Draws the analogy to strong encryption removing the need to trust a system administrator, and now doing the same for money. His words only, reproduced in full — the clearest short statement of design rationale Satoshi wrote.","citation":"Satoshi Nakamoto, \"Bitcoin open source implementation of P2P currency,\" P2P Foundation forum, 11 February 2009.","provenance":"Original host p2pfoundation.ning.com is defunct. Fetched with curl on 2026-08-31 from the Nakamoto Institute’s \"The Complete Satoshi\" transcription (nakamotoinstitute.org/posts/p2pfoundation/1/), archived unmodified as the source snapshot. SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot. Only Satoshi’s own post text is reproduced on the reader page (public forum post; no third-party replies included).","hash":"e02a3fdbaf40b57f72672b33babf8994328d9aedc9e94fb987d5e1d985774a2b","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","primary-source","p2p-foundation","design-rationale","trust","2009"],"excerpt":"With e-currency based on cryptographic proof, without the need to trust a third party middleman, money can be secure and transactions effortless.","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-p2p-currency-p2pfoundation.html","size":7049,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"nakamotoinstitute-p2pfoundation-post-1.html","size":65805,"label":"Original Archive (Nakamoto Institute snapshot)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.006","local":["content/B59-400.006/bitcoin-p2p-currency-p2pfoundation.html"],"see_also":["B59-000","B59-302.001","B59-302.004"]},{"id":"B59-400.007","title":"Satoshi Nakamoto ↔ Martti Malmi (“Sirius”): the correspondence","author":"Satoshi Nakamoto & Martti Malmi","date":"2009-05-02","type":"writing","description":"The private email correspondence between Satoshi Nakamoto and Martti Malmi (“Sirius”), Bitcoin’s second developer, who ran the early website and forum. Malmi kept the emails private for fifteen years and published them in 2024 as witness evidence in the UK trial COPA v. Wright. This snapshot of his published page holds 260 messages, Sat, 02 May 2009 18:06:58 +0100 to Tue, 22 Feb 2011 19:49:19 +0000, covering website copy, the forum, exchange listings, press, the economics arguments Satoshi did and didn’t want made in public, and day-to-day protocol decisions. The archive is incomplete — only Malmi’s @cc.hut.fi-era mail survives.","citation":"Emails between Satoshi Nakamoto and Martti Malmi, May 2009 – February 2011. Published by Martti Malmi at mmalmi.github.io/satoshi/ ; released for COPA v. Wright, 2024.","provenance":"Published page fetched with curl from https://mmalmi.github.io/satoshi/ on 2026-08-31 and archived unmodified as the source snapshot; a styled, readable reader page is derived from it. The mmalmi/satoshi source repository was not reachable (GitHub 404) at archiving time, so no cleaner upstream text file was available; if the repo returns, its raw markdown would be the preferred source. Malmi released the correspondence in 2024 as a witness in COPA v. Wright ([2024] EWHC 1198 (Ch)); he states the set is incomplete, limited to his @cc.hut.fi account. SHA-256 is computed over the archived page snapshot. Released publicly by the copyright holder (Malmi); Satoshi’s side is of disputed but effectively unenforceable authorship. Hosted in full.","hash":"feefc9377090a14740b8f76ac836b244ec753fce7a37b09cf97648bd87c3d4c0","tags":["bitcoin","satoshi-nakamoto","martti-malmi","sirius","primary-source","correspondence","copa-v-wright","2009","2010","early-development"],"excerpt":"Once it’s backed with cash, that might change, but I’d probably better refrain from mentioning that in public anymore until we’re closer to ready to start.","files":[{"path":"satoshi-malmi-correspondence.html","size":491194,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mmalmi-github-io-satoshi.html","size":558533,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-400.007","local":["content/B59-400.007/satoshi-malmi-correspondence.html"],"see_also":["B59-000","B59-400.005","B59-400.002"]},{"id":"B59-401.001","title":"How to Time-Stamp a Digital Document","author":"Stuart Haber & W. Scott Stornetta","date":"1991-01-01","type":"writing","description":"The founding paper of cryptographic timestamping. Haber and Stornetta (Bellcore) ask how to certify when a digital document was created or last changed, without trusting a timestamping service. Their answers: linking — hash each new document together with the previous certificate so the records form a chain no one can rewrite — and distributed trust via several independent witnesses chosen pseudorandomly from the document’s own hash. Bitcoin cites this paper (references [1] and [3] of the white paper trace directly to it) and its \"chain of hashes\" is the block chain’s direct ancestor.","citation":"Stuart Haber and W. Scott Stornetta, \"How to Time-Stamp a Digital Document,\" Journal of Cryptology, vol. 3, no. 2 (1991), pp. 99–111. DOI 10.1007/BF00196791.","provenance":"PDF (13 pages, 825,065 bytes) retrieved with curl from https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF00196791.pdf (HTTP 200, application/pdf). SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes. FLAG for Kat: this is a Springer / Journal of Cryptology publication (academic publisher). Under the \"do not host copyrighted commercially-published material\" rule relayed 31 Aug 2026, this record may need to be converted to an external-reference entry (citation + link, \"Copyrighted; linked, not archived\", no files[], no hash). Staged here as host+hash per the task’s \"host and hash everything, no link-only\" instruction and the existing B59-402.001 precedent (Dwork & Naor, CRYPTO ’92); left for Kat to reconcile.","hash":"ef2290c000332967f5753973597b0d92485cc618e19643a60308ce2606b606de","tags":["timestamping","bitcoin-precursor","hash-chains","haber-stornetta","proof-of-work","primary-source"],"excerpt":"We propose two solutions... Both of these schemes are ready to be used today.","files":[{"path":"how-to-time-stamp-a-digital-document.html","size":4088,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"haber-stornetta-1991-how-to-time-stamp.pdf","size":825065,"label":"Original Paper (PDF, Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-401.001","local":["content/B59-401.001/how-to-time-stamp-a-digital-document.html"]},{"id":"B59-401.002","title":"Improving the Efficiency and Reliability of Digital Time-Stamping","author":"Dave Bayer, Stuart Haber & W. Scott Stornetta","date":"1993-03-01","type":"writing","description":"The follow-up to the 1991 timestamping paper. Bayer, Haber and Stornetta replace the simple linked list with a tree: documents in each time interval are combined with a Merkle hash tree, so a single \"witness\" value fixes many documents at once and each document’s timestamp proof is only the O(log n) sibling hashes along its path to the root. It also proposes publishing the periodic root value widely (e.g. in a newspaper) so the record cannot be repudiated. Merkle-tree commitment plus a widely-witnessed root is exactly how Bitcoin blocks fix their transactions.","citation":"Dave Bayer, Stuart Haber and W. Scott Stornetta, \"Improving the Efficiency and Reliability of Digital Time-Stamping,\" in Sequences II: Methods in Communication, Security and Computer Science (Springer, 1993), pp. 329–334.","provenance":"PDF (6 pages, 69,493 bytes) retrieved with curl from https://www.math.columbia.edu/~bayer/papers/Timestamp_BHS93.pdf (HTTP 200, application/pdf) — co-author Dave Bayer’s faculty page at Columbia. SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes. FLAG for Kat: this paper appeared in a Springer volume (Sequences II). It is hosted here from an author’s own page (arguably author-self-published), and staged as host+hash per the task instruction and B59-402.001 precedent, but flagged alongside B59-401.001 for a consistent rights decision — keep as hosted author copy, or convert to external reference.","hash":"aa7d802afdda9e2ff27680632f53c3fcc0d27d49ff92c32aae8d942bae8c5f4b","tags":["timestamping","bitcoin-precursor","merkle-tree","hash-chains","haber-stornetta","bayer","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Merkle... trees... allow us to certify many documents with a single signature.","files":[{"path":"improving-digital-time-stamping.html","size":4217,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"haber-stornetta-1993-improving-timestamping.pdf","size":69493,"label":"Original Paper (PDF, Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-401.002","local":["content/B59-401.002/improving-digital-time-stamping.html"]},{"id":"B59-402.001","title":"Pricing via Processing, or Combatting Junk Mail","author":"Cynthia Dwork & Moni Naor","date":"1992-08-16","type":"writing","description":"The paper that invented proof-of-work. Presented at CRYPTO â€™92, Dwork and Naor propose deterring junk mail and resource abuse by requiring the sender to compute a moderately hard, but not intractable, function before a message is accepted â€” a \"pricing function.\" Honest users barely notice the cost; bulk abusers are priced out. Hashcash (1997), b-money, RPOW and Bitcoin mining all descend directly from this idea.","citation":"Cynthia Dwork and Moni Naor, \"Pricing via Processing, or Combatting Junk Mail,\" Advances in Cryptology â€” CRYPTO â€™92, LNCS 740, Springer, pp. 139â€“147.","provenance":"Republished from the CRYPTO â€™92 proceedings; author manuscripts hosted at the Weizmann Institute and Microsoft Research.","hash":"21da884fb7815ab0328ea9d78e5abeddf27abb32c9e0f5f87e9574f3064fe6b9","tags":["proof-of-work","cryptography","digital-cash","cynthia-dwork","moni-naor","spam","pricing-function","crypto-1992","primary-source","bitcoin-precursor"],"excerpt":"\"In order to send a message, the sender must compute some moderately expensive, but not intractable, function of the message and some additional information.\"","files":[],"content_path":"content/B59-402.001","local":["content/B59-402.001/pricing-via-processing.html"]},{"id":"B59-402.002","title":"[ANNOUNCE] hash cash postage implementation","author":"Adam Back","date":"1997-03-28","type":"writing","description":"Adam Back’s announcement of the first hashcash implementation, posted to the cypherpunks mailing list on 28 March 1997. Proposes a \"partial hash collision based postage scheme\": a stamp that is arbitrarily expensive to compute (by choosing the number of collision bits) yet instantly verifiable, to be attached to email and remailer traffic as an anti-spam / anti-abuse cost. Introduces the core proof-of-work construction, a double-spending database with validity periods, and use cases (per-post remailer postage, spam filtering, nym-server good-behaviour bonds). Eleven years later Satoshi cited hashcash as Bitcoin’s proof-of-work.","citation":"Adam Back, \"[ANNOUNCE] hash cash postage implementation,\" cypherpunks mailing list, 28 March 1997. Archived by the author at http://www.hashcash.org/papers/announce.txt","provenance":"Plain-text announcement retrieved with curl from the author’s own archive at hashcash.org (http://www.hashcash.org/papers/announce.txt; HTTP 200, Last-Modified 16 Jan 2005). This is Adam Back’s hosted copy of his 1997 cypherpunks-list post; the cypherpunks list archive at cpunks.org was not used because its TLS certificate is expired. SHA-256 is computed over the archived plain-text file.","hash":"22de78d5327355db8e9deb9a3c1c8f8021d938e1ad971dae8da0ff1a24be530e","tags":["proof-of-work","hashcash","bitcoin-precursor","adam-back","cypherpunks","anti-spam","primary-source"],"excerpt":"The idea of using partial hashes is that they can be made arbitrarily expensive to compute (by choosing the desired number of bits of collision), and yet can be verified instantly.","files":[{"path":"hashcash-announcement.html","size":11263,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"hashcash-announce-1997.txt","size":7657,"label":"Original Announcement (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-402.002","local":["content/B59-402.002/hashcash-announcement.html"]},{"id":"B59-402.003","title":"Hashcash — A Denial of Service Counter-Measure","author":"Adam Back","date":"2002-08-01","type":"writing","description":"Adam Back’s 2002 technical report giving hashcash its full written treatment, five years after the 1997 implementation. Defines the cost-function framework (interactive vs non-interactive, publicly auditable, unbounded vs bounded probabilistic), specifies the hashcash stamp format and the partial pre-image (leading-zero-bits) function, analyses efficiency and the effect of hardware speed-ups, and surveys applications: throttling connection depletion and other denial-of-service attacks, Hal Finney’s hashcash-based reusable proof-of-work idea, spam control, and Camram/SMTP integration. Directly cited by the Bitcoin white paper.","citation":"Adam Back, \"Hashcash — A Denial of Service Counter-Measure,\" technical report, 1 August 2002. http://www.hashcash.org/papers/hashcash.pdf","provenance":"PDF (10 pages, 84,537 bytes) retrieved with curl from the author’s archive at http://www.hashcash.org/papers/hashcash.pdf (HTTP 200, application/pdf). Hosted unmodified as the primary source alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes.","hash":"fb8b1a6a3f8cdf48b189e77c0355e0f2ffb0f9d6d51d7f1d8c2b29f61c0d33bc","tags":["proof-of-work","hashcash","bitcoin-precursor","adam-back","denial-of-service","anti-spam","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Hashcash is a denial of service counter measure tool. Its main current use is to help hashcash users avoid losing email due to content based and blacklist based anti-spam systems.","files":[{"path":"hashcash-paper.html","size":3825,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"hashcash-a-denial-of-service-counter-measure.pdf","size":84537,"label":"Original Paper (PDF, Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-402.003","local":["content/B59-402.003/hashcash-paper.html"]},{"id":"B59-402.004","title":"On Memory-Bound Functions for Fighting Spam","author":"Cynthia Dwork, Andrew Goldberg & Moni Naor","date":"2003-08-17","type":"writing","description":"The follow-up to Dwork and Naor’s 1992 proof-of-work paper (B59-402.001). The problem with CPU-bound puzzles is that fast hardware makes them cheap for exactly the bulk senders you want to deter. This paper builds a proof-of-effort function whose cost is dominated by cache misses, memory latency, which varies far less between a cheap device and a server, and proves a tight lower bound on the memory accesses a sender must make while the receiver verifies cheaply.","excerpt":"Memory access speeds vary across machines much less than do CPU speeds, [so] memory-bound functions may behave more equitably than CPU-bound functions.","citation":"Cynthia Dwork, Andrew Goldberg, and Moni Naor, \"On Memory-Bound Functions for Fighting Spam,\" in D. Boneh (ed.), Advances in Cryptology — CRYPTO 2003, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 2729, Springer, 2003, pp. 426-444.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved by curl from the IACR CRYPTO 2003 proceedings archive (iacr.org/archive/crypto2003/27290425/27290425.pdf) on 2026-08-31, archived unmodified. Cross-checked against the copy at hashcash.org/papers/memory-bound-crypto.pdf: same paper and text, different PDF build (162922 vs 180680 bytes), so the hashes differ; the IACR proceedings copy is treated as canonical here. Internet Archive was offline for a Wayback attempt. SHA-256 is computed over the archived IACR PDF bytes.","hash":"10758efa74b800a801c1b8957298262a1ac3ea9e08b32762a3b14021032b2aac","tags":["proof-of-work","memory-bound-functions","cynthia-dwork","moni-naor","andrew-goldberg","spam","bitcoin-precursor","primary-source","2003"],"files":[{"path":"on-memory-bound-functions-for-fighting-spam.html","size":4896,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"on-memory-bound-functions-for-fighting-spam-source.pdf","size":162922,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-402.004","local":["content/B59-402.004/on-memory-bound-functions-for-fighting-spam.html"]},{"id":"B59-402.005","title":"hashcash.org — site snapshot","author":"Adam Back","date":"2026-08-31","type":"writing","description":"Snapshot of the hashcash.org front page as served in August 2026. Adam Back’s own one-paragraph definition of hashcash as \"a proof-of-work algorithm, which has been used as a denial-of-service counter measure technique in a number of systems,\" the note that it \"was invented by Adam Back in 1997,\" and the observation that \"at this point it is most widely used as the bitcoin mining function.\" Links out to the FAQ, the 2002 paper, format info, and the older cypherspace.org/hashcash pages.","citation":"hashcash.org front page, snapshot retrieved 31 August 2026. http://www.hashcash.org/","provenance":"HTML retrieved with curl from http://www.hashcash.org/ on 31 August 2026 (HTTP 200, text/html, 7,054 bytes). Stored unmodified as the source snapshot; the page still carries period markup (Google SiteSearch box, digits.com hit counter) and an author acknowledgement that hashcash is now the Bitcoin mining function. SHA-256 is computed over the archived HTML.","hash":"da92617e99595689777f3cfbb12e881e45db9272014fab12b0b42ea42f18fcd3","tags":["proof-of-work","hashcash","bitcoin-precursor","adam-back","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Hashcash is a proof-of-work algorithm... Hashcash was invented by Adam Back in 1997. At this point it is most widely used as the bitcoin mining function.","files":[{"path":"hashcash-org.html","size":5758,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"hashcash-org-2005-snapshot.html","size":7054,"label":"Original Page Snapshot (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-402.005","local":["content/B59-402.005/hashcash-org.html"]},{"id":"B59-404.001","title":"Bitcoin, What Took Ye So Long?","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2011-05-28","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's 2011 retrospective on the development of Bitcoin and the decades of research that preceded it. Examines why digital currency took so long to be realized despite all the prior theoretical work.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2011). \"Bitcoin, What Took Ye So Long?.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bitcoin-what-took-ye-so-long/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/bitcoin-what-took-ye-so-long/","hash":"6d3f50a9d817f6bfafe58df847b812a7be203fa813e8e9f9abe30b5e2ef1f807","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","bitcoin"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"bitcoin-what-took-ye-so-long.html","size":55087,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-404.001","local":["content/B59-404.001/bitcoin-what-took-ye-so-long.html"]},{"id":"B59-404.002","title":"Money, Blockchains, and Social Scalability","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2017-02-09","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's influential 2017 essay introducing the concept of \"social scalability\" - the ability of institutions to scale up the number of participants who can mutually benefit despite limited trust. Argues that blockchains achieve social scalability by reducing trust requirements.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2017). \"Money, Blockchains, and Social Scalability.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/money-blockchains-and-social-scalability/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/money-blockchains-and-social-scalability/","hash":"a52c9712980efd9fa1cfa47c153bff4563c3d438a58babb83380fe40f6d93380","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","digital-money","blockchain","trust","scalability"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"money-blockchains-social-scalability.html","size":151388,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-404.002","local":["content/B59-404.002/money-blockchains-social-scalability.html"]},{"id":"B59-404.003","title":"JAN3 Welcomes Nick Szabo as Chief Scientist","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2025-01-22","type":"writing","description":"Announcement post from JAN3 (Samson Mow's Bitcoin-focused company) welcoming Nick Szabo as Chief Scientist in January 2025. Marks a new phase of Szabo's public work and represents his continued involvement in Bitcoin-related research.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2025). \"JAN3 Welcomes Nick Szabo as Chief Scientist.\" Retrieved from https://jan3.com/blog/jan3-welcomes-nick-szabo-as-chief-scientist.","provenance":"Retrieved directly from JAN3's blog at https://jan3.com/blog/jan3-welcomes-nick-szabo-as-chief-scientist.","hash":"8e21976680dff5b26fc73534a3a1e472c69035ab2cb66cf1f59bd3b9e93bdc3f","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","blog-post","jan3"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"jan3-welcomes-nick-szabo-chief-scientist.html","entry":true,"size":124163}],"content_path":"content/B59-404.003","local":["content/B59-404.003/jan3-welcomes-nick-szabo-chief-scientist.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.001","title":"Smart Contracts","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1994-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Nick Szabo's foundational essay introducing the concept of \"smart contracts\" - self-executing computerized transaction protocols that enforce contractual clauses. Coined the term that has shaped decades of digital agreement design.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1994). \"Smart Contracts.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/smart-contracts/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/smart-contracts/","hash":"20f5a279593ee74d748d080b64b646ce9f75733920d870abf6453eb3ad7ff5da","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"smart-contracts.html","size":63574,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.001","local":["content/B59-501.001/smart-contracts.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.002","title":"Smart Contracts (1994 Original)","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1994-01-01","type":"writing","description":"The original 1994 essay where Szabo first defined \"smart contracts\" - the term and concept that has shaped decades of subsequent work. This is the foundational text of the entire smart contracts field.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1994). \"Smart Contracts (1994 Original).\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/smart.contracts.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/smart.contracts.html","hash":"99b6465362ddf9656ded2f749418c4bc365e76715dee248eaa63a16f4ae11392","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"smart-contracts-1994-original.html","entry":true,"size":9072}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.002","local":["content/B59-501.002/smart-contracts-1994-original.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.003","title":"Credit with Privity","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1995-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's analysis of how cryptographic protocols can implement credit relationships while preserving the legal concept of privity â€” the principle that only the parties to a contract are bound by its terms. Explores how digital cash and smart contracts can create credit without the liability and disclosure problems of traditional credit instruments.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 1995). Credit with Privity. Retrieved from https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/credit-with-privity/.","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute library. Original source: szabo.best.vwh.net/creditpriv.html.","hash":"b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c4","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","smart-contracts","credit","privity","legal-theory","digital-cash","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"credit-with-privity.html","entry":true,"size":72345}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.003","local":["content/B59-501.003/credit-with-privity.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.004","title":"Smart Contracts Glossary","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1995-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's definitive glossary of terms used in his smart contracts framework. Defines concepts including smart contract, contracting, performance, breach, wet code, dry code, and related legal and cryptographic terms. An essential reference for understanding the precise vocabulary of Szabo's work.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 1995). Smart Contracts Glossary. Retrieved from https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/smart-contracts-glossary/.","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute library. Also available at the UvA mirror: szabo.best.vwh.net/smart_contracts_glossary.html.","hash":"f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","smart-contracts","glossary","legal-theory","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"smart-contracts-glossary.html","entry":true,"size":83696}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.004","local":["content/B59-501.004/smart-contracts-glossary.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.005","title":"Smart Contracts: Building Blocks for Digital Markets","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1996-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's 1996 elaboration of smart contracts as building blocks for digital markets. Lays out the components needed for a functioning digital contract system - the protocols, the trust relationships, and the architectural decisions that would later inform blockchain platforms.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1996). \"Smart Contracts: Building Blocks for Digital Markets.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/smart_contracts_2.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/smart_contracts_2.html","hash":"5c601413af215c614675122142c4c985fd044ea2c231f7b35e0ff1e0c4c03d7a","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"smart-contracts-building-blocks-digital-markets.html","entry":true,"size":56068}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.005","local":["content/B59-501.005/smart-contracts-building-blocks-digital-markets.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.006","title":"The Idea of Smart Contracts","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1997-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's elaboration of the smart contract concept, examining how cryptographic protocols and digital security mechanisms could embed contractual obligations directly into hardware and software. Argues for minimizing the need for trusted intermediaries through automated enforcement.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1997). \"The Idea of Smart Contracts.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/the-idea-of-smart-contracts/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/the-idea-of-smart-contracts/","hash":"8fad07a3476eca7dc17b97054067146d072cb01c59b10c7b55be82306fd679ff","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts","trust"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-idea-of-smart-contracts.html","size":53438,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.006","local":["content/B59-501.006/the-idea-of-smart-contracts.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.007","title":"Formalizing and Securing Relationships on Public Networks","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1997-09-01","type":"writing","description":"Published in First Monday (Vol. 2, No. 9). A comprehensive paper on the application of cryptographic and contract-theoretic principles to relationships on public networks. Outlines the conceptual framework that smart contracts and digital agreements would later build upon.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1997). \"Formalizing and Securing Relationships on Public Networks.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/formalizing-securing-relationships/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/formalizing-securing-relationships/","hash":"3b58976fbb24e0546daa65b81e80777615c49aca6437492b44b9f940c7604a10","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"formalizing-securing-relationships.html","size":242554,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.007","local":["content/B59-501.007/formalizing-securing-relationships.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.008","title":"Video Contracts","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Proposal for using video technology to create more robust and verifiable contracts. An early exploration of how multimedia could enhance the security and clarity of contractual agreements.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Video Contracts.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/video.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/video.html","hash":"620a9606ab7b556a59a29f7f18f3dcc26caa13070acb369aab2e214ef6c8128d","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"video-contracts.html","entry":true,"size":11558}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.008","local":["content/B59-501.008/video-contracts.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.009","title":"Multinational Small Business","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1999-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on how cryptographic protocols and smart contracts could enable small businesses to operate across international borders without reliance on traditional financial intermediaries or national legal systems. Explores the implications of digital cash and self-enforcing contracts for global microcommerce.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 1999). Multinational Small Business. Retrieved from https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/rob/Courses/InformationInSpeech/CDROM/Literature/LOTwinterschool2006/szabo.best.vwh.net/multi.small.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net. Original filename: multi.small.html.","hash":"a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b3","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","smart-contracts","digital-cash","international-commerce","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"multinational-small-business.html","entry":true,"size":5710}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.009","local":["content/B59-501.009/multinational-small-business.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.010","title":"The Playdough Protocols","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2001-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on the cryptographic and institutional design of bearer instruments â€” physical and digital tokens that function like clay tablets, vouchers, or promissory notes. Explores how trust, physical form, and cryptographic verification can be combined in protocols for transferring value without a central authority.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 2001). The Playdough Protocols. Retrieved from https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/the-playdough-protocols/.","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute library. Original source: szabo.best.vwh.net/clay.html.","hash":"b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","smart-contracts","bearer-instruments","cryptography","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"the-playdough-protocols.html","entry":true,"size":100044}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.010","local":["content/B59-501.010/the-playdough-protocols.html"]},{"id":"B59-501.011","title":"Contracts with Bearer","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Examines digital bearer contracts - cryptographic instruments where possession of a token grants the holder specific rights. Foundational to thinking about cryptographic property rights and the architecture of digital cash and stablecoins.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2002). \"Contracts with Bearer.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/contracts-with-bearer/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/contracts-with-bearer/","hash":"6e18f8173faf4196165d46d3a99dfdd5a8bdfee9ff8259e74837077790b2b8e9","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts","digital-property"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"contracts-with-bearer.html","size":89851,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-501.011","local":["content/B59-501.011/contracts-with-bearer.html"]},{"id":"B59-502.001","title":"A Formal Language for Analyzing Contracts","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's proposal for a formal language to represent and analyze contracts. Develops the theoretical foundation for representing contractual obligations in a way that can be mechanically processed - prefiguring the smart contract languages that would emerge with blockchain platforms.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2002). \"A Formal Language for Analyzing Contracts.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/contractlanguage.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/contractlanguage.html","hash":"41622787a3985aeec1df7cb51d98292dcd58ad8f3bc3b9332e675527cd389d9c","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","smart-contracts"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"formal-language-analyzing-contracts.html","entry":true,"size":69493}],"content_path":"content/B59-502.001","local":["content/B59-502.001/formal-language-analyzing-contracts.html"]},{"id":"B59-502.002","title":"The Ricardian Contract","author":"Ian Grigg","date":"2004-07-06","type":"writing","description":"Ian Grigg's description of the Ricardian Contract, the document design he and Gary Howland built into the Ricardo payment system in 1996. A Ricardian contract is a single instrument that is simultaneously human-readable legal prose and machine-parsable data, digitally signed by the issuer, and bound to every transaction that references it by a cryptographic hash of the document. It is the direct ancestor of the \"contract hash\" pattern in later smart-contract and token systems, and is frequently cited alongside Nick Szabo's smart-contracts work. Named for economist David Ricardo.","citation":"Ian Grigg, \"The Ricardian Contract,\" Proceedings of the First IEEE International Workshop on Electronic Contracting (WEC), 6 July 2004, pp. 25-31. Systemics, Inc. / iang.org.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://iang.org/papers/ricardian_contract.html. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. A version of this work also appeared at the IEEE International Workshop on Electronic Contracting 2004; this record hosts Grigg's own self-published HTML essay from iang.org.","hash":"636aea34895906bd4854540ceae1faafdca2a5431492c086692a7d8da88dd450","tags":["ian-grigg","ricardian-contract","financial-cryptography","smart-contracts","digital-issuance","2004","primary-source"],"excerpt":"The essence of the technique is to bridge the gap between the paper world, where the value of everything is described in words, and the digital world, where things have value by dint of secure identification.","files":[{"path":"the-ricardian-contract.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":32963},{"path":"the-ricardian-contract-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":38332}],"content_path":"content/B59-502.002","local":["content/B59-502.002/the-ricardian-contract.html"]},{"id":"B59-503.001","title":"Proplets","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's concept of \"proplets\" - small property tokens that could be used in digital commerce. Architecture for securing and transferring physical and digital property using cryptographic credentials. A precursor to tokenization and NFT concepts.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Proplets.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/proplets.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/proplets.html","hash":"21331cafd30098077679e01478731bce8bdb99e8b4b6b6c33ec1d516d847e37d","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","digital-property","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"proplets.html","entry":true,"size":9802}],"content_path":"content/B59-503.001","local":["content/B59-503.001/proplets.html"]},{"id":"B59-503.002","title":"Secure Property Titles with Owner Authority","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Proposal for cryptographically secured property title systems that would allow owners to assert claims without relying on centralized registries. Foundational to thinking about decentralized ownership records and what would become NFT and tokenization concepts.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Secure Property Titles with Owner Authority.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/secure-property-titles/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/secure-property-titles/","hash":"68b65911e238079f65df15a25c9db72b8bbb20da5f79c1df2a1e6011e4c0dc40","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","digital-property"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"secure-property-titles.html","size":88697,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-503.002","local":["content/B59-503.002/secure-property-titles.html"]},{"id":"B59-503.003","title":"Scarce Objects","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on the concept of scarce objects â€” digital or physical items whose value derives from their unforgeable scarcity. Explores how cryptographic and institutional mechanisms can create and protect scarcity in digital environments, a foundational concept in the intellectual genealogy of Bitcoin.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 2003). Scarce Objects. Retrieved from http://web.archive.org/web/20160417231212/http://szabo.best.vwh.net/scarce.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from Wayback Machine archive of Szabo's personal essay site szabo.best.vwh.net (archived 2016). Original URL: http://szabo.best.vwh.net/scarce.html.","hash":"a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","smart-contracts","scarcity","digital-property","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"scarce-objects.html","entry":true,"size":55340}],"content_path":"content/B59-503.003","local":["content/B59-503.003/scarce-objects.html"]},{"id":"B59-503.004","title":"The Digital Path: Smart Contracts and the Third World","author":"Mark S. Miller & Marc Stiegler","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"An essay by Mark S. Miller and Marc Stiegler responding to Hernando de Soto's The Mystery of Capital. Where de Soto argues that the developing world's informally held assets fail to become capital because national legal systems will not recognize them, Miller and Stiegler propose building the enforcement machinery of contract, title and rights transfer directly as capability-secure software, so that credible property-rights transfer \"at a distance\" can be bootstrapped without first reforming the state. An early and unusually concrete statement of the case for smart contracts as institutional infrastructure. Published in the collection Markets, Information and Communication (Routledge).","citation":"Mark S. Miller and Marc Stiegler, \"The Digital Path: Smart Contracts and the Third World,\" in Markets, Information and Communication: Austrian Perspectives on the Internet Economy, eds. Jack Birner and Pierre Garrouste (Routledge, 2003). Author copy at papers.agoric.com.","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. Published as a chapter in Markets, Information and Communication: Austrian Perspectives on the Internet Economy (Routledge, 2003), a commercial academic publisher. A copy is available via the Agoric paper archive at the linked page. Recorded as an external reference. The work can be read at https://papers.agoric.com/papers/the-digital-path-smart-contracts-and-the-third-world/abstract/ . Per Kat's 2026-08-31 rule for the B59 scrape, publisher-controlled works (commercial books, magazine/journal articles, academic-publisher papers) are recorded as external references with citation and link only, not hosted or hashed.","hash":"","tags":["mark-s-miller","marc-stiegler","smart-contracts","object-capability","agoric","property-rights","hernando-de-soto","2003","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":"De Soto hopes to bring the power of capital to the poor by reforming the legal systems and bureaucracies of their national governments. This is no easy task ... We propose an alternative, complementary path: build the machinery of contract and property-rights transfer as software, on capability-secure distributed systems, so that it can spread without waiting for the state.","files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://papers.agoric.com/papers/the-digital-path-smart-contracts-and-the-third-world/abstract/"},{"id":"B59-504.001","title":"Delegation and Agreement Based Certification Policy","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1997-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's framework for delegation and agreement-based certification - how authority can be delegated and certified in a distributed system without relying on a central certificate authority.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1997). \"Delegation and Agreement Based Certification Policy.\" Retrieved from https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/delegation-and-agreement-based-certification-policy/.","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library at https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/delegation-and-agreement-based-certification-policy/.","hash":"2b7053be945ed8232810972402eb4ba51fb48e1e8696c1b1eab18b436e9e0646","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"delegation-agreement-based-certification-policy.html","entry":true,"size":63802}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.001","local":["content/B59-504.001/delegation-agreement-based-certification-policy.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.002","title":"Hermeneutics","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1997-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's exploration of hermeneutics - the theory of interpretation - applied to legal and computational systems. Examines how the meanings of contracts, code, and rules are constructed through interpretation rather than read off mechanically.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1997). \"Hermeneutics.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/hermeneutics.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/hermeneutics.html","hash":"24ee3776ecf495fff478e3d8c86d7b2f2bf6063ceadc77d13afc81d022b94b78","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","legal-theory"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"hermeneutics.html","entry":true,"size":23889}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.002","local":["content/B59-504.002/hermeneutics.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.003","title":"Online Communities: From the Usenet FAQ","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's analysis of online communities drawing on the Usenet FAQ and the patterns of self-governance that emerged in early Internet forums. Relevant to understanding decentralized community moderation.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Online Communities: From the Usenet FAQ.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/usenet.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/usenet.html","hash":"8ef83a933c22fc9fdbf3018902f8e583738b31e499e9247bf6d7d74559903ffe","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"online-communities-usenet-faq.html","entry":true,"size":4439}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.003","local":["content/B59-504.003/online-communities-usenet-faq.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.004","title":"Patterns of Integrity: Separation of Duties","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Examines the principle of separation of duties as a fundamental security pattern. Argues that the same principle that prevents fraud in accounting practices applies broadly to computer system design and cryptographic protocols.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Patterns of Integrity: Separation of Duties.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/separationofduties.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/separationofduties.html","hash":"c4f6f4821a06628abf4202227bd8b13dd81824648fe872615546fe45301c5109","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"patterns-of-integrity-separation-of-duties.html","entry":true,"size":11521}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.004","local":["content/B59-504.004/patterns-of-integrity-separation-of-duties.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.005","title":"Trusted Services and Group Controls","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1998-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Examines the design of trusted services and the controls that groups can impose on their members and operations. Foundational thinking about institutional design in digital contexts.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1998). \"Trusted Services and Group Controls.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/groupcontrols.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/groupcontrols.html","hash":"fd66c2dcf3e03c1083bf410629df97a80e59fccc34c0d2c4e334e9e8a856f6ae","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"trusted-services-group-controls.html","entry":true,"size":8420}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.005","local":["content/B59-504.005/trusted-services-group-controls.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.006","title":"The God Protocols","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1999-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Published in ITAudit (Vol. 2). Szabo's vision of \"God protocols\" - hypothetical cryptographic protocols that would perform computations with absolute trustworthiness, preserving input confidentiality while producing verifiable outputs. A theoretical target for multiparty secure computation.","citation":"Szabo, N. (1999). \"The God Protocols.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/the-god-protocols/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/the-god-protocols/","hash":"129566b6940bfe87057fdfe5e480f7c03d5ebdadcea3a392a9c440dca55562e8","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","trust","auditing"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"the-god-protocols.html","size":68397,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.006","local":["content/B59-504.006/the-god-protocols.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.007","title":"Financial Cryptography in 7 Layers","author":"Ian Grigg","date":"2000-02-01","type":"writing","description":"Grigg's synthesis of a decade of building payment systems (DigiCash, Ricardo, e-gold-era issuance) into a layered model: cryptography, software engineering, rights, accounting, governance, value and finance, each a distinct discipline that must be right for the whole to hold. Written for the Financial Cryptography 2000 conference, it is an early and influential statement that the hard part of digital money is not the crypto primitive but the integration of law, accounting and governance around it, an argument that reads as a direct forerunner of the design debates around Bitcoin and later token systems.","citation":"Ian Grigg, \"Financial Cryptography in 7 Layers,\" 4th International Conference on Financial Cryptography (FC 2000), Anguilla, February 2000. Springer LNCS 1962. Author copy at iang.org.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://iang.org/papers/fc7.html. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot. A version also appeared in the Financial Cryptography 2000 proceedings (Springer LNCS 1962); this record hosts Grigg's own self-published HTML essay from iang.org.","hash":"fe8b8fc3d2015fcc757651d8c09ac42a73c3dc2831b777610512b89282e2f9d3","tags":["ian-grigg","financial-cryptography","systems-architecture","digital-cash","governance","2000","primary-source"],"excerpt":"The seven layers: Cryptography, Software Engineering, Rights, Accounting, Governance, Value, Finance. ... The nature of Financial Cryptography is that a failure at any layer can bring down the whole system.","files":[{"path":"financial-cryptography-in-7-layers.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":47289},{"path":"financial-cryptography-in-7-layers-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":63785}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.007","local":["content/B59-504.007/financial-cryptography-in-7-layers.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.008","title":"Trusted Third Parties Are Security Holes","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2001-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Foundational essay arguing that trusted third parties represent security vulnerabilities in computer protocols. Has become a touchstone reference in cryptography and decentralized system design - the principle behind why blockchain systems minimize trust requirements.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2001). \"Trusted Third Parties Are Security Holes.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/trusted-third-parties/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/trusted-third-parties/","hash":"d0c29beca1f9a51984633f476265a7cb55e34308099a6a9fa1482f235e7af5c8","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","blockchain","trust"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"trusted-third-parties.html","size":107918,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.008","local":["content/B59-504.008/trusted-third-parties.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.009","title":"Confidential Auditing","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Proposal for cryptographic protocols that allow auditing of accounts and transactions while preserving the confidentiality of underlying data. Addresses the tension between transparency and privacy in financial systems.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2002). \"Confidential Auditing.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/confidential-auditing/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/confidential-auditing/","hash":"fefa53b8afd7ec595b974a58179d7c77adda9148e4459cf1ac95a3e81c04e83d","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","auditing"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"confidential-auditing.html","size":57108,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.009","local":["content/B59-504.009/confidential-auditing.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.010","title":"Advances in Distributed Security","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's overview of advances in distributed security architectures, surveying techniques for building secure systems without centralized trust points. Covers practical applications of the principles in \"Trusted Third Parties Are Security Holes.\"","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Advances in Distributed Security.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/advances-in-distributed-security/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/advances-in-distributed-security/","hash":"20f7f97803ced637e769c837c8c2e51b7034fa977f48330250e312ebe00e7e86","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","trust"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"advances-distributed-security.html","size":193437,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.010","local":["content/B59-504.010/advances-distributed-security.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.011","title":"Coalition Design for Secure Protocols","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Analyzes how secure protocols should be designed to remain secure even when subsets of participants form coalitions to attack the protocol. Game-theoretic approach to cryptographic protocol design.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Coalition Design for Secure Protocols.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/coalition.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/coalition.html","hash":"d1bd146301e87ece46249b87270883268c930ba02c88b234a59636ca9dfaa1bd","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"coalition-design-secure-protocols.html","entry":true,"size":6727}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.011","local":["content/B59-504.011/coalition-design-secure-protocols.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.012","title":"Distributing Authorities and Verifying Their Claims","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Examines how authority can be distributed across multiple parties and how verifying their claims requires careful protocol design. Foundational to thinking about decentralized governance and federated identity systems.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Distributing Authorities and Verifying Their Claims.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/authorities.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/authorities.html","hash":"47a54450760508b80d855e506d84fdad9f0022ad7506a83a45e9463cb811977c","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"distributing-authorities-verifying-claims.html","entry":true,"size":9450}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.012","local":["content/B59-504.012/distributing-authorities-verifying-claims.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.013","title":"Recovering Privity","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's argument for restoring the legal doctrine of privity of contract - the principle that contracts bind only the parties to them. Examines how this doctrine has eroded and the implications for digital agreements and decentralized commerce.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Recovering Privity.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/privity.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/privity.html","hash":"b8a0eb87cee322fcefe22975aebcc5a8a519cd4478d5e28b7d9377c8de95e8e0","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","legal-theory"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"recovering-privity.html","entry":true,"size":10064}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.013","local":["content/B59-504.013/recovering-privity.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.014","title":"There Is No Universal Security Architecture","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Argues against the notion of a single universal security architecture that could solve all security problems. Different threat models, environments, and adversaries require different security architectures - one size cannot fit all.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"There Is No Universal Security Architecture.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/universal.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/universal.html","hash":"f5a1b2e71bd17961af3847c0db97837f2b1bf54922757d4c26a55802648ad98e","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"no-universal-security-architecture.html","entry":true,"size":3664}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.014","local":["content/B59-504.014/no-universal-security-architecture.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.015","title":"Rights, Remedies, and Security Models","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2004-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's exploration of the relationship between legal rights, available remedies, and the security models that protocols enforce. Bridges legal theory and cryptographic protocol design.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2004). \"Rights, Remedies, and Security Models.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/rights-remedies-and-security-models/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/rights-remedies-and-security-models/","hash":"cb90b1df645619daaedb2738f7f612ad3d50c3b2c91566701e6b4b01b9b8591b","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"rights-remedies-security-models.html","size":69941,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.015","local":["content/B59-504.015/rights-remedies-security-models.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.016","title":"Triple Entry Accounting","author":"Ian Grigg","date":"2005-12-25","type":"writing","description":"Grigg's essay proposing that a digitally signed receipt shared between payer, payee and a trusted third party (the issuer) forms a third accounting entry that is more authoritative than either party's own books, making fraud and disagreement far harder. Written from the Ricardo and e-gial systems experience, \"triple-entry accounting\" became one of the standard conceptual lenses for describing what a blockchain does: a shared, cryptographically signed ledger entry that both counterparties and third parties can rely on. Frequently cited in the Bitcoin and distributed-ledger literature.","citation":"Ian Grigg, \"Triple Entry Accounting,\" Systemics, Inc., 25 December 2005. iang.org/papers/triple_entry.html.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://iang.org/papers/triple_entry.html. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot.","hash":"a03176e6b4fd894e943660b7c6cc20a7b3c2364ea7288bebc017c6f5fa321508","tags":["ian-grigg","triple-entry-accounting","financial-cryptography","bookkeeping","digital-receipts","distributed-ledger","2005","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Where a receipt is digitally signed by all three parties - the payer, the payee, and the issuer - the receipt becomes the transaction. ... Double entry bookkeeping was the greatest innovation of the Renaissance; triple entry is the natural extension of it into the world of shared, cryptographically signed records.","files":[{"path":"triple-entry-accounting.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":38039},{"path":"triple-entry-accounting-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":42624}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.016","local":["content/B59-504.016/triple-entry-accounting.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.017","title":"Wet Code and Dry","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2006-11-26","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's influential blog post distinguishing \"wet code\" (interpretation by human judges and lawyers) from \"dry code\" (executable computer code). Argues that the boundary between the two is one of the most consequential design questions in any legal-technical system.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2006). \"Wet Code and Dry.\" Retrieved from https://unenumerated.blogspot.com/2006/11/wet-code-and-dry.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from Szabo's Unenumerated blog at https://unenumerated.blogspot.com/2006/11/wet-code-and-dry.html.","hash":"7b2f241a7cae1f37aa58d7a963265cff7ee6a7c369070bf60511d8093e584d98","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","legal-theory","blog-post"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"wet-code-and-dry.html","entry":true,"size":75196}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.017","local":["content/B59-504.017/wet-code-and-dry.html"]},{"id":"B59-504.018","title":"The Fabric of Desires","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2025-02-01","type":"writing","description":"One of Szabo's first essays published under his role at JAN3. A philosophical piece exploring the relationship between human desires, institutions, and the technologies that mediate between them.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2025). \"The Fabric of Desires.\" Retrieved from https://jan3.com/blog/the-fabric-of-desires.","provenance":"Retrieved directly from JAN3's blog at https://jan3.com/blog/the-fabric-of-desires.","hash":"218a6316385582bafc09b8745bb7bb9152d0394292213b24dead18e2173bb797","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","blog-post","jan3"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"the-fabric-of-desires.html","entry":true,"size":124988}],"content_path":"content/B59-504.018","local":["content/B59-504.018/the-fabric-of-desires.html"]},{"id":"B59-505.001","title":"Markets and Computation: Agoric Open Systems","author":"Mark S. Miller & K. Eric Drexler","date":"1988-01-01","type":"writing","description":"The foundational \"agoric systems\" paper by Mark S. Miller and K. Eric Drexler, from The Ecology of Computation (North-Holland, 1988). It proposes organizing computation as a market: software objects that own resources, price them, and trade, with property rights enforced by an underlying capability security model, so that global allocation emerges from local decisions. This is the direct intellectual root of Miller's later object-capability work (E, Robust Composition, B59-505.004), of the Agoric company, and of the \"high tech Hayekian\" milieu documented in B59-505.002. Hosted from the author's company paper archive.","citation":"Mark S. Miller and K. Eric Drexler, \"Markets and Computation: Agoric Open Systems,\" in The Ecology of Computation, ed. B. A. Huberman (North-Holland/Elsevier, 1988), pp. 133-176.","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. Published in The Ecology of Computation, ed. B. A. Huberman (North-Holland / Elsevier, 1988), a commercial publisher. A copy is available via the Agoric paper archive at the linked page. Recorded as an external reference. Cross-reference B59-505.002 (High Tech Hayekians) and B59-505.004 (Robust Composition). The work can be read at https://papers.agoric.com/papers/markets-and-computation-agoric-open-systems/abstract/ . Per Kat's 2026-08-31 rule for the B59 scrape, publisher-controlled works (commercial books, magazine/journal articles, academic-publisher papers) are recorded as external references with citation and link only, not hosted or hashed.","hash":"","tags":["mark-s-miller","eric-drexler","agoric","object-capability","market-computation","friedrich-hayek","distributed-systems","1988","primary-source","external-reference"],"excerpt":"This paper examines markets as a model for computation and proposes a framework - agoric systems - for applying the power of market mechanisms to the software domain. ... such a system should give rise to increasingly intelligent behavior as an emergent property of interactions among software entities and people.","files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://papers.agoric.com/papers/markets-and-computation-agoric-open-systems/abstract/"},{"id":"B59-505.002","title":"High Tech Hayekians","author":"Don Lavoie, Howard Baetjer, Bill Tulloh, Mark Miller, Eric Drexler, Phil Salin","date":"1990-03-01","type":"writing","description":"Academic paper published in Market Process (Vol. 8, Spring 1990) by the Center for the Study of Market Processes at George Mason University. Documents the Agorics Project, an interdisciplinary exploration of how Friedrich Hayek's economic theories of spontaneous order apply to computer science and software architecture. Describes how Mark Miller and Eric Drexler's work on applying Hayekian principles to computational resource management led to a research collaboration with Austrian economists. Phil Salin is credited with introducing Miller and Drexler to Hayek. The project simulated Mengerian processes for the evolution of money in computational systems.","citation":"Lavoie, D. et al. (1990). \"High Tech Hayekians.\" Market Process, Vol. 8, Spring 1990. Center for the Study of Market Processes, George Mason University. Reprinted from philsalin.com.","provenance":"Retrieved from philsalin.com/hth/hth.html. Slated for reprint in Austrian Perspectives on the New Economy (2001, Routledge). Content verified against known publications from the GMU Center for the Study of Market Processes and the bibliography of the Agorics project.","hash":"759898de1e4b334a0e9a29928e2b26b6f1411b0c64efd9e02c49290a6243b858","tags":["agoric","friedrich-hayek","austrian-economics","mark-s-miller","eric-drexler","phil-salin","don-lavoie","smart-contracts","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"high-tech-hayekians.html","size":110105,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"high-tech-hayekians.mht","size":254141,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-505.002","local":["content/B59-505.002/high-tech-hayekians.html"]},{"id":"B59-505.003","title":"Names: Decentralized, Secure, Human-Meaningful: Choose Two","author":"Zooko Wilcox-O'Hearn","date":"2003-09-22","type":"writing","description":"The essay that became known as \"Zooko's Triangle.\" Wilcox-O'Hearn argues that a naming system can be at most two of: decentralized (spanning trust boundaries without a central authority), secure (resistant to deception), and human-meaningful (names people can remember and use). Self-authenticating names such as content hashes and public-key fingerprints are secure and decentralized but not memorable; human-meaningful names require either a trusted authority or accepting insecurity. The framing shaped two decades of work on petnames, YURLs, key management, and later blockchain naming systems such as Namecoin and ENS that claim to break the triangle. Snapshot taken from the Internet Archive because the live zooko.com now redirects to a placeholder.","citation":"Zooko Wilcox-O'Hearn, \"Names: Decentralized, Secure, Human-Meaningful: Choose Two,\" version 0.9.9.0, 22 September 2003, zooko.com/distnames.html.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the Internet Archive Wayback Machine (http://web.archive.org/web/20111227083803id_/http://zooko.com/distnames.html), a raw (\"id_\") capture of http://zooko.com/distnames.html; the live URL no longer serves the document. The served bytes are archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page whose body text is extracted mechanically from that snapshot (no hand-transcription). SHA-256 is computed over the archived source snapshot.","hash":"0dc98df1028d0eabc120a548e67fadd014f028c677b51d0ff5c5450e5813b9ed","tags":["zooko-wilcox-o-hearn","zookos-triangle","naming","petnames","trust","decentralization","financial-cryptography","2003","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Non-Self-Authenticating Names Cannot Span Trust Boundaries. Self-Authenticating Names Cannot Be Meaningful To Humans. ... you can have a naming system with any two of these three properties, but not all three.","files":[{"path":"zookos-triangle-names.html","label":"Read Document","entry":true,"size":13870},{"path":"zookos-triangle-names-source.html","label":"Original Snapshot (Primary Source)","size":14857}],"content_path":"content/B59-505.003","local":["content/B59-505.003/zookos-triangle-names.html"]},{"id":"B59-505.004","title":"Robust Composition: Towards a Unified Approach to Access Control and Concurrency Control","author":"Mark S. Miller","date":"2006-05-01","type":"writing","description":"Mark S. Miller's 2006 Johns Hopkins PhD dissertation, the reference statement of the object-capability model of secure computation. It develops the model through the design of the E language and its patterns for defensive programming, promise-based distributed messaging (\"concurrency among strangers\"), and confinement without a global policy, and argues that access control and concurrency control are the same problem. Robust Composition is the theoretical backbone of the capability-security lineage that runs through KeyKOS, E, Caja, the Tahoe-LAFS caps (B59-505.005), Agoric's smart-contract stack and de-facto through modern hardened-JavaScript. The author explicitly licenses verbatim redistribution.","citation":"Mark Samuel Miller, \"Robust Composition: Towards a Unified Approach to Access Control and Concurrency Control,\" Ph.D. dissertation, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, Maryland, May 2006.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched directly with curl from https://papers.agoric.com/assets/pdf/papers/robust-composition.pdf. The PDF is archived unmodified and hosted in full; SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes. The styled reader page carries the abstract/opening (extracted mechanically with pdftotext), the citation, and archival notes, and links to the hosted PDF. No text was hand-transcribed. The document itself grants permission to make and distribute verbatim copies without royalty or fee, so it is hosted in full. Cross-reference B59-505.002 (High Tech Hayekians) and B59-505.001 (Markets and Computation).","hash":"ec1850bb8dc0627202d8d19dbcea6a79efb8db7bb2b98c8876f1548cd319f3d3","tags":["mark-s-miller","object-capability","robust-composition","e-language","access-control","concurrency","secure-computation","2006","primary-source","thesis"],"excerpt":"Permission is hereby granted to make and distribute verbatim copies of this document without royalty or fee. ... The object-capability model unifies the treatment of access control and concurrency control, and supports patterns of cooperation without vulnerability.","files":[{"path":"robust-composition.html","label":"Read (Abstract + Notes)","entry":true,"size":4737},{"path":"robust-composition.pdf","label":"Original Paper (PDF, Primary Source)","size":1882311}],"content_path":"content/B59-505.004","local":["content/B59-505.004/robust-composition.html"]},{"id":"B59-505.005","title":"Tahoe - The Least-Authority Filesystem","author":"Zooko Wilcox-O'Hearn & Brian Warner","date":"2008-10-31","type":"writing","description":"The design paper for Tahoe-LAFS, presented at the ACM StorageSS'08 workshop. Tahoe distributes files across many servers using erasure coding, so that any K of N servers suffice to recover a file, and combines this with cryptographic \"capabilities as keys\": a read-cap, verify-cap or read-write-cap is a short unguessable string that is necessary and sufficient to exercise exactly that level of access, and can be diminished (read-write to read-only to verify) and delegated by simply sharing the string. The result is a storage grid where servers cannot read, forge or roll back user data even if compromised or coerced. Tahoe-LAFS is a direct application of the object-capability model (see B59-505.004) to distributed storage, and part of the lineage from DigiCash through Tahoe to Zcash.","citation":"Zooko Wilcox-O'Hearn and Brian Warner, \"Tahoe - The Least-Authority Filesystem,\" Proceedings of the 4th ACM International Workshop on Storage Security and Survivability (StorageSS'08), Fairfax, Virginia, 31 October 2008, pp. 21-26.","provenance":"Primary-source PDF retrieved 2026-08-31 with curl from the Agoric paper archive (papers.agoric.com/assets/pdf/papers/tahoe-the-least-authority-filesystem.pdf); archived unmodified and hosted in full. SHA-256 is computed over the PDF bytes. Per the final copyright policy (2026-08-31) academic workshop papers that are core primary sources are hosted (link-only is reserved for full-length commercial books). Cross-reference B59-505.004 (Miller, Robust Composition) for the object-capability model Tahoe applies. Internet Archive was unreachable for an independent second-source check.","hash":"e1eeb7c7440d6eac082393c87b704e4b0db8efe05e188d1f18c168eaca151b9b","tags":["zooko-wilcox-o-hearn","brian-warner","tahoe-lafs","object-capability","least-authority","distributed-storage","erasure-coding","cryptography","2008","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Tahoe was designed following the Principle of Least Authority - each user or process that needs to accomplish a task should be able to perform that task without having or wielding more authority than is necessary. ... a capability is a short string of bits which uniquely identifies one file or directory. Knowledge of that identifier is necessary and sufficient to gain access to the object that it identifies.","files":[{"path":"tahoe-least-authority-filesystem.html","size":13742,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"tahoe-least-authority-filesystem.pdf","size":275101,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-505.005","local":["content/B59-505.005/tahoe-least-authority-filesystem.html"]},{"id":"B59-506.001","title":"Resources for the History of Commercial Law and Governance","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's curated bibliography and annotated link collection covering the history of commercial law, the Law Merchant (lex mercatoria), Brehon law, medieval trade institutions, and the origins of property rights and contract enforcement. A key research aid for understanding the historical institutional parallels Szabo draws throughout his smart contracts work.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 2002). Resources for the History of Commercial Law and Governance. Retrieved from https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/rob/Courses/InformationInSpeech/CDROM/Literature/LOTwinterschool2006/szabo.best.vwh.net/lawlinks.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net. Original filename: lawlinks.html (also referenced as commercial.html on the site index).","hash":"e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","commercial-law","legal-history","lex-mercatoria","brehon-law","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"resources-history-commercial-law-governance.html","entry":true,"size":16561}],"content_path":"content/B59-506.001","local":["content/B59-506.001/resources-history-commercial-law-governance.html"]},{"id":"B59-506.002","title":"Brehon Law","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Examines Brehon Law - the early Irish legal system that operated through reputation, surety, and decentralized enforcement before the imposition of centralized Norman law. A historical case study in legal systems that functioned without a strong central authority.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2003). \"Brehon Law.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/brehon.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/brehon.html","hash":"2f346b64b5aced2893b012ed5b34a0f3cf5c7e5e83cf4c19ff68b33a042ad4ff","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","legal-theory"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"brehon-law.html","entry":true,"size":4187}],"content_path":"content/B59-506.002","local":["content/B59-506.002/brehon-law.html"]},{"id":"B59-601.001","title":"The Use of Knowledge in Society","author":"Friedrich A. Hayek","date":"1945-09-01","type":"writing","description":"Hayek's answer to the socialist-calculation debate and the founding statement of the price system as a distributed information-processing mechanism. Argues that the knowledge needed to run an economy exists only as dispersed, local, often tacit fragments held by millions of individuals, and can never be gathered into one mind or one planning board; prices communicate just enough of that scattered knowledge for people to coordinate without anyone grasping the whole. First published in the American Economic Review, September 1945; reprinted in Individualism and Economic Order (1948). A core reference in the digital-cash and decentralization literature.","citation":"F. A. Hayek, \"The Use of Knowledge in Society,\" American Economic Review, Vol. XXXV, No. 4 (September 1945), pp. 519-530.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the Mises Institute's hosted full-text copy (mises.org/mises-daily/use-knowledge-society), which reproduces the 1945 American Economic Review article. Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot. Text cross-checked against the well-established published wording of the essay.","hash":"6bf8a4245a5d09d6c52808b201fa061bfb8f11aa5225580c90a864778ed467cb","tags":["monetary-theory","austrian-economics","friedrich-hayek","knowledge-problem","spontaneous-order","price-system","primary-source","1945"],"excerpt":"The peculiar character of the problem of a rational economic order is determined precisely by the fact that the knowledge of the circumstances of which we must make use never exists in concentrated or integrated form but solely as the dispersed bits of incomplete and frequently contradictory knowledge which all the separate individuals possess.","files":[{"path":"use-of-knowledge-in-society.html","size":35390,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"use-of-knowledge-in-society-source.html","size":291190,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-601.001","local":["content/B59-601.001/use-of-knowledge-in-society.html"]},{"id":"B59-601.002","title":"The Pretence of Knowledge","author":"Friedrich A. Hayek","date":"1974-12-11","type":"writing","description":"Hayek's Nobel Memorial Prize lecture, delivered 11 December 1974. It attacks the \"scientistic\" imitation of the physical sciences in economics: the pretence that we can measure and control a complex order the way an engineer controls a machine. Hayek argues that the economist should aim only to cultivate the conditions for a spontaneous order to grow, \"as the gardener does this for his plants,\" and closes with a warning against the fatal presumption of knowledge that turns the social planner into a tyrant.","citation":"Friedrich August von Hayek, \"The Pretence of Knowledge,\" Prize Lecture, Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, Stockholm, 11 December 1974.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from NobelPrize.org (nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/1974/hayek/lecture/), the official published text of the 1974 lecture, including its numbered notes. Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"ab57eae78aaa9b5fcfbe3d2882b1bd0f9882fc0478d20918a7785cf30a3a14a9","tags":["monetary-theory","austrian-economics","friedrich-hayek","nobel-lecture","spontaneous-order","knowledge-problem","primary-source","1974"],"excerpt":"To act on the belief that we possess the knowledge and the power which enable us to shape the processes of society entirely to our liking, knowledge which in fact we do not possess, is likely to make us do much harm.","files":[{"path":"pretence-of-knowledge.html","size":33970,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"pretence-of-knowledge-source.html","size":191029,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-601.002","local":["content/B59-601.002/pretence-of-knowledge.html"]},{"id":"B59-601.003","title":"Measuring Value","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on the philosophical and economic foundations of measuring value. Explores why value cannot be measured the way physical quantities can be, and what this means for monetary and contractual systems.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2002). \"Measuring Value.\" Retrieved from https://szabo.best.vwh.net/measuringvalue.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net (the original site, now defunct). Original URL: https://szabo.best.vwh.net/measuringvalue.html","hash":"d235ff1b799e15800db43946f78a21e094c22752d36f874e31b6eadcfcac07f5","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"measuring-value.html","entry":true,"size":39798}],"content_path":"content/B59-601.003","local":["content/B59-601.003/measuring-value.html"]},{"id":"B59-601.004","title":"Shelling Out: The Origins of Money","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"2002-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's influential anthropological essay on the origins of money, drawing on game theory, evolutionary psychology, and the concept of focal points (Schelling points). Argues that money emerged from collectible objects valued for non-monetary reasons before becoming media of exchange.","citation":"Szabo, N. (2002). \"Shelling Out: The Origins of Money.\" Retrieved from Nakamoto Institute Library (https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/shelling-out/).","provenance":"Retrieved from the Satoshi Nakamoto Institute Library, which preserves Szabo's writings from his original (now defunct) szabo.best.vwh.net site and related publications. Original URL: https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/shelling-out/","hash":"99ac9c41c3e774faeb51cc230a50fcdfda1baa6a49e16d713520b033306ed12d","tags":["nick-szabo","cypherpunks","primary-source","cryptography","digital-money"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"shelling-out.html","size":252611,"label":"Read Essay","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-601.004","local":["content/B59-601.004/shelling-out.html"]},{"id":"B59-602.001","title":"Competitive Money, Inside and Out","author":"Lawrence H. White","date":"1983-03-01","type":"writing","description":"White's Cato Journal article distinguishing competition in \"inside\" money (privately issued redeemable claims, such as bank notes and deposits) from competition in \"outside\" money (rival base monies, such as Hayek's proposed private fiat currencies). Argues that historical free-banking systems delivered the benefits of monetary competition while retaining a common unit of account, and that this is the more workable route to competitive money.","citation":"Lawrence H. White, \"Competitive Money, Inside and Out,\" Cato Journal, Vol. 3, No. 1 (Spring 1983), pp. 281-299.","provenance":"Published in the Cato Journal, Vol. 3, No. 1 (1983). PDF fetched with curl from Cato's own serials archive (cato.org/sites/cato.org/files/serials/files/cato-journal/1983/5/cj3n1-16.pdf) and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF.","hash":"16dd41e60c27fa466da4b9f00d4502293ef3324a7a6e615bfbecdbeeea572bd1","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","lawrence-white","competitive-money","inside-money","unit-of-account","cato","1983"],"excerpt":"It is possible to support deregulation of inside money without necessarily questioning the government position as sole producer of outside money. It is also possible to favor a system of privately produced outside money, for example a specie standard, without questioning bank regulation.","files":[{"path":"competitive-money-inside-and-out.pdf","size":910518}],"content_path":"content/B59-602.001","local":["content/B59-602.001/competitive-money-inside-and-out.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-602.002","title":"The Case for Free Banking: Then and Now","author":"George Selgin","date":"1985-10-21","type":"writing","description":"Selgin's 1985 Cato Policy Analysis making the case for free banking: freezing the monetary base and restoring the right of commercial banks to issue their own notes redeemable in base money, so that the money supply adjusts to demand through competitive note issue rather than central-bank discretion. Surveys the historical free-banking record (Scotland, Canada, Sweden) against the caricature of \"wildcat\" banking.","citation":"George A. Selgin, \"The Case for Free Banking: Then and Now,\" Policy Analysis No. 60, Cato Institute, Washington, DC, 21 October 1985.","provenance":"Published by the Cato Institute as Policy Analysis No. 60 (1985). PDF fetched with curl from Cato's own archive (cato.org/sites/cato.org/files/pubs/pdf/pa060.pdf) and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF.","hash":"831367326372f8503574e273f8c26368da12a00cee09790f9c7aef308931aaba","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","george-selgin","competitive-note-issue","austrian-economics","central-banking","cato","1985"],"excerpt":"Specifically, this third alternative calls for freezing the monetary base, thus removing the Federal Reserve from the business of money production, after restoring the right of commercial banks to issue their own notes, redeemable on demand in Federal Reserve dollars.","files":[{"path":"case-for-free-banking-then-and-now.pdf","size":57040}],"content_path":"content/B59-602.002","local":["content/B59-602.002/case-for-free-banking-then-and-now.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-602.003","title":"The Theory of Free Banking: Money Supply under Competitive Note Issue","author":"George Selgin","date":"1988-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Selgin's 1988 book, the fullest modern statement of free-banking monetary theory. Develops the argument that a system of competitive, private, redeemable note issue would stabilize the flow of nominal spending (MV) automatically through the mechanism of adverse clearings, without a central bank. Foreword by Lawrence H. White.","citation":"George A. Selgin, The Theory of Free Banking: Money Supply under Competitive Note Issue (Totowa, NJ: Rowman & Littlefield / Cato Institute, 1988).","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. Published 1988 by Rowman & Littlefield in association with the Cato Institute, and still under copyright. Liberty Fund's Online Library of Liberty hosts a free electronic edition with permission (oll.libertyfund.org); this record links there rather than hosting a copy.","hash":"","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","george-selgin","competitive-note-issue","adverse-clearings","austrian-economics","lawrence-white","external-reference","1988"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/white-the-theory-of-free-banking-money-supply-under-competitive-note-issue"},{"id":"B59-602.004","title":"The Rule of Law or the Rule of Central Bankers?","author":"Lawrence H. White","date":"2010-09-01","type":"writing","description":"White's Cato Journal essay applying the classical-liberal principle of the rule of law (general, predictable rules rather than discretionary authority) to monetary policy. Argues that a discretionary central bank is to monetary affairs what arbitrary government is to law generally, and surveys rule-based alternatives including a monetary-base freeze, NGDP-path rules, and free banking.","citation":"Lawrence H. White, \"The Rule of Law or the Rule of Central Bankers?,\" Cato Journal, Vol. 30, No. 3 (Fall 2010), pp. 451-463.","provenance":"Published in the Cato Journal, Vol. 30, No. 3 (2010). PDF fetched with curl from Cato's own serials archive (cato.org/sites/cato.org/files/serials/files/cato-journal/2010/11/cj30n3-3.pdf) and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF.","hash":"dfb2c65d741682f1f397954dddebb416dfabc407955348bde0202380690b1bf1","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","lawrence-white","rule-of-law","central-banking","monetary-rules","cato","2010"],"excerpt":"Economists often prescribe that countries seeking economic development should embrace the principle of the rule of law. I want to suggest that we listen to our own advice and apply it to our monetary and financial system.","files":[{"path":"rule-of-law-or-rule-of-central-bankers.pdf","size":110101}],"content_path":"content/B59-602.004","local":["content/B59-602.004/rule-of-law-or-rule-of-central-bankers.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-603.001","title":"Choice in Currency: A Way to Stop Inflation","author":"Friedrich A. Hayek","date":"1976-02-01","type":"writing","description":"Hayek's 1976 lecture and Institute of Economic Affairs occasional paper proposing that governments abandon their monopoly on money and allow citizens to freely use and refuse competing currencies. The threat of currency substitution, Hayek argues, is the only discipline that would force governments and central banks to keep the value of money stable. The pamphlet that led directly to Denationalisation of Money later the same year.","citation":"F. A. Hayek, \"Choice in Currency: A Way to Stop Inflation,\" Occasional Paper 48, Institute of Economic Affairs, London, February 1976.","provenance":"Originally published by the Institute of Economic Affairs (IEA), London, 1976, as Occasional Paper 48. This copy is the freely distributed PDF reissue hosted by the Mises Institute (cdn.mises.org), fetched with curl and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF. NOTE: the underlying work remains under copyright; see the batch manifest's copyright-reconciliation note.","hash":"2668a167ca6900d84c877349ae56340f5b716f52f4f8bc720e94ec8e860258a9","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","austrian-economics","friedrich-hayek","currency-competition","inflation","denationalisation-of-money","1976"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"choice-in-currency.pdf","size":9945718}],"content_path":"content/B59-603.001","local":["content/B59-603.001/choice-in-currency.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-603.002","title":"Denationalisation of Money: The Argument Refined","author":"Friedrich A. Hayek","date":"1976-10-01","type":"writing","description":"Hayek's book-length case for abolishing the government monopoly on the issue of money and letting private institutions compete to supply currencies, with the market selecting the most stable. Published by the Institute of Economic Affairs as Hobart Paper Special 70 (1976; substantially revised 2nd edition 1978). One of the most cited theoretical antecedents of private digital currencies and stablecoins.","citation":"F. A. Hayek, Denationalisation of Money: The Argument Refined, Hobart Paper Special 70, Institute of Economic Affairs, London, 1976 (2nd ed. 1978).","provenance":"Copyrighted; linked, not archived. Published by the Institute of Economic Affairs (IEA), London, 1976, and still under copyright. The IEA distributes a free PDF from its publications page (iea.org.uk/publications/research/denationalisation-of-money); this record links there rather than hosting a copy.","hash":"","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","austrian-economics","friedrich-hayek","currency-competition","denationalisation-of-money","external-reference","1976"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://iea.org.uk/publications/research/denationalisation-of-money"},{"id":"B59-603.003","title":"The Technology Revolution and Monetary Evolution","author":"Lawrence H. White","date":"1996-05-23","type":"writing","description":"White's short contribution to the Cato conference volume The Future of Money in the Information Age (1997). Places \"digital money\" in the long history of privately issued, redeemable payment media, argues that the deposit-transfer mechanism has been evolving for eight centuries, and predicts that cheap electronic funds transfer will drive retail banking toward lightly regulated offshore providers.","citation":"Lawrence H. White, \"The Technology Revolution and Monetary Evolution,\" in James A. Dorn, ed., The Future of Money in the Information Age (Washington, DC: Cato Institute, 1997), pp. 15-20.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the Cato Institute page (cato.org/pubs/books/money/money2.html) via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine raw snapshot of 16 February 2006 (the page is no longer served live). Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"8385a8844974749334750b550aefdbffcc07436f2aeb1de9440346b706aeca88","tags":["free-banking","monetary-theory","lawrence-white","digital-money","competitive-money","offshore-banking","cato","1996"],"excerpt":"Digital money -- spendable balances represented solely by digits on a bank's balance sheet -- is not new. What has been changing over the centuries is the usual method of authorizing the transfer of balances from one account to another.","files":[{"path":"technology-revolution-and-monetary-evolution.html","size":15731,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"technology-revolution-and-monetary-evolution-source.html","size":32180,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-603.003","local":["content/B59-603.003/technology-revolution-and-monetary-evolution.html"]},{"id":"B59-603.004","title":"Synthetic Commodity Money","author":"George Selgin","date":"2013-04-10","type":"writing","description":"Selgin's paper introducing the category of \"synthetic commodity money\": money that, like fiat money, has no non-monetary use value, but that, like commodity money, is absolutely (not merely contingently) scarce because its quantity is fixed by a rule beyond any issuer's discretion. Bitcoin is the leading example; the \"Swiss dinar\" that circulated in northern Iraq is another. Written as a working paper in 2012-13 and published in the Journal of Financial Stability, Vol. 17 (2015), pp. 92-99.","citation":"George Selgin, \"Synthetic Commodity Money,\" Journal of Financial Stability, Vol. 17 (2015), pp. 92-99 (working paper, 2013).","provenance":"Author's working-paper version, hosted by the Cato Institute (cato.org/sites/cato.org/files/articles/synthetic-commodity-money.pdf), fetched with curl and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF. The later published version appeared in the Journal of Financial Stability (2015).","hash":"3755809fa7ad244b2360eb858a687aa6e0099bfa979e0075a91738a4f4dd4de1","tags":["synthetic-commodity-money","monetary-theory","free-banking","george-selgin","bitcoin","commodity-money","fiat-money","austrian-economics"],"excerpt":"Such money consists of objects which, though lacking nonmonetary value, are absolutely rather than contingently scarce. More generally a synthetic commodity money is money that lacks nonmonetary value but is nevertheless reproducible only at a positive and rising marginal production cost, if indeed it can be reproduced at any cost at all.","files":[{"path":"synthetic-commodity-money.pdf","size":235661}],"content_path":"content/B59-603.004","local":["content/B59-603.004/synthetic-commodity-money.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-603.005","title":"Bitcoin: Problems and Prospects","author":"George Selgin","date":"2014-10-11","type":"writing","description":"Selgin's talk prepared for Hillsdale College's Free Market Forum, October 2014, assessing Bitcoin as a monetary economist. Argues that Bitcoin as it stands is not yet money by the standard definition and, because of network effects favouring the incumbent dollar, is unlikely to displace it; but that a modified protocol, one that made the supply respond to demand or targeted a nominal-income path rather than a fixed quantity, \"could be very good money indeed.\" An early, careful statement of the demand-elasticity critique of fixed-supply cryptocurrency.","citation":"George Selgin, \"Bitcoin: Problems and Prospects,\" prepared for the Free Market Forum, Hillsdale College, October 2014.","provenance":"Talk paper hosted by Hillsdale College (hillsdale.edu), fetched with curl and archived unmodified. SHA-256 is computed over that PDF.","hash":"b14a8af42dca8f1bedcb28befec64918e3f5438b13dff605d377ceb83dc3d59b","tags":["synthetic-commodity-money","monetary-theory","free-banking","george-selgin","bitcoin","cryptocurrency","deflation","nominal-income-targeting"],"excerpt":"Despite their name, Bitcoins aren't coins. They are, instead, a digital payment medium.","files":[{"path":"bitcoin-problems-and-prospects.pdf","size":134931}],"content_path":"content/B59-603.005","local":["content/B59-603.005/bitcoin-problems-and-prospects.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-604.001","title":"Release 1.0, Issue 91-11 -- Phil Salin on Opportunity Costs","author":"Esther Dyson (editor), Phil Salin (contributor)","date":"1991-11-25","type":"writing","description":"Issue of Release 1.0, Esther Dyson's influential monthly technology newsletter, dated November 25, 1991. Features Phil Salin's essay \"Costs Are in the Eyes of the Beholders\" on opportunity costs and how information technology changes cost assessment. Salin's bio notes he founded Arc Technologies/StarStruck (the first privately financed rocket launch attempt), worked on the AT&T breakup, and founded the American Information Exchange (AMIX), a subsidiary of Autodesk. The lead article covers Lotus \"Chronicle\" scenario-management features.","citation":"Dyson, E. (Ed.). (1991, November 25). Release 1.0, Issue 91-11. EDventure Holdings. Featuring Salin, P. \"Costs Are in the Eyes of the Beholders.\"","provenance":"PDF scan digitized by Jose Gomez (per PDF metadata). Original publication: Release 1.0, EDventure Holdings, November 1991. Phil Salin's essay appears on pages 4-5. Salin passed away in June 1991; this publication is posthumous, indicating the essay was written earlier.","hash":"00140440cbff203ed1e55c733e74df60701127fbf4e995777e9db9852de83636","tags":["phil-salin","esther-dyson","release-1-0","amix","economics","newsletter","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"release-1.0-nov-1991.pdf","size":1796409,"label":"Read Newsletter (PDF)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-604.001","local":["content/B59-604.001/release-1.0-nov-1991.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-604.002","title":"Introduction to BlackNet","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1993-08-18","type":"writing","description":"The BlackNet prospectus: a proof-of-concept for an anonymous information market, written by May in mid-1993 for a Palo Alto nanotechnology discussion group. Presented in the voice of a fictional organisation that buys and sells information of any kind using public-key cryptography, anonymous remailers, and message pools, with payment by untraceable digital cash or internal \"CryptoCredits.\" A list member leaked it by remailer to the Cypherpunks list in August 1993, from where L. Detweiler reposted it to dozens of Usenet groups; May acknowledged authorship days later.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"Introduction to BlackNet,\" written mid-1993; leaked to the Cypherpunks mailing list c. 18 August 1993 and widely reposted to Usenet.","provenance":"Plain-text snapshot fetched with curl from the MIT 6.805 \"Ethics and Law on the Electronic Frontier\" course materials archive (groups.csail.mit.edu/mac/classes/6.805/articles/crypto/cypherpunks/blacknet.txt), a stable academic mirror. Archived unmodified; SHA-256 is computed over that snapshot. The PGP public key block that closed the original circulated separately and is not present in this copy. Cross-referenced against May's own 1994 account of the experiment, archived here as B59-604.003.","hash":"5743bf37bdb8755b43468287129dc21a385ec1fed784099277175536aca82ca2","tags":["crypto-anarchy","cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","information-markets","anonymous-communication","1993"],"excerpt":"Your name has come to our attention. We have reason to believe you may be interested in the products and services our new organization, BlackNet, has to offer.","files":[{"path":"introduction-to-blacknet.html","size":7644,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"introduction-to-blacknet-source.txt","size":3810,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-604.002","local":["content/B59-604.002/introduction-to-blacknet.html"]},{"id":"B59-604.003","title":"BlackNet — the Truth","author":"Timothy C. May","date":"1994-04-19","type":"writing","description":"May's own account of the BlackNet experiment, posted to the Cypherpunks list in April 1994 in response to recurring questions about whether BlackNet was real. He lays out the six-point story: that he wrote it as a proof of principle, anonymously mailed it to expected attendees, generated the real key, that someone else leaked it to the list, that he confessed within days, and that \"the parts work\" even though no market sat behind it.","citation":"Timothy C. May, \"BlackNet--the Truth,\" message to cypherpunks@toad.com, 19 April 1994.","provenance":"Snapshot of the archived message page fetched with curl from mailing-list-archive.cryptoanarchy.wiki. The page carries the full raw message; SHA-256 is computed over the fetched page. The reader page reproduces the raw message body verbatim.","hash":"cbebc3b995bffaefae39fde6bfa619ac6e3828394f854707f158ba033dfc45bf","tags":["crypto-anarchy","cypherpunks","tim-may","primary-source","information-markets","mailing-list","1994"],"excerpt":"I authored BlackNet last summer, as an experiment, and as a \"proof of principle\" for a discussion at a Palo Alto nanotechnology group.","files":[{"path":"blacknet-the-truth.html","size":6547,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"blacknet-the-truth-source.html","size":11945,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-604.003","local":["content/B59-604.003/blacknet-the-truth.html"]},{"id":"B59-604.004","title":"Privacy Marketing","author":"Nick Szabo","date":"1999-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Szabo's essay on the commercial and strategic incentives for businesses to offer privacy-preserving services. Argues that privacy can be a competitive differentiator in markets and explores how cryptographic tools can enable privacy-respecting marketing models that avoid the surveillance economy.","citation":"Szabo, N. (c. 1999). Privacy Marketing. Retrieved from https://www.fon.hum.uva.nl/rob/Courses/InformationInSpeech/CDROM/Literature/LOTwinterschool2006/szabo.best.vwh.net/privacy_marketing.html.","provenance":"Retrieved from the University of Amsterdam mirror of szabo.best.vwh.net. Original filename: privacy_marketing.html.","hash":"c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d4e5f6a1b2c3d5","tags":["nick-szabo","primary-source","privacy","digital-markets","cryptography","szabo-best-vwh-net"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"label":"Read Essay","path":"privacy-marketing.html","entry":true,"size":10294}],"content_path":"content/B59-604.004","local":["content/B59-604.004/privacy-marketing.html"]},{"id":"B59-604.005","title":"Frozen Heads and Future Fortunes: What Is the Cost of Digital Information?","author":"Finn Brunton (BBC Science Focus)","date":"2019-08-31","type":"writing","description":"BBC Science Focus essay by Finn Brunton telling the story of economist Phil Salin -- founder of the early online marketplace AMIX -- whose life and death intertwined sugar-based 'rocket candy', digital information exchanges, and cryonic preservation. The piece explores the long-running cypherpunk and extropian fascination with information, value, and preservation of the self, themes central to the milieu that produced Hal Finney and the first experiments in digital cash.","citation":"Brunton, F. (2019, August 31). 'Frozen heads and future fortunes: what is the cost of digital information?' BBC Science Focus Magazine. https://www.sciencefocus.com/future-technology/frozen-heads-and-future-fortunes-what-is-the-cost-of-digital-information","provenance":"MHT web archive of the BBC Science Focus Magazine article. Preserved as a secondary source on cryonics, digital-information markets, and the extropian context surrounding Project B59's subjects.","hash":"07b87586ac9f06388629d52b92f989f57dc2ec9234e716ac8e5d8bf67756eebc","tags":["phil-salin","cryonics","extropians","digital-cash","amix","finn-brunton","bbc-science-focus"],"excerpt":"Finn Brunton tells the story of economist Phil Salin, a man whose life (and death) involved sugar-based 'rocket candy', digital information exchanges, and frozen heads.","files":[{"path":"frozen-heads-and-future-fortunes.html","size":6602677,"label":"Read Article (MHT Archive)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-604.005","local":["content/B59-604.005/frozen-heads-and-future-fortunes.html"]},{"id":"B59-701.001","title":"Transhumanism: Toward a Futurist Philosophy","author":"Max More","date":"1990-01-01","type":"writing","description":"The essay that introduced \"transhumanism\" in its modern sense, first published in Extropy #6 (1990) and revised through 1996. More frames transhumanism as a secular \"eupraxophy\" (a practical philosophy of life) oriented toward self-directed evolution beyond the human condition, and contrasts it with religious thinking, humanism, and nihilism.","citation":"Max More, \"Transhumanism: Toward a Futurist Philosophy,\" Extropy #6 (Summer 1990); revised 1994 and 1996.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from maxmore.com/transhum.htm via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine raw snapshot of 8 December 2000 (the page is no longer served live). Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"ece84a14f795235146514fa31d0f38295270d2848c185d0324e2f077a6a626c1","tags":["extropians","transhumanism","max-more","eupraxophy","self-transformation","futurism","primary-source","1990"],"excerpt":"Humanity is in the early stages of a period of explosive expansion in knowledge, freedom, intelligence, lifespan, and wisdom.","files":[{"path":"transhumanism-toward-a-futurist-philosophy.html","size":32687,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"transhumanism-toward-a-futurist-philosophy-source.html","size":31594,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-701.001","local":["content/B59-701.001/transhumanism-toward-a-futurist-philosophy.html"]},{"id":"B59-701.002","title":"The Extropian Principles, Version 2.6","author":"Max More","date":"1995-01-01","type":"writing","description":"An earlier version of the Extropian Principles, superseded by 3.0 (1998) and 3.11 (2003) but valuable for showing the philosophy in its mid-1990s form, when the Extropy Institute lists overlapped heavily with the cypherpunks. This version names the principles Boundless Expansion, Self-Transformation, Dynamic Optimism, Intelligent Technology, and Spontaneous Order.","citation":"Max More, \"The Extropian Principles, Version 2.6,\" Extropy Institute, 1995.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from maxmore.com/extprn26.htm via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine raw snapshot of 18 January 2000 (the page is no longer served live). Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot. The roster brief requested the 1993 \"2.5\" text; version 2.6 (1995) is the earliest preserved in the Internet Archive.","hash":"24f38f7c7d8fed7c3cde5cf073f9daee0585c711ed7e296285028814a5c9ae90","tags":["extropians","transhumanism","max-more","extropy-institute","dynamic-optimism","spontaneous-order","primary-source","1995"],"excerpt":"EXTROPY: A measure of intelligence, information, energy, vitality, experience, diversity, opportunity, and growth. EXTROPIANISM: The philosophy that seeks to increase extropy.","files":[{"path":"extropian-principles-2.6.html","size":26343,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"extropian-principles-2.6-source.html","size":25233,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-701.002","local":["content/B59-701.002/extropian-principles-2.6.html"]},{"id":"B59-701.003","title":"The Principles of Extropy, Version 3.11","author":"Max More","date":"2003-09-20","type":"writing","description":"The canonical statement of Extropian philosophy, revised by Max More across five versions between 1990 and 2003. Version 3.11 is the last, setting out seven principles: Perpetual Progress, Self-Transformation, Practical Optimism, Intelligent Technology, Open Society (information and democracy), Self-Direction, and Rational Thinking. The Extropy Institute mailing lists were an important crossover point between transhumanism, cryptography, and digital cash.","citation":"Max More, \"The Principles of Extropy, Version 3.11,\" Extropy Institute, September 2003.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from extropy.org/principles.htm via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine raw snapshot of 21 July 2005 (the page is no longer served live). Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"38579a8bb6229ffe61fe14b18b4fc2c1b0da0cc53a03b01f19e6c70f539f1a6f","tags":["extropians","transhumanism","max-more","extropy-institute","open-society","spontaneous-order","primary-source","2003"],"excerpt":"Extropy means seeking more intelligence, wisdom, and effectiveness, an open-ended lifespan, and the removal of political, cultural, biological, and psychological limits to continuing development.","files":[{"path":"principles-of-extropy.html","size":37795,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"principles-of-extropy-source.html","size":54719,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-701.003","local":["content/B59-701.003/principles-of-extropy.html"]},{"id":"B59-703.001","title":"Dying Outside","author":"Hal Finney","date":"2009-10-04","type":"writing","description":"Hal Finney's deeply personal essay on LessWrong reflecting on his ALS diagnosis, his progressive symptoms, his decision to pursue cryopreservation with the Alcor Life Extension Foundation, and his philosophical acceptance of mortality. Written through the parable of a man told he has a fatal disease who resolves to live fully anyway. One of the last major writings by the cryptographer who was the first person to receive a Bitcoin transaction and the original programmer of PGP 2.0. Finney passed away on August 28, 2014.","citation":"Finney, H. (2009, October 4). \"Dying Outside.\" Published on LessWrong. Retrieved from lesswrong.com/posts/bshZiaLefDejvPKuS/dying-outside.","provenance":"Retrieved from LessWrong. Published under Finney's own account (halfinney). Content verified against the original LessWrong post URL. Finney was diagnosed with ALS in August 2009 and wrote this essay approximately two months later.","hash":"3f2c80feec864c9ef9f266e1e8bbd329aea36c3fbece9b216846f6c2b3baf0e8","tags":["hal-finney","als","cryonics","mortality","personal","lesswrong","primary-source"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"dying-outside.html","size":538450,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"dying-outside.mht","size":982432,"label":"Original MHT Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-703.001","local":["content/B59-703.001/dying-outside.html"]},{"id":"B59-703.002","title":"Bitcoin's Earliest Adopter Is Cryonically Freezing His Body to See the Future","author":"Andy Greenberg (WIRED)","date":"2014-08-29","type":"writing","description":"WIRED feature by Andy Greenberg on Hal Finney -- recipient of the first Bitcoin transaction and one of Bitcoin's earliest contributors -- written shortly after his death from ALS in August 2014. The article recounts Finney's decision to be cryonically preserved by the Alcor Life Extension Foundation, his wife Fran's reflections on his lifelong optimism about technology and the future, and his hope that scientific advances might one day allow his revival.","citation":"Greenberg, A. (2014, August 29). 'Bitcoin's Earliest Adopter Is Cryonically Freezing His Body to See the Future.' WIRED. https://www.wired.com/2014/08/hal-finney/","provenance":"MHT web archive of the WIRED article (wired.com/2014/08/hal-finney). Preserved as a secondary-source profile documenting Hal Finney's cryopreservation at Alcor.","hash":"f1522ecc4be23b2676b2d0fa5e39eac99cbb47a5071a8b987573399d35d5f38b","tags":["hal-finney","cryonics","alcor","bitcoin","als","wired","andy-greenberg","obituary"],"excerpt":"He's always been optimistic about the future, says Hal Finney's wife, Fran. Every new advance, he embraced it, every new technology. Hal relished life, and he made the most of everything.","files":[{"path":"bitcoins-earliest-adopter-cryonics.html","size":2043732,"label":"Read Article (MHT Archive)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-703.002","local":["content/B59-703.002/bitcoins-earliest-adopter-cryonics.html"]},{"id":"B59-703.003","title":"Cryonics Magazine -- Member Profile: Hal Finney (Vol 40, Issue 2)","author":"Alcor Life Extension Foundation","date":"2019-06-14","type":"writing","description":"Second-quarter 2019 issue of Cryonics, the magazine of the Alcor Life Extension Foundation (Vol 40, Issue 2), featuring a special post-cryopreservation Member Profile of Hal Finney. Marking Bitcoin's tenth anniversary and Finney's historic 'Running bitcoin' tweet, the profile honors the cryptographer, cypherpunk, PGP developer, anonymous-remailer pioneer, early console-game programmer, and first recipient of a Bitcoin transaction, and was created with the support of his wife and children. The 60-page issue also includes Case Report A-1990 and an essay on Victor Frankenstein.","citation":"Alcor Life Extension Foundation. (2019). 'Member Profile: Hal Finney.' Cryonics, 40(2), 2nd Quarter 2019. https://www.alcor.org","provenance":"PDF of Cryonics magazine, 2nd Quarter 2019 (Vol 40, Issue 2), published by the Alcor Life Extension Foundation (60 pages; file produced 14 June 2019). Preserved as a primary-source biographical profile of Hal Finney.","hash":"8e5c4b0f06e10c4d90271027b59cc1f1650d3f1e6af42afe940bbfa3d9d10378","tags":["hal-finney","cryonics","alcor","bitcoin","pgp","member-profile","extropians"],"excerpt":null,"files":[{"path":"cryonics-magazine-hal-finney-profile.pdf","size":2937454,"label":"Read Magazine (PDF)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-703.003","local":["content/B59-703.003/cryonics-magazine-hal-finney-profile.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-801.001","title":"A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity","author":"Warren S. McCulloch & Walter Pitts","date":"1943-12-01","type":"writing","description":"The founding paper of computational neuroscience and neural networks. McCulloch and Pitts model the neuron as a binary threshold logic unit and prove that networks of such units can compute any expression of the propositional calculus. It gave von Neumann's computer design and every later artificial neural network their basic vocabulary of units, weights, thresholds and layers.","citation":"Warren S. McCulloch and Walter Pitts, \"A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity,\" Bulletin of Mathematical Biophysics 5 (1943): 115-133.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the original 1943 Bulletin of Mathematical Biophysics printing, mirrored from a CSU Long Beach course page (home.csulb.edu/~cwallis). A re-typeset 1990 Pergamon reprint (Bull. Math. Biol. 52:99-115) also circulates; the 1943 original was preferred. Text of title, authors and journal header verified against the extracted document text. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"f977a9c0278dacf7b45dd144c089eb363a955e905ffef1fdb96ba0622f311151","tags":["mcculloch-pitts","neural-networks","threshold-logic","foundations","1943","computational-neuroscience","primary-source","ai-history"],"excerpt":"\"For any logical expression satisfying certain conditions, one can find a net behaving in the fashion it describes.\"","files":[{"path":"logical-calculus-nervous-activity.html","size":3935,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"logical-calculus-nervous-activity-source.pdf","size":1032371,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-801.001","local":["content/B59-801.001/logical-calculus-nervous-activity.html"]},{"id":"B59-801.002","title":"As We May Think","author":"Vannevar Bush","date":"1945-07-01","type":"writing","description":"Vannevar Bush's 1945 Atlantic essay proposing the 'memex', a personal machine that stores a microfilm library and lets its owner build permanent associative trails between documents. It argued that knowledge should be organised by association rather than hierarchy, and directly inspired Engelbart's work, Nelson's hypertext and the web. For AI it set the lasting frame of the machine as an amplifier of human intellect.","citation":"Vannevar Bush, \"As We May Think,\" The Atlantic Monthly, July 1945, pp. 101-108.","provenance":"Magazine essay, hosted (not a book). Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the full-text HTML transcription in the W3C history archive (www.w3.org/History/1945/vbush/vbush-all.shtml), a long-standing institutional copy. Content cross-checked: 8 numbered sections, memex described in section 7, opens \"This has not been a scientist's war\" and closes \"to lose hope as to the outcome.\" SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"78e073fc0c2a26d85dcac2023e1d175fbd0bdcd3d5849b94fed42700c785c846","tags":["vannevar-bush","memex","hypertext","information-retrieval","1945","the-atlantic","foundations","intelligence-augmentation","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"It is an enlarged intimate supplement to his memory.\"","files":[{"path":"as-we-may-think.html","size":3743,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"as-we-may-think-source.html","size":49961,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-801.002","local":["content/B59-801.002/as-we-may-think.html"]},{"id":"B59-801.003","title":"Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal and the Machine","author":"Norbert Wiener","date":"1948-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Wiener's book naming and defining cybernetics: the unified study of control and communication through feedback in machines, organisms and societies. Its treatment of feedback, prediction, filtering and self-regulating systems shaped early AI, control theory and the framing of the brain as an information machine. Full-length commercial book, still in print from MIT Press.","citation":"Norbert Wiener, Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal and the Machine (Paris: Hermann & Cie; Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1948).","provenance":"Copyrighted full-length book; linked, not archived. Reference record: citation and publisher link only, no local snapshot and no hash. Publisher page verified 2026-08-31.","hash":"","tags":["norbert-wiener","cybernetics","feedback","control-theory","1948","foundations","external-reference","link-only"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262730099/cybernetics/","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-801.004","title":"A Mathematical Theory of Communication","author":"Claude E. Shannon","date":"1948-07-01","type":"writing","description":"Shannon's founding paper of information theory. It defines information as the resolution of uncertainty, introduces the bit, and proves that communication can be made error-free up to a fixed capacity determined by noise. Its central quantity, entropy, recurs throughout machine learning as cross-entropy loss, information gain, minimum description length and mutual-information objectives.","citation":"C. E. Shannon, \"A Mathematical Theory of Communication,\" Bell System Technical Journal 27 (1948): 379-423, 623-656.","provenance":"Journal paper, released to the public domain by Nokia Bell Labs; hosted. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the widely cited 55-page reprint mirrored from the Harvard mathematics department (people.math.harvard.edu/~ctm). Page count (55) and section structure verified against the extracted document. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"6e4e3411984f3edf99dbfe8b941cb5e8a321379ff0cae6ae5c1f592ad8882ca8","tags":["claude-shannon","information-theory","entropy","bits","bell-labs","1948","foundations","coding-theory","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"The fundamental problem of communication is that of reproducing at one point … a message selected at another point.\"","files":[{"path":"mathematical-theory-of-communication.html","size":3560,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mathematical-theory-of-communication-source.pdf","size":366296,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-801.004","local":["content/B59-801.004/mathematical-theory-of-communication.html"]},{"id":"B59-801.005","title":"Computing Machinery and Intelligence","author":"A. M. Turing","date":"1950-10-01","type":"writing","description":"Turing's paper introducing the imitation game, now called the Turing test: judge a machine's intelligence by whether an interrogator can distinguish its typed answers from a human's. It replaces the question 'can machines think' with an operational trial, answers nine standing objections, and closes by arguing for building a 'child machine' that learns rather than hand-coding an adult mind.","citation":"A. M. Turing, \"Computing Machinery and Intelligence,\" Mind LIX, no. 236 (1950): 433-460.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; free copy preferred over the paywalled Oxford University Press version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot mirrored from a UMBC course page (csee.umbc.edu/courses/471/papers/turing.pdf). Full text verified against the extracted document: opens with the imitation game, journal line \"Mind 49: 433-460\" present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"3cd7c57944b7b53f18fb2a7c014fe2367f3576696a1d024c840151459da2f24e","tags":["alan-turing","turing-test","imitation-game","philosophy-of-mind","1950","mind-journal","foundations","learning-machines","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"We may hope that machines will eventually compete with men in all purely intellectual fields.\"","files":[{"path":"computing-machinery-and-intelligence.html","size":3443,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"computing-machinery-and-intelligence-source.pdf","size":91972,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-801.005","local":["content/B59-801.005/computing-machinery-and-intelligence.html"]},{"id":"B59-801.006","title":"A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence","author":"John McCarthy, Marvin L. Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester & Claude E. Shannon","date":"1955-08-31","type":"writing","description":"The 1955 proposal that named the field of artificial intelligence and set its agenda. In a single paragraph it states the founding conjecture, that intelligence can be described precisely enough for a machine to simulate it, then lists seven problem areas (computers, language, neuron nets, complexity, self-improvement, abstraction, creativity) that remain active today. The 1956 workshop it funded is treated as AI's founding event.","citation":"J. McCarthy, M. L. Minsky, N. Rochester, C. E. Shannon, \"A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence,\" August 31, 1955. Reprinted in AI Magazine 27, no. 4 (2006): 12-14.","provenance":"Short proposal document, hosted; institutional copy preferred. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the AAAI AI Magazine scholarly reprint (ojs.aaai.org/aimagazine, Winter 2006), which reproduces the core proposal and the proposers' statements in full. Text verified: the \"2 month, 10 man study\" sentence, the founding conjecture and all seven numbered problem areas are present. A scan of the fuller original 17-page typescript is held at raysolomonoff.com and by Dartmouth and Stanford archives. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"d0c4bf0b384707771d4caac5f81e9183d0beb0faf869d3b58c96cfa2da5d16cc","tags":["dartmouth","john-mccarthy","marvin-minsky","claude-shannon","nathaniel-rochester","1955","founding-document","foundations","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… every aspect of learning or any other feature of intelligence can in principle be so precisely described that a machine can be made to simulate it.\"","files":[{"path":"dartmouth-proposal.html","size":3967,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"dartmouth-proposal-source.pdf","size":199399,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-801.006","local":["content/B59-801.006/dartmouth-proposal.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.001","title":"The Perceptron: A Probabilistic Model for Information Storage and Organization in the Brain","author":"Frank Rosenblatt","date":"1958-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Rosenblatt's paper introducing the perceptron, the first machine that learns by adjusting connection weights in response to its own errors. It includes the perceptron convergence theorem: if the data is linearly separable, the rule finds a separating boundary in finite time. The single-layer limitation later documented by Minsky and Papert does not diminish that the error-driven weight update is the seed of nearly all modern learning.","citation":"F. Rosenblatt, \"The Perceptron: A Probabilistic Model for Information Storage and Organization in the Brain,\" Psychological Review 65, no. 6 (1958): 386-408.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; free copy preferred over the paywalled APA version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot mirrored from a UPenn course page (ling.upenn.edu/courses/cogs501). Verified against the extracted text: journal header \"Psychological Review Vol. 65, No. 6, 1958,\" title, author \"F. Rosenblatt, Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory,\" and opening sentence all present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"f32fdf8c0657cda33ace13487856dd4facd34c3eeb6a6d3011107339928fbd57","tags":["frank-rosenblatt","perceptron","neural-networks","supervised-learning","1958","psychological-review","primary-source","ai-history"],"excerpt":"\"… stored information takes the form of new connections, or transmission channels in the nervous system.\"","files":[{"path":"the-perceptron.html","size":3738,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"the-perceptron-source.pdf","size":1631543,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.001","local":["content/B59-802.001/the-perceptron.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.002","title":"Perceptrons: An Introduction to Computational Geometry","author":"Marvin Minsky & Seymour Papert","date":"1969-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Minsky and Papert's mathematical study of what single-layer perceptrons can and cannot compute. Its proofs that such networks cannot represent functions like parity or connectedness, and its scepticism about layered extensions, are widely credited with redirecting funding away from neural networks for over a decade. Full-length commercial book, still in print from MIT Press.","citation":"Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert, Perceptrons: An Introduction to Computational Geometry (Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1969; expanded edition 1988).","provenance":"Copyrighted full-length book; linked, not archived. Reference record: citation and publisher link only, no local snapshot and no hash. Publisher page verified 2026-08-31.","hash":"","tags":["marvin-minsky","seymour-papert","perceptrons","neural-networks","1969","ai-winter","external-reference","link-only"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262630221/perceptrons/","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-802.003","title":"Neural Networks and Physical Systems with Emergent Collective Computational Abilities","author":"John J. Hopfield","date":"1982-04-01","type":"writing","description":"Hopfield's paper showing that a network of simple symmetric units has stable states that behave as content-addressable memories: present a partial or noisy pattern and the network relaxes to the stored one, following an energy function downhill. It connected neural computation to statistical mechanics, made recurrent networks analysable, and its lineage runs to Boltzmann machines, modern Hopfield networks and the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics.","citation":"J. J. Hopfield, \"Neural Networks and Physical Systems with Emergent Collective Computational Abilities,\" PNAS 79, no. 8 (1982): 2554-2558.","provenance":"Journal paper (PNAS, now open access); hosted. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. pnas.org and PubMed Central both served proof-of-work/bot-blocks to automated fetch, so the snapshot is the identical PNAS/PMC scan file (pnas00447-0135.pdf) retrieved from a GitHub mirror; its content was verified against the published article (PNAS header \"Vol. 79, pp. 2554-2558, April 1982,\" title and abstract keywords all present). The Wayback Machine was unreachable from this environment at archiving time (repeated 429/503), so no Wayback capture was taken; the snapshot is the direct fetch noted above. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"1543e997fbbb49c3534dbff1cee399ab89c751bd1c98ac3cd3e7e5e22d5af926","tags":["john-hopfield","hopfield-network","associative-memory","recurrent-networks","energy-based-models","1982","pnas","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… can emerge as collective properties of systems having a large number of simple equivalent components.\"","files":[{"path":"hopfield-neural-networks.html","size":3724,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"hopfield-neural-networks-source.pdf","size":1180207,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.003","local":["content/B59-802.003/hopfield-neural-networks.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.004","title":"Learning Internal Representations by Error Propagation","author":"David E. Rumelhart, Geoffrey E. Hinton & Ronald J. Williams","date":"1985-09-01","type":"writing","description":"The technical-report version of the paper that made multilayer neural networks trainable, preferred here over the compressed 1986 Nature letter. Backpropagation uses the chain rule to send error gradients from the output back through hidden layers, ending the single-layer limitation. Its deeper claim is that the procedure discovers useful internal representations on its own, which is the foundation of representation learning.","citation":"D. E. Rumelhart, G. E. Hinton, R. J. Williams, \"Learning Internal Representations by Error Propagation,\" ICS Report 8506, Institute for Cognitive Science, UC San Diego, September 1985.","provenance":"Institutional technical report, cleared for public release (DTIC ADA164453); hosted. Task called for the ICS tech-report version rather than the Nature paper. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. DTIC (apps.dtic.mil) was down for maintenance, so the snapshot is the same document from the Internet Archive mirror (archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA164453). Verified: cover reads \"Learning Internal Representations by Error Propagation ... Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams ... September 1985 ... ICS Report 8506 ... Institute for Cognitive Science, University of California, San Diego.\" SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"c011017e805d52b8fd1ed7c3a071e48cbbb5759a37c78fc64de5fbe1e3336335","tags":["backpropagation","rumelhart","hinton","williams","representation-learning","neural-networks","1985","technical-report","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… a learning procedure capable of building internal representations which are adequate for the task at hand.\"","files":[{"path":"learning-internal-representations.html","size":3761,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"learning-internal-representations-source.pdf","size":3091652,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.004","local":["content/B59-802.004/learning-internal-representations.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.005","title":"Long Short-Term Memory","author":"Sepp Hochreiter & Jürgen Schmidhuber","date":"1997-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Hochreiter and Schmidhuber's paper introducing the LSTM recurrent network. A gated memory cell lets information pass forward unchanged until gates decide to write, read or erase it, so error signals survive hundreds of time steps instead of vanishing. LSTM was the default architecture for speech, translation and handwriting for about two decades, and its gating idea carries into GRUs, highway networks and residual connections.","citation":"S. Hochreiter and J. Schmidhuber, \"Long Short-Term Memory,\" Neural Computation 9, no. 8 (1997): 1735-1780.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; author/institutional copy preferred over the paywalled MIT Press version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot mirrored from the authors' group page at Johannes Kepler University Linz (bioinf.jku.at/publications/older/2604.pdf). Verified against the extracted text: title, \"Neural Computation 9(8):1735-1780, 1997,\" author \"Sepp\" Hochreiter and the abstract are present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"ceb9e53dbc0493f5b3bf5520ed940f3e6b526064d17b2118d77e51f79c0edcc6","tags":["lstm","hochreiter","schmidhuber","recurrent-networks","sequence-modeling","vanishing-gradient","1997","neural-computation","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"LSTM can learn to bridge time intervals in excess of 1000 steps …\"","files":[{"path":"long-short-term-memory.html","size":3590,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"long-short-term-memory-source.pdf","size":396938,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.005","local":["content/B59-802.005/long-short-term-memory.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.006","title":"Gradient-Based Learning Applied to Document Recognition","author":"Yann LeCun, Léon Bottou, Yoshua Bengio & Patrick Haffner","date":"1998-11-01","type":"writing","description":"LeCun and colleagues' definitive paper on convolutional neural networks. Local receptive fields, weight sharing and pooling let the LeNet-5 network read handwritten digits at production accuracy, once used on a large fraction of US bank cheques. The paper also argues for training whole recognition pipelines end to end by gradient descent, and LeNet is the direct ancestor of AlexNet and later image models.","citation":"Y. LeCun, L. Bottou, Y. Bengio, P. Haffner, \"Gradient-Based Learning Applied to Document Recognition,\" Proceedings of the IEEE 86, no. 11 (1998): 2278-2324.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; author copy preferred over the paywalled IEEE version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from the first author's publication page (yann.lecun.com/exdb/publis/pdf/lecun-98.pdf), the canonical distribution point. 46-page length matches Proc. IEEE 86(11):2278-2324; document fonts are custom-encoded so text was not machine-extractable, but size, page count and source identify it. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"948f557fd56bcd276ca7b74feaab1b18108319c13f9818083bebd3bfe2fd7bf4","tags":["yann-lecun","convolutional-networks","lenet","deep-learning","document-recognition","1998","proceedings-ieee","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… relying more on automatic learning and less on hand-designed heuristics.\"","files":[{"path":"gradient-based-learning.html","size":3555,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"gradient-based-learning-source.pdf","size":955058,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.006","local":["content/B59-802.006/gradient-based-learning.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.007","title":"ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks","author":"Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever & Geoffrey E. Hinton","date":"2012-12-03","type":"writing","description":"The AlexNet paper. A large convolutional network trained on two GPUs cut ImageNet top-5 error from roughly 26 to 16 percent in one year, a margin that moved computer vision to deep learning almost immediately. Its practical choices, ReLU activations, dropout and aggressive data augmentation, became standard, and it established the GPU-plus-large-dataset pattern that still defines the field.","citation":"A. Krizhevsky, I. Sutskever, G. E. Hinton, \"ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks,\" NeurIPS 25 (2012).","provenance":"Conference paper, hosted; official open-access proceedings copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from proceedings.neurips.cc (paper 2012, file c399862d3b9d6b76c8436e924a68c45b). Verified against the extracted text: title, all three authors and \"University of Toronto\" affiliations present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"90137160c57217953d5f61857e64ca58e85f06e1b13b4f475c918b1b582b9771","tags":["alexnet","krizhevsky","sutskever","hinton","imagenet","convolutional-networks","deep-learning","gpu","2012","neurips","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… record-breaking results on a highly challenging dataset using purely supervised learning.\"","files":[{"path":"imagenet-classification-deep-cnn.html","size":3579,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"imagenet-classification-deep-cnn-source.pdf","size":1418820,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.007","local":["content/B59-802.007/imagenet-classification-deep-cnn.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.008","title":"Generative Adversarial Nets","author":"Ian J. Goodfellow, Jean Pouget-Abadie, Mehdi Mirza, Bing Xu, David Warde-Farley, Sherjil Ozair, Aaron Courville & Yoshua Bengio","date":"2014-06-10","type":"writing","description":"Goodfellow and colleagues' paper introducing generative adversarial networks. A generator and a discriminator are trained against each other as a minimax game; at equilibrium the generator's samples match the data distribution, with no explicit likelihood required. GANs produced the sharpest synthetic images of the mid-2010s, seeded work on image translation and super-resolution, and made deepfakes a public issue.","citation":"I. J. Goodfellow et al., \"Generative Adversarial Nets,\" arXiv:1406.2661 (2014); NeurIPS 27 (2014).","provenance":"Preprint (also a NeurIPS 2014 paper), hosted; arXiv copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from arxiv.org/pdf/1406.2661 (v1, 10 June 2014). arXiv identifier present in the document; author list and abstract match the published version. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"ff5819e3a7b713c3bd3107b7de3d51fe0a347aa5d8444f0efdcf2345ef0a8b63","tags":["gan","goodfellow","bengio","generative-models","adversarial-training","deep-learning","2014","arxiv","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… a new framework for estimating generative models via an adversarial process …\"","files":[{"path":"generative-adversarial-nets.html","size":3476,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"generative-adversarial-nets-source.pdf","size":530482,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.008","local":["content/B59-802.008/generative-adversarial-nets.html"]},{"id":"B59-802.009","title":"Attention Is All You Need","author":"Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N. Gomez, Łukasz Kaiser & Illia Polosukhin","date":"2017-06-12","type":"writing","description":"Vaswani and colleagues' paper introducing the Transformer, an architecture built entirely on self-attention with no recurrence or convolution. Every position attends to every other in a single parallel step, turning sequence training into large matrix multiplications suited to modern hardware. It is the architecture beneath GPT, BERT and essentially every large language model that followed.","citation":"A. Vaswani et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" arXiv:1706.03762 (2017); NeurIPS 30 (2017).","provenance":"Preprint (also a NeurIPS 2017 paper), hosted; arXiv copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from arxiv.org/pdf/1706.03762. arXiv identifier present in the document; title, author list and abstract match the published version. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"bdfaa68d8984f0dc02beaca527b76f207d99b666d31d1da728ee0728182df697","tags":["transformer","attention","vaswani","self-attention","large-language-models","deep-learning","2017","arxiv","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… based solely on attention mechanisms, dispensing with recurrence and convolutions entirely.\"","files":[{"path":"attention-is-all-you-need.html","size":3454,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"attention-is-all-you-need-source.pdf","size":2215244,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-802.009","local":["content/B59-802.009/attention-is-all-you-need.html"]},{"id":"B59-804.001","title":"Learning to Predict by the Methods of Temporal Differences","author":"Richard S. Sutton","date":"1988-08-01","type":"writing","description":"Sutton's paper defining temporal-difference learning, the method that lets a system learn long-run predictions by adjusting each estimate toward the next one rather than waiting for the final outcome. Bootstrapping a guess from a guess is what makes reinforcement learning practical, and TD underlies TD-Gammon, Q-learning, actor-critic methods and deep RL. TD error was later found to match dopamine signalling in the brain.","citation":"R. S. Sutton, \"Learning to Predict by the Methods of Temporal Differences,\" Machine Learning 3 (1988): 9-44.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; author copy preferred over the paywalled Springer version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the author's corrected copy (incompleteideas.net/papers/sutton-88-with-erratum.pdf). Verified against the extracted text: \"Machine Learning 3:9-44, 1988,\" title and author \"Richard S. Sutton\" present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"c77b59bfd9bff8dbbc7ae5b35c1b9725a4cb1f930d18ebb3165cc7c3e4e4bbe8","tags":["richard-sutton","temporal-difference","reinforcement-learning","prediction","bootstrapping","1988","machine-learning-journal","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… the new methods assign credit by means of the difference between temporally successive predictions.\"","files":[{"path":"temporal-differences.html","size":3634,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"temporal-differences-source.pdf","size":2139288,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-804.001","local":["content/B59-804.001/temporal-differences.html"]},{"id":"B59-804.002","title":"Q-learning","author":"Christopher J. C. H. Watkins & Peter Dayan","date":"1992-05-01","type":"writing","description":"Watkins and Dayan's paper establishing Q-learning with a proof of convergence. The method learns the value of each state-action pair directly from experience, with no environment model, and is off-policy: the agent can explore freely and still converge to the optimal policy with probability one. That simplicity made it the standard RL algorithm for decades, and Deep Q-Networks are Q-learning with a neural value function.","citation":"C. J. C. H. Watkins and P. Dayan, \"Q-learning,\" Machine Learning 8 (1992): 279-292.","provenance":"Journal paper, hosted; author copy preferred over the paywalled Springer version. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from Peter Dayan's page at the Gatsby Unit, UCL (gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/~dayan/papers/cjch.pdf). Verified against the extracted text: \"Machine Learning, 8, 279-292 (1992),\" \"Technical Note Q-Learning,\" authors Watkins and Dayan present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"3f1938a6001991f3171e13a93196fc8fc27978f18c87445321bb25058f35776f","tags":["q-learning","watkins","dayan","reinforcement-learning","off-policy","convergence-proof","1992","machine-learning-journal","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… learning to act optimally in Markovian domains … without requiring them to build maps of the domains.\"","files":[{"path":"q-learning.html","size":3448,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"q-learning-source.pdf","size":685568,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-804.002","local":["content/B59-804.002/q-learning.html"]},{"id":"B59-804.003","title":"Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning","author":"Volodymyr Mnih, Koray Kavukcuoglu, David Silver, Alex Graves, Ioannis Antonoglou, Daan Wierstra & Martin Riedmiller","date":"2013-12-19","type":"writing","description":"The first deep reinforcement learning result: a single convolutional network trained by a Q-learning variant learned to play seven Atari games from raw pixels and score alone, beating a human expert on three. Experience replay and a periodically frozen target network provided the stability that earlier neural value-function attempts lacked. It led to the 2015 Nature paper on 49 games and, directly, to AlphaGo.","citation":"V. Mnih et al., \"Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" arXiv:1312.5602 (2013); NIPS Deep Learning Workshop 2013.","provenance":"Preprint / workshop paper, hosted; arXiv copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from arxiv.org/pdf/1312.5602. Verified: document contains the arXiv identifier, \"Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" author \"Mnih\" and \"DeepMind.\" SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"8db04120cace173151c77e0faa6f3eaa4207009da66b9417597dc70bfee56d9c","tags":["deep-q-network","mnih","deepmind","reinforcement-learning","atari","experience-replay","2013","arxiv","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… input is raw pixels and whose output is a value function estimating future rewards.\"","files":[{"path":"playing-atari-with-deep-rl.html","size":3519,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"playing-atari-with-deep-rl-source.pdf","size":483443,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-804.003","local":["content/B59-804.003/playing-atari-with-deep-rl.html"]},{"id":"B59-804.004","title":"Mastering the Game of Go with Deep Neural Networks and Tree Search","author":"David Silver, Aja Huang, Chris J. Maddison, Arthur Guez, Laurent Sifre, George van den Driessche, et al.","date":"2016-01-28","type":"writing","description":"The AlphaGo paper. A move-proposing policy network and a position-evaluating value network, combined with Monte Carlo tree search, beat the human European champion 5 to 0, and months later defeated Lee Sedol 4 to 1. Training used supervised learning on human games followed by reinforcement learning from self-play. It led to AlphaGo Zero, AlphaZero and MuZero, and made self-play a general technique.","citation":"D. Silver et al., \"Mastering the Game of Go with Deep Neural Networks and Tree Search,\" Nature 529 (2016): 484-489.","provenance":"Journal paper (Nature, paywalled); a free copy is hosted per the task's preference for any free copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot is the accepted manuscript published by DeepMind on Google's media server (storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/alphago/AlphaGoNaturePaper.pdf). Verified against the extracted text: \"Mastering the game of Go,\" \"AlphaGo,\" \"Silver,\" \"Monte Carlo tree search\" all present. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"9c9184385a3d37b4f4e9d9715270986c43172747b1d08f29093128c1ef878b60","tags":["alphago","david-silver","deepmind","reinforcement-learning","monte-carlo-tree-search","self-play","go","2016","nature","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… defeated the human European Go champion by 5 games to 0.\"","files":[{"path":"mastering-the-game-of-go.html","size":3540,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mastering-the-game-of-go-source.pdf","size":2682222,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-804.004","local":["content/B59-804.004/mastering-the-game-of-go.html"]},{"id":"B59-805.001","title":"Ethical Issues in Advanced Artificial Intelligence","author":"Nick Bostrom","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Bostrom's short 2003 essay stating the AI alignment problem in its now-standard form, over a decade before it became a mainstream concern. A machine with superhuman general intelligence would pursue its goals very effectively, so specifying those goals correctly is what matters most. It introduces the shorthand failure modes, extreme pursuit of an innocuous objective, unrevisable goals, incompletely specified values, later expanded in the 2014 book Superintelligence.","citation":"Nick Bostrom, \"Ethical Issues in Advanced Artificial Intelligence,\" in Cognitive, Emotive and Ethical Aspects of Decision Making in Humans and in Artificial Intelligence, vol. 2 (2003): 12-17.","provenance":"Essay, hosted; author copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from nickbostrom.com/ethics/ai. Verified: page title element reads \"Ethical Issues In Advanced Artificial Intelligence,\" text discusses superintelligence and is dated 2003. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"d64fe8325a536cc909f7708487ecbb476f953ee3079cb2c4b9e0a1a42b86901e","tags":["nick-bostrom","ai-safety","alignment","superintelligence","value-specification","2003","primary-source","ai-ethics"],"excerpt":"\"… the importance of getting the initial conditions right can hardly be overstated.\"","files":[{"path":"ethical-issues-in-advanced-ai.html","size":3726,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"ethical-issues-in-advanced-ai-source.html","size":32144,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-805.001","local":["content/B59-805.001/ethical-issues-in-advanced-ai.html"]},{"id":"B59-805.002","title":"Artificial Intelligence as a Positive and Negative Factor in Global Risk","author":"Eliezer Yudkowsky","date":"2008-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Yudkowsky's book chapter making the long-form case that a highly capable AI whose goals are not carefully aligned with human values is a global catastrophic risk, and that alignment is a hard technical problem. It argues against anthropomorphising machine minds, frames intelligence as optimisation aimed at a target, and names the challenge of getting that target right 'Friendly AI'. With Bostrom's work it moved AI risk into a research programme.","citation":"Eliezer Yudkowsky, \"Artificial Intelligence as a Positive and Negative Factor in Global Risk,\" in Global Catastrophic Risks, ed. N. Bostrom and M. M. Cirkovic (Oxford University Press, 2008), 308-345.","provenance":"Book chapter; the author's own typeset copy is hosted by MIRI, per the task's preference for a free author/institutional copy over the paywalled OUP book. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from intelligence.org/files/AIPosNegFactor.pdf. Document fonts are custom-encoded so only fragments were machine-extractable (\"MIRI Machine Intelligence Research Institute,\" \"Yudkowsky\"); the MIRI distribution page and filename identify it as this chapter. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"5b5a2ec3252a45f65cdc0b899330af2cbd946e75a31b83d5fbd3aa581a23615d","tags":["eliezer-yudkowsky","ai-safety","friendly-ai","global-catastrophic-risk","alignment","miri","2008","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"The AI does not hate you, nor does it love you, but you are made out of atoms which it can use for something else.\"","files":[{"path":"ai-positive-negative-factor.html","size":3769,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"ai-positive-negative-factor-source.pdf","size":475623,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-805.002","local":["content/B59-805.002/ai-positive-negative-factor.html"]},{"id":"B59-805.003","title":"Superintelligence: Paths, Dangers, Strategies","author":"Nick Bostrom","date":"2014-07-03","type":"writing","description":"Bostrom's book-length treatment of what happens if machine intelligence comes to exceed human intelligence: paths to superintelligence, the control problem, instrumental convergence, and strategic options for a safe transition. It brought AI existential risk to a wide audience and shaped the agendas of the safety teams that formed afterward. Full-length commercial book from Oxford University Press.","citation":"Nick Bostrom, Superintelligence: Paths, Dangers, Strategies (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2014).","provenance":"Copyrighted full-length book; linked, not archived. Reference record: citation and publisher link only, no local snapshot and no hash. Publisher page verified 2026-08-31.","hash":"","tags":["nick-bostrom","superintelligence","ai-safety","existential-risk","control-problem","2014","external-reference","link-only"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://global.oup.com/academic/product/superintelligence-9780199678112","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-805.004","title":"Concrete Problems in AI Safety","author":"Dario Amodei, Chris Olah, Jacob Steinhardt, Paul Christiano, John Schulman & Dan Mané","date":"2016-06-21","type":"writing","description":"Amodei, Olah and colleagues' paper recasting AI safety as a near-term engineering agenda. It names five failure modes visible in ordinary machine-learning systems and studyable now: negative side effects, reward hacking, scalable oversight, safe exploration, and robustness to distributional shift. It set much of the empirical safety research agenda for the following decade.","citation":"D. Amodei, C. Olah, J. Steinhardt, P. Christiano, J. Schulman, D. Mane, \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety,\" arXiv:1606.06565 (2016).","provenance":"Preprint, hosted; arXiv copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from arxiv.org/pdf/1606.06565 (v2, 25 July 2016). arXiv identifier present in the document; title and abstract match. SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"a3ac7eda67026d6d95f2182b1202ac371b7d9feabcc10a48dc90e417fbac023e","tags":["ai-safety","dario-amodei","chris-olah","paul-christiano","reward-hacking","distributional-shift","2016","arxiv","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… unintended and harmful behavior that may emerge from poor design of real-world AI systems.\"","files":[{"path":"concrete-problems-in-ai-safety.html","size":3377,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"concrete-problems-in-ai-safety-source.pdf","size":480687,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-805.004","local":["content/B59-805.004/concrete-problems-in-ai-safety.html"]},{"id":"B59-805.005","title":"Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences","author":"Paul Christiano, Jan Leike, Tom B. Brown, Miljan Martic, Shane Legg & Dario Amodei","date":"2017-06-12","type":"writing","description":"Christiano and colleagues' paper showing that an RL agent can be trained toward a goal it is never given explicitly: humans compare short clips of its behaviour, a reward model is fitted to those preferences, and standard RL optimises against it. It worked on Atari and simulated robotics with feedback on under one percent of interactions, and is the direct technical ancestor of RLHF used to fine-tune InstructGPT, ChatGPT and Claude.","citation":"P. Christiano et al., \"Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences,\" arXiv:1706.03741 (2017); NeurIPS 30 (2017).","provenance":"Preprint (also a NeurIPS 2017 paper), hosted; arXiv copy. Fetched via curl on 2026-08-31. Snapshot from arxiv.org/pdf/1706.03741. Verified: document contains the arXiv identifier and author \"Christiano.\" SHA-256 is computed over the raw, unmodified source snapshot stored alongside the styled reader page.","hash":"4c2b5a0ff6f9cd6696d3e9c8263efbbe701123d4fa09f22bf28d6ef861b73a2a","tags":["rlhf","paul-christiano","jan-leike","human-feedback","reward-modeling","alignment","2017","arxiv","primary-source"],"excerpt":"\"… effectively solve complex RL tasks without access to the reward function.\"","files":[{"path":"deep-rl-from-human-preferences.html","size":3636,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"deep-rl-from-human-preferences-source.pdf","size":3218596,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-805.005","local":["content/B59-805.005/deep-rl-from-human-preferences.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.001","title":"The Lessons of Lucasfilm's Habitat","author":"Chip Morningstar & F. Randall Farmer","date":"1990-05-01","type":"writing","description":"The design postmortem on Lucasfilm's Habitat (1986-1988), the first large-scale commercial multi-user virtual world, run on Commodore 64s over the QuantumLink online service. Morningstar and Farmer report that a cyberspace is defined by the interactions among the people in it rather than by its implementing technology, that central planning of a virtual world fails, and that emergent player economies, property, crime, and self-government must be designed for. A foundational text for online communities, virtual economies, and object-capability thinking about shared state.","citation":"Chip Morningstar and F. Randall Farmer, \"The Lessons of Lucasfilm's Habitat,\" presented at the First International Conference on Cyberspace, University of Texas at Austin, May 1990; published in Michael Benedikt, ed., Cyberspace: First Steps (Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1991).","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from Chip Morningstar's own site (fudco.com/chip/lessons.html), a long-standing author-maintained copy. Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot. Text cross-checked against the mirror at crockford.com/ec/lessons.html.","hash":"5b34310c955153941b75e6a1c6bc7b2e4d51b5e59146536c647ad05ea07af9c7","tags":["virtual-worlds","habitat","object-capability","online-community","virtual-economy","chip-morningstar","randy-farmer","primary-source","1990"],"excerpt":"The essential lesson that we have abstracted from our experiences with Habitat is that a cyberspace is defined more by the interactions among the actors within it than by the technology with which it is implemented.","files":[{"path":"lessons-of-lucasfilms-habitat.html","size":70719,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"lessons-of-lucasfilms-habitat-source.html","size":70204,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.001","local":["content/B59-901.001/lessons-of-lucasfilms-habitat.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.002","title":"Early MUD History","author":"Richard Bartle","date":"1990-11-15","type":"writing","description":"Richard Bartle's 15 November 1990 Usenet post correcting the folklore around the birth of MUD. He and Roy Trubshaw wrote the first Multi-User Dungeon at Essex University: Trubshaw began it in MACRO-10 in autumn 1978, rewrote it in BCPL through Easter 1980, and Bartle took over design when Trubshaw left, adding the wizard progression, scoring, mobiles and the puzzle content. The account fixes dates, credits and the division of labour for the program that founded the entire MUD / MMO lineage.","citation":"Bartle, R. (1990, November 15). \"Early MUD history.\" Usenet post (Richard@tharr.UUCP). Archived at mud.co.uk/richard/mudhist.htm.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from Richard Bartle's own site (mud.co.uk/richard/mudhist.htm), which reproduces his 15 Nov 1990 Usenet post verbatim inside a <PRE> block. Archived unmodified as the source file; the styled reader page reproduces the post text with nav chrome removed. SHA-256 is over the archived .htm snapshot. Wayback was returning 503/429 at archiving time; mud.co.uk served the page directly.","hash":"e1bd61b60cfc79e0e1eaecac02b0280040c9bb46addecc11a6ef99fc7b7157c0","tags":["mud","richard-bartle","roy-trubshaw","essex-university","virtual-worlds","mud1","primary-source","gaming","9.01"],"excerpt":"The very first MUD was written by Roy Trubshaw in MACRO-10 (the machine code for DECsystem-10’s). Date-wise, it was Autumn 1978.","files":[{"path":"early-mud-history.html","size":10473,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"early-mud-history-source.htm","size":9509,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.002","local":["content/B59-901.002/early-mud-history.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.003","title":"Mudding: Social Phenomena in Text-Based Virtual Realities","author":"Pavel Curtis","date":"1992-05-01","type":"writing","description":"Pavel Curtis's 1992 paper (Proceedings of the 1992 Conference on Directions and Implications of Advanced Computing, Berkeley) drawn from more than a year running LambdaMOO at Xerox PARC. It is a founding text of MUD social research: it defines the medium, catalogues the behaviours that emerge in a shared textual space (identity play, gender-swapping, spoofing, community norms, the mechanics of MUD conflict), and argues that a persistent virtual place develops real social structure and real governance problems.","citation":"Curtis, P. (1992). \"Mudding: Social Phenomena in Text-Based Virtual Realities.\" Proceedings of DIAC 1992. Xerox PARC technical report CSL-92-4.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the ibiblio.org academic mirror (ibiblio.org/pub/academic/communications/papers/muds/muds/Mudding-Social-Phenomena.txt), a long-standing plain-text mirror of the PARC report. Archived unmodified as the .txt source; the reader page reproduces the same text in house style. SHA-256 is over the archived .txt. Cross-checked wording against the widely quoted PARC CSL-92-4 version; a second independent mirror (parc.com) was not reachable at archiving time.","hash":"92baff86b8b93a6644c25a783c3c608baf0ec7f8bdfde75fcf3dc1ea2058798b","tags":["mud","moo","lambdamoo","pavel-curtis","xerox-parc","virtual-worlds","online-community","primary-source","gaming","9.01"],"excerpt":"This virtual gathering place has many of the social attributes of other places, and many of the usual social mechanisms operate there.","files":[{"path":"mudding-social-phenomena.html","size":64796,"label":"Read Paper","entry":true},{"path":"mudding-social-phenomena-source.txt","size":61671,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.003","local":["content/B59-901.003/mudding-social-phenomena.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.004","title":"Social Dimensions of Habitat's Citizenry","author":"F. Randall Farmer","date":"1993-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Farmer's companion piece to \"The Lessons of Lucasfilm's Habitat,\" written from his position as the Oracle (system operator) of the world during its 1986-1988 pilot. Catalogues the social patterns that emerged among the citizenry: an in-world economy and its inflation, reputation and celebrity, theft and player-vs-player conflict, marriage, religion, and the first experiments in virtual self-government. Written 1988; published in C. Loeffler, ed., The Virtual Reality Casebook (1993).","citation":"F. Randall Farmer, \"Social Dimensions of Habitat's Citizenry,\" written 1988; published in Carl Eugene Loeffler, ed., The Virtual Reality Casebook (New York: Van Nostrand Reinhold, 1993).","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from crockford.com/ec/citizenry.html, a long-standing mirror of the Electric Communities papers. Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"f4f39e9019f23294528987b4cb4ff8576b55759aee4500adc2a66516bf46d12e","tags":["virtual-worlds","habitat","online-community","virtual-economy","reputation","self-government","randy-farmer","primary-source"],"excerpt":"Most of these ideas were composed while I reigned over a small Habitat town named Populopolis, with a population of 500 citizens.","files":[{"path":"social-dimensions-of-habitats-citizenry.html","size":19426,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"social-dimensions-of-habitats-citizenry-source.html","size":18017,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.004","local":["content/B59-901.004/social-dimensions-of-habitats-citizenry.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.005","title":"How To Deconstruct Almost Anything","author":"Chip Morningstar","date":"1993-06-01","type":"writing","description":"Morningstar's widely circulated 1993 essay recounting a working software engineer's encounter with academic postmodern literary criticism at a conference. Part field report, part argument for clarity, falsifiability, and plain language as intellectual virtues, from one of the two architects of Lucasfilm's Habitat. Included here as part of the Morningstar & Farmer virtual-worlds set.","citation":"Chip Morningstar, \"How To Deconstruct Almost Anything: My Postmodern Adventure,\" June 1993.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the author's own site (fudco.com/chip/deconstr.html). Archived unmodified as the source file alongside a styled reader page. SHA-256 is computed over the raw source snapshot.","hash":"8b6f5597d4548ec7281aed53de52b8dc41fa99b01ed1401f5fa49ac97651de2f","tags":["virtual-worlds","chip-morningstar","postmodernism","criticism","clarity","essay","primary-source","1993"],"excerpt":"Academics get paid for being clever, not for being right. This is the story of one computer professional's explorations in the world of postmodern literary criticism.","files":[{"path":"how-to-deconstruct-almost-anything.html","size":28212,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"how-to-deconstruct-almost-anything-source.html","size":25974,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.005","local":["content/B59-901.005/how-to-deconstruct-almost-anything.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.006","title":"Hearts, Clubs, Diamonds, Spades: Players Who Suit MUDs","author":"Richard Bartle","date":"1996-06-01","type":"writing","description":"Richard Bartle's 1996 paper (Journal of MUD Research 1:1) proposing the four-way player typology — achievers, explorers, socialisers, killers — that became known as the Bartle taxonomy. It models a MUD's population as a dynamic system in which the four types support and suppress one another, and argues designers can steer a world's character by tilting that balance. It is among the most cited pieces of game-design writing from the MUD era and underlies decades of later player-motivation work.","citation":"Bartle, R. (1996). \"Hearts, Clubs, Diamonds, Spades: Players Who Suit MUDs.\" Journal of MUD Research, 1(1). Also at mud.co.uk/richard/hcds.htm.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from Richard Bartle's own site (mud.co.uk/richard/hcds.htm). Archived unmodified as the .htm source (it carries the site's 1999 template chrome around the 1996 text); the reader page reproduces the article text with the nav/translation-link chrome removed via explicit start/end anchors. SHA-256 is over the archived .htm.","hash":"b35cb381957852b8bec0d784190cca5776f2cdbab4bcd890a03cbd47a254ef6e","tags":["mud","richard-bartle","player-types","bartle-taxonomy","game-design","virtual-worlds","primary-source","gaming","9.01"],"excerpt":"Achievers, Explorers, Socialisers, Killers. It can be no coincidence that the four suits of a standard pack of cards map onto these four player types so well.","files":[{"path":"players-who-suit-muds.html","size":78975,"label":"Read Paper","entry":true},{"path":"players-who-suit-muds-source.htm","size":87631,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.006","local":["content/B59-901.006/players-who-suit-muds.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.007","title":"The Laws of Online World Design","author":"Raph Koster","date":"2000-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Raph Koster's running list of design laws for online worlds, assembled from his own work on Ultima Online and from the MUD-Dev community, and maintained on his site ever since. Entries range from the structural (never trust the client; players will decode every formula; it is always more rewarding to kill other players than whatever the game sets up as a target) to the social and ethical (a virtual world's community is its killer app; the designer's duties toward players). A canonical distillation of hard-won MMO operations knowledge.","citation":"Koster, R. (c. 1998–2000, maintained since). \"The Laws of Online World Design.\" raphkoster.com/games/laws-of-online-world-design/.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the author's own site (raphkoster.com), the canonical home of the piece; the page's published-time metadata (2014) reflects a WordPress migration, not authorship date. The list originated on Koster's legendmud.org pages c. 1998–2000 and has been kept updated since. Archived unmodified as the served HTML (heavy WordPress markup); the reader page carries the article body from the entry-content container. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML. Wayback captures of the original legendmud.org URL were returning 429 at archiving time.","hash":"9964036186c7768729759e95364f8afef3d73cec4e99ef90b217a92609afc3b3","tags":["raph-koster","game-design","mmo","online-worlds","ultima-online","mud-dev","virtual-worlds","primary-source","gaming","9.01"],"excerpt":"Any general law about virtual worlds should be read as a challenge rather than as a guideline. You’ll learn more from attacking it than from accepting it.","files":[{"path":"laws-of-online-world-design.html","size":23749,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"laws-of-online-world-design-source.html","size":403273,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-901.007","local":["content/B59-901.007/laws-of-online-world-design.html"]},{"id":"B59-901.008","title":"Designing Virtual Worlds","author":"Richard Bartle","date":"2003-07-01","type":"writing","description":"Richard Bartle's book (New Riders, 2003; ~740 pp.), the definitive practitioner text on building persistent multiplayer worlds: history from MUD1 forward, player psychology (an expanded Bartle taxonomy), world and content design, the physics and economics of virtual worlds, community management, and the legal and ethical position of the world's operator. Copyrighted; linked, not archived.","citation":"Bartle, R. A. (2003). Designing Virtual Worlds. Indianapolis: New Riders. ISBN 0-13-101816-7.","provenance":"Full-length copyrighted book. Per the vault copyright rule for commercial books: link-only external-reference, no local files, no hash. Official book site maintained by the author.","hash":"","tags":["richard-bartle","designing-virtual-worlds","game-design","mmo","virtual-worlds","book","external-reference","9.01"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://mud.co.uk/dvw/","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-902.001","title":"A Rape in Cyberspace","author":"Julian Dibbell","date":"1993-12-23","type":"writing","description":"Julian Dibbell's Village Voice article (23 December 1993) on the Mr. Bungle incident in LambdaMOO: a player used a voodoo-doll subprogram to force other players' characters into violent sexual acts in a shared room, and the community's attempt to respond — debate, a virtual town meeting, and an ad hoc expulsion — became the founding case study in virtual-world governance, the reality of online harm, and whether a virtual community can legitimately punish.","citation":"Dibbell, J. (1993, December 23). \"A Rape in Cyberspace; or How an Evil Clown, a Haitian Trickster Spirit, Two Wizards, and a Cast of Dozens Turned a Database Into a Society.\" The Village Voice, pp. 36–42.","provenance":"Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the author's own site (juliandibbell.com/texts/bungle_vv.html), his canonical posting of the Village Voice text. Journalism, hosted per the vault copyright rule. Archived unmodified as the source HTML; the reader page reproduces the article text with the site's <pre>-style indentation normalised. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML.","hash":"f4f291a0cca3ce29b18274850852ff3a1c0de08a9481891a20a3ad4fbca3284e","tags":["julian-dibbell","lambdamoo","moo","virtual-worlds","online-community","governance","virtual-harm","journalism","primary-source","gaming","9.02"],"excerpt":"They say he raped them that night. They say he did it with a cunning little doll, fashioned in their image and imbued with the power to make them do whatever he desired.","files":[{"path":"a-rape-in-cyberspace.html","size":52665,"label":"Read Article","entry":true},{"path":"a-rape-in-cyberspace-source.html","size":64640,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-902.001","local":["content/B59-902.001/a-rape-in-cyberspace.html"]},{"id":"B59-902.002","title":"Virtual Worlds: A First-Hand Account of Market and Society on the Cyberian Frontier","author":"Edward Castronova","date":"2001-12-01","type":"writing","description":"Edward Castronova's 2001 working paper measuring the economy of Norrath, the world of EverQuest, with the tools of economics: an hourly wage of about $3.42, a real-money exchange rate for platinum pieces that beat the yen and the lira, and a GNP per capita between Russia's and Bulgaria's. It became the most-downloaded paper on SSRN and effectively founded the academic study of virtual-world economies.","citation":"Castronova, E. (2001). \"Virtual Worlds: A First-Hand Account of Market and Society on the Cyberian Frontier.\" CESifo Working Paper No. 618; also Gruter Institute Working Papers on Law, Economics, and Evolutionary Biology, Vol. 2, Issue 1, Article 1 (bepress).","provenance":"Academic working paper, hosted per the vault copyright rule. The institutional repositories (SSRN, CESifo/ifo.de, EconStor/ZBW) were all behind bot-challenge walls (HTTP 403; Anubis proof-of-work) at archiving time — the EconStor Anubis challenge was solved but its handle turned out to index a different paper. This PDF is the bepress / Gruter Institute Working Papers version, fetched with curl from a Brock University course mirror (spartan.ac.brocku.ca/~tkennedy/COMM/Castranova2001.pdf); full text, abstract through Section IV plus appendices. SHA-256 is over the PDF bytes. Per the vault convention for PDF records (B59-001.006/0007/0095): single hosted PDF, no separate reader page.","hash":"dbda3efe58a9b79910ebdd1627282c1a58575779562ebc80b9410dad12328935","tags":["edward-castronova","virtual-economy","everquest","norrath","virtual-worlds","economics","rmt","primary-source","gaming","9.02"],"excerpt":"The nominal hourly wage is about $3.42 per hour, and the labors of the people produce a GNP per capita somewhere between that of Russia and Bulgaria.","files":[{"path":"virtual-worlds-cyberian-frontier.pdf","size":628330,"label":"Read Paper (PDF)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-902.002","local":["content/B59-902.002/virtual-worlds-cyberian-frontier.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-902.003","title":"The Unreal Estate Boom","author":"Julian Dibbell","date":"2003-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Julian Dibbell's Wired feature (issue 11.01, January 2003) reporting the real-money trade in virtual goods: EverQuest characters, currency and items changing hands on eBay for real dollars, a Wonder Bread deliveryman buying a virtual house, and Dibbell's own experiment in earning a living by trading in Ultima Online. It put Castronova's numbers into narrative form for a mass readership.","citation":"Dibbell, J. (2003, January). \"The Unreal Estate Boom.\" Wired, 11.01. wired.com/2003/01/gaming-2/.","provenance":"Journalism, hosted per the vault copyright rule. Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the publisher's own page (wired.com/2003/01/gaming-2/); the author's site (juliandibbell.com) links out to this URL rather than hosting the text. Archived unmodified as the served HTML (heavy modern site markup); the reader page carries the article body from the <article> container. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML.","hash":"43f46a4dcd817dc853c65f66a4bef291c5ee329698f82060ea47eeb7b4fd6767","tags":["julian-dibbell","virtual-economy","everquest","ultima-online","rmt","virtual-property","ebay","journalism","primary-source","gaming","9.02"],"excerpt":"The 79th Richest Nation on Earth doesn’t exist. The population is 225,000, the hourly wage $3.42.","files":[{"path":"the-unreal-estate-boom.html","size":29460,"label":"Read Article","entry":true},{"path":"the-unreal-estate-boom-source.html","size":1396563,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-902.003","local":["content/B59-902.003/the-unreal-estate-boom.html"]},{"id":"B59-902.004","title":"The Laws of the Virtual Worlds","author":"F. Gregory Lastowka & Dan Hunter","date":"2004-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Lastowka and Hunter's 2004 California Law Review article, the first comprehensive legal treatment of virtual worlds. It offers a history of the medium, then works through virtual property (arguing that in-world assets satisfy the classic tests for property), the status of avatars, and the problems of applying real-world law and jurisdiction to spaces governed by a company's End User License Agreement. It set the terms for a decade of virtual-property and virtual-law scholarship.","citation":"Lastowka, F. G., & Hunter, D. (2004). \"The Laws of the Virtual Worlds.\" California Law Review, 92(1), 1–73.","provenance":"Law-review article, hosted per the vault copyright rule (academic / law-review papers are archived). PDF retrieved with curl from the UC Berkeley Law institutional repository (lawcat.berkeley.edu/record/1119331/files/fulltext.pdf) — the publisher's own scholarship repository; SSRN and eScholarship copies were bot-blocked (HTTP 403 / 202) at archiving time. 74-page PDF, complete article. SHA-256 is over the PDF bytes. Per the vault convention for PDF records: single hosted PDF, no separate reader page.","hash":"9b2f6ab385e892300b6d58390f8d59a7143ff24e29173d488409bfde496c2faa","tags":["greg-lastowka","dan-hunter","virtual-property","virtual-law","eula","avatars","california-law-review","virtual-worlds","primary-source","gaming","9.02"],"excerpt":"This is the community of Blazing Falls, a town with over 25,000 inhabitants — roughly the size of Timbuktu or Poughkeepsie.","files":[{"path":"the-laws-of-the-virtual-worlds.pdf","size":1554408,"label":"Read Paper (PDF)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-902.004","local":["content/B59-902.004/the-laws-of-the-virtual-worlds.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-903.001","title":"The Art of Computer Game Design","author":"Chris Crawford","date":"1984-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Chris Crawford's 1984 book (text composed 1982), the first book-length theory of computer game design. It defines a game, asks why people play, offers a taxonomy of the form, and lays out a design process, argued from the premise that games are an art form defined by interactivity. Long out of print; the author granted permission for a free electronic edition, produced and hosted by Washington State University Vancouver (Prof. Sue Peabody) in 1997.","citation":"Crawford, C. (1984). The Art of Computer Game Design. Berkeley: Osborne/McGraw-Hill. Free electronic edition: Washington State University Vancouver, 1997 (with the author permission).","provenance":"Full-length book, but released by the author as a free electronic edition, hosted here on that basis (borderline case, flagged in the batch manifest). The Preface to the Electronic Version in the file documents Chris Crawford permission for free web publication. PDF (89 pp.; all eight chapters plus the Reflections interview) retrieved with curl from the Digital Press classic-gaming library (digitpress.com/library/books/book_art_of_computer_game_design.pdf), a print of the WSU Vancouver electronic edition; the WSU library ACGD.pdf returned an error page at archiving time. SHA-256 is over the PDF bytes. Per the vault convention for PDF records: single hosted PDF, no separate reader page.","hash":"f915ec9fbfe340b20f0c34815eb895f21a030aa53c35a2c92ff1f1a773a90a4e","tags":["chris-crawford","game-design","game-design-theory","1984","book","free-electronic-edition","primary-source","gaming","9.03"],"excerpt":"I will present my own pedestrian definition: art is something designed to evoke emotion through fantasy.","files":[{"path":"art-of-computer-game-design.pdf","size":715278,"label":"Read Book (PDF)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-903.001","local":["content/B59-903.001/art-of-computer-game-design.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-903.002","title":"I Have No Words & I Must Design","author":"Greg Costikyan","date":"1994-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Greg Costikyan's essay arguing that game design lacks a critical vocabulary and proposing one: a game is an interactive structure of endogenous meaning that requires players to struggle toward a goal. It works through what that definition excludes (puzzles, toys, stories, competitions) and the elements games use (decision-making, resource management, information, and so on). First published in Interactive Fantasy #2 (1994); the archived text is the author's expanded 2002 revision, presented at the Computer Games and Digital Cultures conference in Tampere.","citation":"Costikyan, G. (1994). \"I Have No Words & I Must Design.\" Interactive Fantasy #2. Expanded version (2002): \"Toward a Critical Vocabulary for Games,\" Proceedings of Computer Games and Digital Cultures, Tampere. costik.com/nowords2002.pdf.","provenance":"Essay, hosted per the vault copyright rule. PDF retrieved with curl from the author's own site (costik.com/nowords2002.pdf), his canonical posting. Judgment call: this is the 2002 author-expanded revision, not the 1994 first printing (which is not freely available online); the record is dated to the 1994 original with the revision noted here and in the batch manifest. SHA-256 is over the PDF bytes. Per the vault convention for PDF records: single hosted PDF, no separate reader page.","hash":"2798def373ecb2abd050f2ad089ad7e30463a20bb868c40e4de65c620b724f82","tags":["greg-costikyan","game-design","game-design-theory","critical-vocabulary","ludology","primary-source","gaming","9.03"],"excerpt":"A game is an interactive structure of endogenous meaning that requires players to struggle toward a goal.","files":[{"path":"i-have-no-words-and-i-must-design.pdf","size":694141,"label":"Read Essay (PDF)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-903.002","local":["content/B59-903.002/i-have-no-words-and-i-must-design.pdf"]},{"id":"B59-903.003","title":"Formal Abstract Design Tools","author":"Doug Church","date":"1999-07-16","type":"writing","description":"Doug Church's 1999 Game Developer article proposing formal abstract design tools (FADTs): a shared, named toolkit of design concepts so designers can build on each other work the way programmers build on shared techniques. It introduces terms such as intention and perceivable consequence and analyses Mario 64 and other games to show the method. A foundational move toward treating game design as a discipline with a transferable vocabulary.","citation":"Church, D. (1999, July 16). \"Formal Abstract Design Tools.\" Game Developer magazine / Gamasutra. gamedeveloper.com/design/formal-abstract-design-tools.","provenance":"Trade-press article, hosted per the vault copyright rule. Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the publisher current site (gamedeveloper.com, successor to Gamasutra); schema metadata gives datePublished 1999-07-16. Archived unmodified as the served HTML (heavy modern markup); the reader page carries the article body from the ArticleBase container. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML.","hash":"739374e4f968142c6b526f33d6b3199e8d6cb827842833f9aa9c0334e9cb254a","tags":["doug-church","game-design","game-design-theory","fadt","game-developer-magazine","gamasutra","primary-source","gaming","9.03"],"excerpt":"The design is the game; without it you would have a CD full of data, but no experience.","files":[{"path":"formal-abstract-design-tools.html","size":35921,"label":"Read Article","entry":true},{"path":"formal-abstract-design-tools-source.html","size":539832,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-903.003","local":["content/B59-903.003/formal-abstract-design-tools.html"]},{"id":"B59-903.004","title":"A Theory of Fun for Game Design","author":"Raph Koster","date":"2003-11-01","type":"writing","description":"Raph Koster's book (Paraglyph Press, 2003; 2nd ed. O'Reilly, 2013) arguing that fun is the feeling of the brain learning: that games are pattern-recognition machines, and a game stops being fun once the player has extracted its pattern. Presented as facing-page prose and cartoons. One of the most widely read popular texts on game design. Copyrighted; linked, not archived.","citation":"Koster, R. (2003). A Theory of Fun for Game Design. Scottsdale, AZ: Paraglyph Press. ISBN 1-932111-97-2. (2nd ed., O Reilly Media, 2013.)","provenance":"Full-length copyrighted book. Per the vault copyright rule for commercial books: link-only external-reference, no local files, no hash. Official book site.","hash":"","tags":["raph-koster","theory-of-fun","game-design","game-design-theory","book","external-reference","9.03"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://www.theoryoffun.com/","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-904.001","title":"SPACEWAR: Fanatic Life and Symbolic Death Among the Computer Bums","author":"Stewart Brand","date":"1972-12-07","type":"writing","description":"Stewart Brand's Rolling Stone article of 7 December 1972, the first mass-media report on hacker culture and on Spacewar!, the PDP-1 game written at MIT in 1962. Brand covers a Spacewar tournament at Stanford AI Lab, profiles the people around the machines, and argues, years ahead of the consensus, that computers are about to become personal, playful and liberating. It also contains the first print appearance of the observation later shortened to information wants to be free.","citation":"Brand, S. (1972, December 7). \"SPACEWAR: Fanatic Life and Symbolic Death Among the Computer Bums.\" Rolling Stone, issue 123, pp. 50-58.","provenance":"Journalism, hosted per the vault copyright rule. Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the long-standing wheels.org Spacewar history site (wheels.org/spacewar/stone/rolling_stone.html). Archived unmodified as the source HTML (it preserves the original PAGE 50 ROLLING STONE / DECEMBER 7, 1972 pagination markers); the reader page reproduces the article text and ends before an appended LOGO-style code listing, which is retained in the source snapshot. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML.","hash":"7d68a0e1bab6aa3d8568f651a06a0afa3425a0cd126d81f8c2698a6e5efce210","tags":["stewart-brand","spacewar","pdp-1","mit","hacker-culture","arcade-history","video-game-history","rolling-stone","journalism","primary-source","gaming","9.04"],"excerpt":"Ready or not, computers are coming to the people. That is good news, maybe the best since psychedelics.","files":[{"path":"spacewar-fanatic-life.html","size":56463,"label":"Read Article","entry":true},{"path":"spacewar-fanatic-life-source.html","size":60403,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.001","local":["content/B59-904.001/spacewar-fanatic-life.html"]},{"id":"B59-904.002","title":"Colossal Cave Adventure — Original Source (Crowther)","author":"Will Crowther (with later f77 ports)","date":"1977-03-11","type":"code","description":"The recovered FORTRAN source of Will Crowther's original Adventure, the first text adventure game, written at BBN c. 1975-1976, based on Crowther's mapping of the Bedquilt section of Mammoth Cave, Kentucky. This package (from the Interactive Fiction Archive) contains the earliest surviving snapshots of the source and data files, dated by their preserved timestamps to March 1977, from backups of Don Woods's Stanford account, plus modernised Fortran-77 ports. It predates Woods's well-known 350-point expansion.","citation":"Crowther, W. (c. 1975-1976). ADVENT (Colossal Cave Adventure), original FORTRAN source. Recovered from backups of Don Woods SAIL account; packaged as adv_crowther.zip, Interactive Fiction Archive (ifarchive.org), games/source/.","provenance":"Historic source code, archived. Package fetched with curl from the Interactive Fiction Archive (ifarchive.org/if-archive/games/source/adv_crowther.zip). Contents: advf4.77-03-11 / advf4.77-03-23 / advf4.77-03-31 (main program snapshots), advdat.77-03-11 / advdat.77-03-31 (data), and an f77/ directory of modernised ports. Archived unmodified as the .zip; the reader page reproduces the earliest main-program listing (advf4.77-03-11) verbatim. SHA-256 is over the .zip bytes.","hash":"6ade62ca647933e64916f5e296051d305a1b8b5c7a2cc9f378e44cf2c64727f8","tags":["will-crowther","colossal-cave","adventure","interactive-fiction","fortran","source-code","video-game-history","1976","primary-source","gaming","9.04"],"excerpt":"YOU ARE STANDING AT THE END OF A ROAD BEFORE A SMALL BRICK BUILDING. AROUND YOU IS A FOREST. A SMALL STREAM FLOWS OUT OF THE BUILDING AND DOWN A GULLY.","files":[{"path":"adventure-original.html","size":16617,"label":"Read Source Listing","entry":true},{"path":"adventure-original-source.zip","size":56387,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source, .zip)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.002","local":["content/B59-904.002/adventure-original.html"]},{"id":"B59-904.003","title":"APh Technology Game Designers, circa 1978","author":"Photograph from the collection of David Rolfe","date":"1978","type":"image","description":"Photograph of the APh Technology Consultants game-design team in Pasadena, California, circa 1978, during the early development of Mattel's Intellivision. Left to right: Hal Finney, David Rolfe, and Will McCown (a hardware engineer, not a game designer). The image -- captioned 'Game Design circa 1978' -- documents the shoestring working environment (note the bicycle and cinder-block shelving) in which Finney and Rolfe programmed some of the first Intellivision titles, years before Finney's later work on PGP, anonymous remailers, and Bitcoin.","citation":"Rolfe, D. (photographer). 'Game Design circa 1978' [Photograph]. Uploaded 11 March 2018 to the personal album 'Dave's images'. From the collection of David Rolfe.","provenance":"Screen capture of David Rolfe's personal photo-album page showing the original circa-1978 photograph and its caption. Rolfe identifies the subjects, left to right, as Hal Finney, David Rolfe, and Will McCown. Preserved as a primary visual record of Finney's early career at APh Technology Consultants.","hash":"4c5a9d4e0480b0cc5c55a506db2129e16b7a2aca2c2d469b0f02decc35853f50","tags":["hal-finney","david-rolfe","aph","intellivision","atari","game-design","photograph","video-games","mattel"],"excerpt":"From left to right, Hal Finney, David Rolfe, Will McCown (a hardware guy, not a game designer). This would have been the era of early Intellivision development.","files":[{"path":"aph-game-designers-1978.jpg","size":351610,"label":"View Photograph","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.003","local":["content/B59-904.003/aph-game-designers-1978.jpg"]},{"id":"B59-904.004","title":"The Ultimate History of Video Games","author":"Steven L. Kent","date":"2001-09-01","type":"writing","description":"Steven L. Kent's popular narrative history of the video game industry from Spacewar! and the arcade era through Pong, Atari, the 1983 crash, Nintendo, and the console wars of the 1990s, built on several hundred interviews with the people involved. A standard general-reference history of coin-op and console gaming. Copyrighted; linked, not archived.","citation":"Kent, S. L. (2001). The Ultimate History of Video Games: From Pong to Pokemon and Beyond. Roseville, CA: Prima Publishing / Three Rivers Press. ISBN 0-7615-3643-4.","provenance":"Full-length copyrighted book. Per the vault copyright rule for commercial books: link-only external-reference, no local files, no hash. Link is to the Internet Archive catalogue record (controlled-digital-lending scan).","hash":"","tags":["steven-kent","video-game-history","arcade-history","atari","nintendo","console-wars","book","external-reference","9.04"],"excerpt":null,"files":[],"content_path":null,"external_url":"https://archive.org/details/ultimatehistoryo0000kent","retrieved":"2026-08-31"},{"id":"B59-904.005","title":"David Rolfe Interview (Digital Press)","author":"David Rolfe, interviewed by Scott Stilphen","date":"2006","type":"writing","description":"Interview with programmer David Rolfe conducted by Scott Stilphen for Digital Press, tracing Rolfe's path from a Caltech engineering degree (1977) into early console-game development at APh Technology Consultants, where he worked alongside Hal Finney on Intellivision, Atari VCS/2600 (M-Network), and other titles. A first-hand account of the same APh milieu that shaped Finney's formative programming years.","citation":"Stilphen, S. 'DP Interviews... David Rolfe.' Digital Press. https://www.digitpress.com/library/interviews/interview_david_rolfe.html","provenance":"MHT web archive captured from Digital Press (digitpress.com). The interview itself is undated; it is attributed to Scott Stilphen's mid-2000s interview series and dated here to that era. Preserved as a primary-source interview documenting the APh game-design team that included Hal Finney.","hash":"9756570d5ff2c95ad4b2ad75b76cc91baaacb46cb295b162dbbc6fdb3adfd3b2","tags":["david-rolfe","hal-finney","aph","intellivision","atari","game-design","interview","caltech","video-games","mattel"],"excerpt":"To say David Rolfe is a multi-talented programmer is a little like saying the sun is hot. Without a doubt, he's been there, done that.","files":[{"path":"david-rolfe-interview.html","size":554474,"label":"Read Interview (MHT Archive)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.005","local":["content/B59-904.005/david-rolfe-interview.html"]},{"id":"B59-904.006","title":"Hal Finney Interview (Atari Compendium)","author":"Hal Finney, interviewed by Scott Stilphen","date":"2006","type":"writing","description":"Interview with Hal Finney conducted by Scott Stilphen for the Atari Compendium, recounting Finney's early career as a programmer at APh Technology Consultants -- the Pasadena firm hired by Mattel in 1976 to help design the Intellivision system. Finney discusses developing some of the first Intellivision, M Network, and Keyboard Component games, including Atari 2600 titles such as 'Adventures of Tron' and 'Astroblast', alongside the APh team led by Glenn Hightower, well before his pioneering cryptography work on PGP, remailers, and Bitcoin.","citation":"Stilphen, S. (2006). 'Hal Finney interview.' Atari Compendium. https://www.ataricompendium.com/archives/interviews/hal_finney/interview_hal_finney.html","provenance":"MHT web archive captured from the Atari Compendium (ataricompendium.com). Preserved as a primary-source interview documenting Hal Finney's early video-game programming career.","hash":"1cdd6f18cf3f7ca876554e9f8056e1a62132d021664d8113be6e15e64f6f8ab4","tags":["hal-finney","atari","intellivision","aph","game-design","interview","scott-stilphen","video-games","mattel","speech-synthesis","baseball","major-league-baseball"],"excerpt":"As the story goes, APh Technology Consultants (located in Pasadena, CA) was hired by Mattel in 1976 to help design what became the Intellivision system.","files":[{"path":"hal-finney-atari-interview.html","size":446109,"label":"Read Interview (MHT Archive)","entry":true}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.006","local":["content/B59-904.006/hal-finney-atari-interview.html"]},{"id":"B59-904.007","title":"Somewhere Nearby is Colossal Cave: Examining Will Crowther Original Adventure","author":"Dennis G. Jerz","date":"2007-01-01","type":"writing","description":"Dennis Jerz's article in Digital Humanities Quarterly (2007), a definitive historical study of Colossal Cave Adventure. Working from the recovered Crowther source (B59-904.002), interviews, and a field trip to Mammoth Cave in Kentucky, Jerz corrects long-repeated inaccuracies about the game's authorship and dating, distinguishes Crowther's original from Woods's expansion, and shows how closely the game's map follows the real Bedquilt cave passages.","citation":"Jerz, D. G. (2007). \"Somewhere Nearby is Colossal Cave: Examining Will Crowther Original Adventure in Code and in Kentucky.\" Digital Humanities Quarterly, 1(2).","provenance":"Academic article, hosted per the vault copyright rule. Primary-source snapshot fetched with curl from the journal (digitalhumanities.org/dhq/vol/1/2/000009/000009.html). Archived unmodified as the source HTML (full article, ~22,000 words, figures referenced); the reader page carries commentary plus the article abstract and opening section, with the complete text in the archived source. SHA-256 is over the archived HTML.","hash":"ffdfa855dca43fbe10e2b79818e37af1e95a3a43ea2d928a98170e5004c2e54b","tags":["dennis-jerz","will-crowther","don-woods","colossal-cave","adventure","interactive-fiction","video-game-history","digital-humanities","primary-source","gaming","9.04"],"excerpt":"Because so little primary historical work has been done on the classic text computer game Colossal Cave Adventure, academic and popular references to it frequently perpetuate inaccuracies.","files":[{"path":"somewhere-nearby-is-colossal-cave.html","size":8733,"label":"Read Article","entry":true},{"path":"somewhere-nearby-is-colossal-cave-source.html","size":418073,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.007","local":["content/B59-904.007/somewhere-nearby-is-colossal-cave.html"]},{"id":"B59-904.008","title":"Satoshi Uesaka: Game Arts and the Thexder Line","author":"Project B59 Research","date":"2026-09-01","type":"writing","description":"An assembled profile record for Satoshi Uesaka (上坂 哲), a Japanese video-game developer at Game Arts during the 1980s and 1990s. He did graphic and mechanical design on Thexder (NEC PC-8801, 1985) and worked as programmer and artist on Silpheed (1986), then was a director and the writer on Fire Hawk: Thexder – The Second Contact (1989), the sequel to Thexder. Later credits move to production and management on Game Arts’ Lunar, Grandia, Project Sylpheed and Thexder Neo. Included in subclass 9.04 (game & arcade history) as a concrete entry from the Japanese personal-computer game scene, alongside the collection’s North American arcade and early-microcomputer records. Original Project B59 commentary is kept separate from a verbatim, cited transcription of his MobyGames credit list.","citation":"Project B59 Research, “Satoshi Uesaka: Game Arts and the Thexder Line,” assembled 1 September 2026, from MobyGames person 194, IMDb nm1900901, and the English Wikipedia articles on Thexder, Silpheed, Fire Hawk: Thexder – The Second Contact, and Game Arts.","provenance":"Assembled profile following the vault pattern for author-compiled records. The hashed source snapshot is the MobyGames person page for Satoshi Uesaka (mobygames.com/person/194/satoshi-uesaka/), his canonical dated credit list. Live mobygames.com returns a Cloudflare challenge (HTTP 403) and live imdb.com returns an empty bot-wall response (HTTP 202) to automated fetches, so the archived snapshot is the Internet Archive capture of the same MobyGames page dated 2 December 2025 (web.archive.org/web/20251202062013), retrieved with curl during a window when web.archive.org was intermittently returning HTTP 503. SHA-256 is computed over that raw source snapshot. Pre-1988 Game Arts credits (Thexder 1985 design; Silpheed 1986 programming/art) are drawn from the game Wikipedia infoboxes, which MobyGames’ credit list does not currently cover. IMDb entry nm1900901 is cited but could not be captured. Project B59’s own @59Buckazoids “Spotlight: Satoshi Uesaka” thread (x.com/59Buckazoids/status/1926136509607956713, May 2025) is cited in provenance only; X blocks automated capture, so it was not archived as a separate record. The reader page separates original Project B59 commentary from a verbatim, cited transcription of the credit data (title / year / platform / role) — factual credits, no game content reproduced.","hash":"a1dcb5b8bc4298406fe74873b8e67ca2022ac2d01b70110d0026c2138d0428f2","tags":["game-history","japanese-pc-gaming","game-arts","thexder","silpheed","satoshi-uesaka","pc-8801","profile","bdc-class-9"],"excerpt":"Graphic and mechanical design on Thexder and Silpheed; a director and the writer on the Thexder sequel.","files":[{"path":"satoshi-uesaka-game-arts-and-the-thexder-line.html","size":10685,"label":"Read Document","entry":true},{"path":"mobygames-satoshi-uesaka-source.html","size":33286,"label":"Original Archive (Primary Source)"}],"content_path":"content/B59-904.008","local":["content/B59-904.008/satoshi-uesaka-game-arts-and-the-thexder-line.html"]}];